{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NvyMqgZ9sj6AqdUDhWYf9t","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Handing off my-3dge\n2\t\n3\tFor the owner, and for an agent picking the engine up somewhere new. Where things stand is docs/PROGRESS.md; the\n4\tdecision behind this page is ADR-0024.\n5\t\n6\t## Handing the engine to game agents\n7\t\n8\t**The prompt, lean (the default).** Give every agent, from any lab, the same lines, with the game changed:\n9\t\n10\t```text\n11\tMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine. First read this whole file (plain Markdown; download it if you can):\n12\thttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/main/lean/README.md\n13\t```\n14\t\n15\tThat is all, for a chat in a browser as much as for a coding agent. lean/README.md is one file, under 20,000 tokens by\n16\tits budget (`LEAN_README_BUDGET_BYTES`, tools/lib/lean.ts; ADR-0024 amendments 1 and 2): the model, the rules worth\n17\tkeeping, game.html (a small game that plays), every look and filter, every built-in id, and every public name by\n18\tarea. The agent writes\n19\tthe game as one HTML page that loads the engine (lean/my3dge.js) from jsDelivr, as a page loads three.js; the page runs\n20\twhen it is opened in a browser with WebGPU. Nothing is installed, built or tested: the engine's repository tests the\n21\tpackage before every merge, so a game does not have to. An agent that cannot open links gets the same file attached\n22\t(lean/README.md).\n23\t\n24\tThe link is the raw file, not its GitHub page (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/blob/main/lean/README.md,\n25\tfor a person): the raw file is the Markdown alone, while the page wraps it in about ten times as much site, and an\n26\tagent's web tool may hand it a summary of a long page instead of every line. The game's head, with its import map,\n27\thas to be copied exactly, so \"download it if you can\" lets an agent with a shell take the file with `curl`.\n28\t\n29\t**The prompt, full workspace.** For a coding agent in a sandbox that should check its work as the engine does\n30\t(headless tests, replays, renders read as numbers):\n31\t\n32\t```text\n33\tMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine, in its full workspace: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\n34\tStart with the \"Make a game with my-3dge\" section of its README.\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\tThe agent runs one command in its workspace (an empty repository you made for the game, or a folder): a shallow\n38\tclone of the engine into a temporary folder, then tools/game/new-game.mjs, which writes the game:\n39\t\n40\t- the engine, copied byte for byte and pinned by `engine-lock.json` (the commit and every file's SHA-256);\n41\t- the starter game, labs/game/: a small game that plays, is won by its bot on 10 seeds and passes its tests;\n42\t- the game's own AGENTS.md (CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md import it): about 50 lines on making this game, which every\n43\t  coding agent loads by itself; docs/GAME-GUIDE.md, the engine for game makers, read once (under 15,000 tokens by\n44\t  its budget, `GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES`);\n45\t- ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, where the agent writes what it needed from the engine and did not get.\n46\t\n47\tThen the agent sets up (`bash scripts/setup.sh`, or `npm ci`), runs `npm test`, and grows the game from the starter,\n48\tchecking its work with `npm run check`, `npm test`, `node x sim` and `node x shot`. The engine is read-only in a game:\n49\t`npm run check` fails when an engine file changes without a declared patch. An agent that cannot reach GitHub but can\n50\ttake a file: the ZIP of main (about 13 MB), https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/archive/refs/heads/main.zip, and\n51\t\"run `node <unzipped folder>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir>`\"; installing still needs npm's registry.\n52\t\n53\t**Many games at once.**\n54\t\n55\t- Lean games need nothing but a browser that reaches jsDelivr: a chat can write one, and you open it. They take the\n56\t  engine at `@main`; a game pins today's engine by putting a commit in place of `main` in its two engine URLs.\n57\t- One repository per game in the full workspace. Agents in cloud sandboxes need somewhere to push, and this repository\n58\t  takes no games: its AGENTS.md sends a game agent to the README.\n59\t- Games share nothing while they run (a workspace carries its own pinned engine, about 20 MB), so ten or a hundred\n60\t  agents work in parallel without touching each other.\n61\t- A workspace agent's sandbox must reach github.com once (the clone) and npm's registry (the install); most coding-agent\n62\t  sandboxes allow both. scripts/setup.sh also downloads, only when missing, Node 24 from nodejs.org and Chromium (Chrome\n63\t  for Testing) from storage.googleapis.com; without them a game runs on Node 22.12 or newer (Vite's floor), all but the\n64\t  browser commands. Where a platform cuts the network while the agent works, put the command in its setup step.\n65\t- Every game made the same day is on the same engine (main, as it was). A game moves to a newer engine only when you\n66\t  ask its agent: `node x engine --update`, then `npm ci`, `npm run check`, `npm test`, and its replays' goldens\n67\t  recorded again where play changed.\n68\t- To improve the engine from many games, give an engine agent in this repository the list of game repositories and\n69\t  ask it to read their ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md files and turn what recurs into work packages.\n70\t\n71\t## How it works, and why this way\n72\t\n73\tWhat a coding agent needs, whoever made it: one obvious first step; instructions that load by themselves and are\n74\tabout its job (AGENTS.md is read by most agents, CLAUDE.md by Claude Code, GEMINI.md by Gemini CLI); a working example\n75\tto change rather than a blank page; checks it runs itself, without a screen; docs to search rather than read whole;\n76\tpinned versions; and nothing that loads by itself and misleads.\n77\t\n78\tAn agent pays for what it reads, not for what is on disk: a repository's size costs nothing, while every line that\n79\tloads each session, and every file opened while finding its way, costs tokens and attention. So a game loads about\n80\t50 lines of rules and reads one guide once; docs/API.md (about 1.2 MB) and the engine's file comments are there to\n81\tgrep.\n82\t\n83\tWhy not the engine's own repository, with the game inside it: a game agent there would load the engine's rules (work\n84\tpackages, the ledger, escalation records), a setup hook that clones the projects the engine was ported from, an\n85\thour-long CI and over 5,000 engine tests, and ten agents pushing games into one repository would collide.\n86\t\n87\tWhy lean is the default (the owner's call of 2026-10-16, after a dozen trials): a library ships as three.js, Rapier\n88\tor Babylon.js do, as code and docs, without its tests and checks; and a model building in a chat answers in minutes,\n89\toften with no sandbox at all, so it cannot run an hour of tests or a toolchain. The engine's repository keeps the\n90\tchecks for itself and runs them on the package before every merge.\n91\t\n92\tWhy not hand-made packages: each would need rebuilding and retesting at every engine change, and would go stale. Here\n93\teach comes from the same checkout by rule, and is tested: the lean package is written by `node x docs --write`, held\n94\tcurrent by `x docs --check` and played in Chromium by tests/e2e/lean.spec.ts; the full workspace is made by one\n95\tcommand and proved after every merge (`node x engine --test`, in the full run); the repository, or its ZIP, is the\n96\tcomplete one.\n97\t\n98\tWhy the engine is copied into each game, not linked: it works offline, with no submodule for a cloud agent to trip\n99\ton, and it stays searchable (agents' search tools skip node_modules). The lock does what a pin would.\n100\t\n101\t## Handing over the engine itself\n102\t\n103\tFor an agent that is to keep building the engine, not a game, give the repository:\n104\t\n105\t```text\n106\tContinue developing the my-3dge game engine: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\n107\tClone it, run `bash scripts/setup.sh`, then follow its AGENTS.md; docs/PROGRESS.md says where things stand.\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\tAGENTS.md holds the engine's rules and loads by itself in most coding agents; CLAUDE.md (Claude Code) and GEMINI.md\n111\t(Gemini CLI) import it, and Claude Code's SessionStart hook runs the setup. Every step below runs in a fresh clone:\n112\tGitHub's full run does exactly that after each merge.\n113\t\n114\t## Starting in a new environment\n115\t\n116\t```bash\n117\tgit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge && cd my-3dge && bash scripts/setup.sh\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tsetup.sh installs Node 24.22.2, clones the read-only sources at their pinned commits and runs `npm ci`; CLAUDE.md\n121\tsays where Chromium comes from (the cloud container's, or setup.sh's download, ADR-0014 amendment 8). `main` holds\n122\tevery stage, one squashed commit per stage PR (the `integration` branch that kept every lane's commit was deleted on\n123\tGitHub on 2026-10-14; the PRs' pages keep each stage's commits; the owner deleted the `game/*` branches, on purpose,\n124\tADR-0023). The owner's testing budget is ADR-0022, with what the stage runs learned in its amendment 1; ADR-0023 keeps\n125\tlong tests from holding up work: merges wait on the quick gate, and the full run follows on GitHub.\n126\t\n127\t## The box against my-3d2dge's stress-world\n128\t\n129\tWhat a person opens is the playground, `playground.html` (labs/playground/README.md); this compares the Stress Box, an\n130\tengine tool, with stress-world. https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-world (about 2,600 lines, a temporary lab built in\n131\thours) shows most of what the box shows: the hall, the hero's moves, the walker, slime and wisp crowd with flow fields\n132\tand attack tokens, the cel, pixel, bloom and FXAA filters, 5,000 bodies. It shows more on the surface: a full panel\n133\twith presets, live fps and a bottleneck readout, a benchmark button, monster skins, waves, the card characters and a\n134\tWebGL fallback. What my-3dge adds is underneath: audio, the 1,218-clip animation library with reactions, replays with\n135\tthe same golden hashes in Node and Chromium, over 5,000 tests, and a documented public API for game code. That only\n136\tpays off once agents build games on it. The box's menu (labs/box/menu.ts) is the first step toward the panel.\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"527c7593-f1b9-480b-8c53-ca01133c2b2a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:41.950Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/HANDOFF.md","content":"# Handing off my-3dge\n\nFor the owner, and for an agent picking the engine up somewhere new. Where things stand is docs/PROGRESS.md; the\ndecision behind this page is ADR-0024.\n\n## Handing the engine to game agents\n\n**The prompt, lean (the default).** Give every agent, from any lab, the same lines, with the game changed:\n\n```text\nMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine. First read this whole file (plain Markdown; download it if you can):\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/main/lean/README.md\n```\n\nThat is all, for a chat in a browser as much as for a coding agent. lean/README.md is one file, under 20,000 tokens by\nits budget (`LEAN_README_BUDGET_BYTES`, tools/lib/lean.ts; ADR-0024 amendments 1 and 2): the model, the rules worth\nkeeping, game.html (a small game that plays), every look and filter, every built-in id, and every public name by\narea. The agent writes\nthe game as one HTML page that loads the engine (lean/my3dge.js) from jsDelivr, as a page loads three.js; the page runs\nwhen it is opened in a browser with WebGPU. Nothing is installed, built or tested: the engine's repository tests the\npackage before every merge, so a game does not have to. An agent that cannot open links gets the same file attached\n(lean/README.md).\n\nThe link is the raw file, not its GitHub page (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/blob/main/lean/README.md,\nfor a person): the raw file is the Markdown alone, while the page wraps it in about ten times as much site, and an\nagent's web tool may hand it a summary of a long page instead of every line. The game's head, with its import map,\nhas to be copied exactly, so \"download it if you can\" lets an agent with a shell take the file with `curl`.\n\n**The prompt, full workspace.** For a coding agent in a sandbox that should check its work as the engine does\n(headless tests, replays, renders read as numbers):\n\n```text\nMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine, in its full workspace: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\nStart with the \"Make a game with my-3dge\" section of its README.\n```\n\nThe agent runs one command in its workspace (an empty repository you made for the game, or a folder): a shallow\nclone of the engine into a temporary folder, then tools/game/new-game.mjs, which writes the game:\n\n- the engine, copied byte for byte and pinned by `engine-lock.json` (the commit and every file's SHA-256);\n- the starter game, labs/game/: a small game that plays, is won by its bot on 10 seeds and passes its tests;\n- the game's own AGENTS.md (CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md import it): about 50 lines on making this game, which every\n  coding agent loads by itself; docs/GAME-GUIDE.md, the engine for game makers, read once (under 15,000 tokens by\n  its budget, `GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES`);\n- ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, where the agent writes what it needed from the engine and did not get.\n\nThen the agent sets up (`bash scripts/setup.sh`, or `npm ci`), runs `npm test`, and grows the game from the starter,\nchecking its work with `npm run check`, `npm test`, `node x sim` and `node x shot`. The engine is read-only in a game:\n`npm run check` fails when an engine file changes without a declared patch. An agent that cannot reach GitHub but can\ntake a file: the ZIP of main (about 13 MB), https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/archive/refs/heads/main.zip, and\n\"run `node <unzipped folder>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir>`\"; installing still needs npm's registry.\n\n**Many games at once.**\n\n- Lean games need nothing but a browser that reaches jsDelivr: a chat can write one, and you open it. They take the\n  engine at `@main`; a game pins today's engine by putting a commit in place of `main` in its two engine URLs.\n- One repository per game in the full workspace. Agents in cloud sandboxes need somewhere to push, and this repository\n  takes no games: its AGENTS.md sends a game agent to the README.\n- Games share nothing while they run (a workspace carries its own pinned engine, about 20 MB), so ten or a hundred\n  agents work in parallel without touching each other.\n- A workspace agent's sandbox must reach github.com once (the clone) and npm's registry (the install); most coding-agent\n  sandboxes allow both. scripts/setup.sh also downloads, only when missing, Node 24 from nodejs.org and Chromium (Chrome\n  for Testing) from storage.googleapis.com; without them a game runs on Node 22.12 or newer (Vite's floor), all but the\n  browser commands. Where a platform cuts the network while the agent works, put the command in its setup step.\n- Every game made the same day is on the same engine (main, as it was). A game moves to a newer engine only when you\n  ask its agent: `node x engine --update`, then `npm ci`, `npm run check`, `npm test`, and its replays' goldens\n  recorded again where play changed.\n- To improve the engine from many games, give an engine agent in this repository the list of game repositories and\n  ask it to read their ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md files and turn what recurs into work packages.\n\n## How it works, and why this way\n\nWhat a coding agent needs, whoever made it: one obvious first step; instructions that load by themselves and are\nabout its job (AGENTS.md is read by most agents, CLAUDE.md by Claude Code, GEMINI.md by Gemini CLI); a working example\nto change rather than a blank page; checks it runs itself, without a screen; docs to search rather than read whole;\npinned versions; and nothing that loads by itself and misleads.\n\nAn agent pays for what it reads, not for what is on disk: a repository's size costs nothing, while every line that\nloads each session, and every file opened while finding its way, costs tokens and attention. So a game loads about\n50 lines of rules and reads one guide once; docs/API.md (about 1.2 MB) and the engine's file comments are there to\ngrep.\n\nWhy not the engine's own repository, with the game inside it: a game agent there would load the engine's rules (work\npackages, the ledger, escalation records), a setup hook that clones the projects the engine was ported from, an\nhour-long CI and over 5,000 engine tests, and ten agents pushing games into one repository would collide.\n\nWhy lean is the default (the owner's call of 2026-10-16, after a dozen trials): a library ships as three.js, Rapier\nor Babylon.js do, as code and docs, without its tests and checks; and a model building in a chat answers in minutes,\noften with no sandbox at all, so it cannot run an hour of tests or a toolchain. The engine's repository keeps the\nchecks for itself and runs them on the package before every merge.\n\nWhy not hand-made packages: each would need rebuilding and retesting at every engine change, and would go stale. Here\neach comes from the same checkout by rule, and is tested: the lean package is written by `node x docs --write`, held\ncurrent by `x docs --check` and played in Chromium by tests/e2e/lean.spec.ts; the full workspace is made by one\ncommand and proved after every merge (`node x engine --test`, in the full run); the repository, or its ZIP, is the\ncomplete one.\n\nWhy the engine is copied into each game, not linked: it works offline, with no submodule for a cloud agent to trip\non, and it stays searchable (agents' search tools skip node_modules). The lock does what a pin would.\n\n## Handing over the engine itself\n\nFor an agent that is to keep building the engine, not a game, give the repository:\n\n```text\nContinue developing the my-3dge game engine: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\nClone it, run `bash scripts/setup.sh`, then follow its AGENTS.md; docs/PROGRESS.md says where things stand.\n```\n\nAGENTS.md holds the engine's rules and loads by itself in most coding agents; CLAUDE.md (Claude Code) and GEMINI.md\n(Gemini CLI) import it, and Claude Code's SessionStart hook runs the setup. Every step below runs in a fresh clone:\nGitHub's full run does exactly that after each merge.\n\n## Starting in a new environment\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge && cd my-3dge && bash scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nsetup.sh installs Node 24.22.2, clones the read-only sources at their pinned commits and runs `npm ci`; CLAUDE.md\nsays where Chromium comes from (the cloud container's, or setup.sh's download, ADR-0014 amendment 8). `main` holds\nevery stage, one squashed commit per stage PR (the `integration` branch that kept every lane's commit was deleted on\nGitHub on 2026-10-14; the PRs' pages keep each stage's commits; the owner deleted the `game/*` branches, on purpose,\nADR-0023). The owner's testing budget is ADR-0022, with what the stage runs learned in its amendment 1; ADR-0023 keeps\nlong tests from holding up work: merges wait on the quick gate, and the full run follows on GitHub.\n\n## The box against my-3d2dge's stress-world\n\nWhat a person opens is the playground, `playground.html` (labs/playground/README.md); this compares the Stress Box, an\nengine tool, with stress-world. https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-world (about 2,600 lines, a temporary lab built in\nhours) shows most of what the box shows: the hall, the hero's moves, the walker, slime and wisp crowd with flow fields\nand attack tokens, the cel, pixel, bloom and FXAA filters, 5,000 bodies. It shows more on the surface: a full panel\nwith presets, live fps and a bottleneck readout, a benchmark button, monster skins, waves, the card characters and a\nWebGL fallback. What my-3dge adds is underneath: audio, the 1,218-clip animation library with reactions, replays with\nthe same golden hashes in Node and Chromium, over 5,000 tests, and a documented public API for game code. That only\npays off once agents build games on it. The box's menu (labs/box/menu.ts) is the first step toward the panel.\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NvyMqgZ9sj6AqdUDhWYf9t","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9JHFDmHGYqgWdERLcA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014U8uE16jzvrJnhz34YZRck","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/PROGRESS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3812,"cache_read_input_tokens":51954,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3812},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"17ae6dcc-d611-4250-8f8b-a5c23727a34d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:42.728Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9JGzLUhhpvHh1bPre2","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014U8uE16jzvrJnhz34YZRck":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/PROGRESS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014U8uE16jzvrJnhz34YZRck","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Progress\n2\t\n3\tRead this first: where things stand, what is next and the open issues, on one screen (`node x docs --check` fails it\n4\tpast 40 lines). The integrator rewrites it at each gate; every WP's status and notes are in the ledger, PLAN.md §14.\n5\t\n6\t## Next: nothing scheduled; the owner's playtest of the tech demo\n7\t\n8\t- Gate G13 (PR #34): Phase 13 is done, with every WP it fired: the front end (WP 13.25), the governor (7.5),\n9\t  GPU-driven crowds' stage 1 (11.4), templates and skills (12.1), the usability evals and mutation testing (12.2),\n10\t  `x mcp` (7.6), the tour with its attract mode and a film per look (13.26). What follows waits: upgrades U-2 to U-7\n11\t  from their dates (`node x deps --qualify`), on-demand WPs for their triggers (§9.1), Phase H for a game to ship.\n12\t- The owner's, all in the playground's Owner checks (start menu; WP 13.28), each in plain words with Go there: the\n13\t  playtest (docs/PLAYGROUND.md §9.3), the looks to judge (the clean default, clean and cel at full resolution, the\n14\t  accent weak from isometric, the cel look's dark night there, its near-black brick), the sound and a pad, and ESC-0002\n15\t  to ESC-0006's calls (the Quaternius GLBs, model access for the live evals, the crowd's CPU render, the mutants outside\n16\t  the sample), answered from Copy my answers. Their go-ahead (2026-10-22): a bot may play with infinite health and ammo.\n17\t\n18\t## Where things stand (2026-11-05)\n19\t\n20\t- Phases 0–9 and 13 are done (G10–G13: PR #22 to #34). The playground (`npm run dev`) is one map, the block, behind a\n21\t  splash and a start menu (WP 13.33), shut doors walls to its cameras (13.34), no z-fighting (13.35–13.37), the audit's\n22\t  gaps closed (13.38); the player moves as Apex in five cameras (doors, pickups, melee, guns, ragdolls, neighbours, a\n23\t  dog, sound, day and night, GPU rain; clean, cel and pixel looks); Watch the tour plays it all. Agents drive it through\n24\t  `node x`, `__engine` or `x mcp`. Games: lean/. Shipped as playground.html. Faster starts (ADR-0008 amendments 11, 12;\n25\t  Owner: a move still re-keys lit pipelines); Style Lab's filters in Tune the look.\n26\t\n27\t## What waits, and the open issues\n28\t\n29\t- Amendment 22 proposes triangle-mesh terrain. QA baselines: the timelines' knockdown slide, WP 8.8's. Owner:\n30\t  top-down's pixel look panned across a whole pixel changes 7–10 pixels of a soft shadow band (amendment 35). Clip\n31\t  contacts: hands read as holds in walks, idles and more (WP 8.8's row); no foot lock reads a clip's foot contacts yet.\n32\t  Not verifiable here: speakers, touch (ADR-0021), pads, real GPUs (`stats().gpuMs`: the rain's 1 ms; load times), the\n33\t  pixel look's glide, how the filters read, a desktop MCP client. A key pressed in a warm-up is lost (amendment 48).\n34\t- Mutation (WP 12.2, ESC-0006): the run's tenth catches 94.40%, the rest 74.3%; rotate its seed and redraw each gate.\n35\t- Near the size cap (390–397 lines), split on the next change: sim/world.ts, anim/{quadruped,clip/validate}.ts,\n36\t  tools/cmd/replay.ts, app/engine.ts; four tests at 399–400.\n37\t- The quick gate is over its 5 min (CI_BUDGET), its long pole the playground's soak and site tests; under load\n38\t  `npm test` can fail on RPC timeouts; audio.spec's spatial test hung once in an offline render on GitHub (e6f38ba).\n39\t- The tour's page goldens come from its spec (amendment 41). Random play lost the shooter's chest in 4–5% of frames\n40\t  (before WP 13.27). A shot's ID pass builds a pipeline again after each lambert–toon style switch (frames never do).\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"5440e2ba-fd7b-4914-875e-f908c30f351f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:42.747Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/PROGRESS.md","content":"# Progress\n\nRead this first: where things stand, what is next and the open issues, on one screen (`node x docs --check` fails it\npast 40 lines). The integrator rewrites it at each gate; every WP's status and notes are in the ledger, PLAN.md §14.\n\n## Next: nothing scheduled; the owner's playtest of the tech demo\n\n- Gate G13 (PR #34): Phase 13 is done, with every WP it fired: the front end (WP 13.25), the governor (7.5),\n  GPU-driven crowds' stage 1 (11.4), templates and skills (12.1), the usability evals and mutation testing (12.2),\n  `x mcp` (7.6), the tour with its attract mode and a film per look (13.26). What follows waits: upgrades U-2 to U-7\n  from their dates (`node x deps --qualify`), on-demand WPs for their triggers (§9.1), Phase H for a game to ship.\n- The owner's, all in the playground's Owner checks (start menu; WP 13.28), each in plain words with Go there: the\n  playtest (docs/PLAYGROUND.md §9.3), the looks to judge (the clean default, clean and cel at full resolution, the\n  accent weak from isometric, the cel look's dark night there, its near-black brick), the sound and a pad, and ESC-0002\n  to ESC-0006's calls (the Quaternius GLBs, model access for the live evals, the crowd's CPU render, the mutants outside\n  the sample), answered from Copy my answers. Their go-ahead (2026-10-22): a bot may play with infinite health and ammo.\n\n## Where things stand (2026-11-05)\n\n- Phases 0–9 and 13 are done (G10–G13: PR #22 to #34). The playground (`npm run dev`) is one map, the block, behind a\n  splash and a start menu (WP 13.33), shut doors walls to its cameras (13.34), no z-fighting (13.35–13.37), the audit's\n  gaps closed (13.38); the player moves as Apex in five cameras (doors, pickups, melee, guns, ragdolls, neighbours, a\n  dog, sound, day and night, GPU rain; clean, cel and pixel looks); Watch the tour plays it all. Agents drive it through\n  `node x`, `__engine` or `x mcp`. Games: lean/. Shipped as playground.html. Faster starts (ADR-0008 amendments 11, 12;\n  Owner: a move still re-keys lit pipelines); Style Lab's filters in Tune the look.\n\n## What waits, and the open issues\n\n- Amendment 22 proposes triangle-mesh terrain. QA baselines: the timelines' knockdown slide, WP 8.8's. Owner:\n  top-down's pixel look panned across a whole pixel changes 7–10 pixels of a soft shadow band (amendment 35). Clip\n  contacts: hands read as holds in walks, idles and more (WP 8.8's row); no foot lock reads a clip's foot contacts yet.\n  Not verifiable here: speakers, touch (ADR-0021), pads, real GPUs (`stats().gpuMs`: the rain's 1 ms; load times), the\n  pixel look's glide, how the filters read, a desktop MCP client. A key pressed in a warm-up is lost (amendment 48).\n- Mutation (WP 12.2, ESC-0006): the run's tenth catches 94.40%, the rest 74.3%; rotate its seed and redraw each gate.\n- Near the size cap (390–397 lines), split on the next change: sim/world.ts, anim/{quadruped,clip/validate}.ts,\n  tools/cmd/replay.ts, app/engine.ts; four tests at 399–400.\n- The quick gate is over its 5 min (CI_BUDGET), its long pole the playground's soak and site tests; under load\n  `npm test` can fail on RPC timeouts; audio.spec's spatial test hung once in an offline render on GitHub (e6f38ba).\n- The tour's page goldens come from its spec (amendment 41). Random play lost the shooter's chest in 4–5% of frames\n  (before WP 13.27). A shot's ID pass builds a pipeline again after each lambert–toon style switch (frames never do).\n","numLines":41,"startLine":1,"totalLines":41}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014U8uE16jzvrJnhz34YZRck","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9JHFDmHGYqgWdERLcA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DMDf566JcmFfwBAD3DY5HJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/lean/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3812,"cache_read_input_tokens":51954,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3812},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"e752d4a6-d706-4dfa-a6ec-8b4b4aae0986","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:43.414Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9JGzLUhhpvHh1bPre2","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DMDf566JcmFfwBAD3DY5HJ":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/lean/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMDf566JcmFfwBAD3DY5HJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3dge, lean\n2\t\n3\tThe my-3dge game engine as a library, the way three.js or Rapier ship: one ES module, nothing to install, build or\n4\ttest. It makes 3D games for the browser: three.js r182 draws on WebGPU, Rapier simulates the physics, a fixed 60 Hz\n5\tsim runs the rules, and HTML/CSS draws the UI. This file is all you need, in a chat with no sandbox too: a game is\n6\tone HTML file.\n7\t\n8\t## Start\n9\t\n10\t1. Take `game.html` (in full below): a small game that already plays. You run round an arena and collect the gems\n11\t   before the clock runs out.\n12\t2. Change it into the game you are asked for. Keep its head: it loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr.\n13\t3. Open it in a browser with WebGPU (current Chrome, Edge or Safari; Firefox where it has WebGPU): double-click it, or\n14\t   serve its folder (`npx serve`, `python3 -m http.server`). There is no WebGL fallback.\n15\t\n16\tA bigger game can split its code into module files that each import `my3dge`; serve the folder then, since browsers\n17\tload module files over http, not from a double-clicked page.\n18\t\n19\t## How a game is made\n20\t\n21\t**Two halves.** The _sim_ is the rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them every\n22\tstep, and events. The _page_ calls `createEngine`, which starts the sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into\n23\tintents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame` for the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The\n24\tpage reads the sim and never writes it.\n25\t\n26\t**A scene** is a game mode: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings, setup(w), step(w, intents) })`.\n27\t`setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. A page offers its scenes with\n28\t`createEngine({ scenes })`, and `?scene=<id>` picks one.\n29\t\n30\t**The world, `w`,** is what systems get:\n31\t\n32\t- `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields (`number`, `integer`,\n33\t  `string`, `boolean`) with defaults. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an id; `w.query('gem')` returns the\n34\t  entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)` the entity (`.gem`), `w.add(id, 'gem', values)`, `w.remove(id,\n35\t  'gem')` and `w.despawn(id)` do the rest. A component with `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box, which\n36\t  also reads `y` (its bottom), `yaw`, `size` (its side, 0.5 m) and `material` (an id): name a place you only keep\n37\t  otherwise.\n38\t- Systems run in phases, in order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`:\n39\t  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` runs after the physics moved the bodies.\n40\t- `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)`; a page reads\n41\t  `frame.world.events.trace()`.\n42\t- Time is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800).\n43\t- Randomness comes from named streams: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n44\t- Settings are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum, when:\n45\t  'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`, set from the URL (`?game.gems=20`).\n46\t\n47\t**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once a step: `move` (a direction `[x, z]`), buttons held and\n48\tpressed, and the game's own keys. A player actor (`spawnActor(w, { player: true, … })`) reads them itself; systems\n49\tget them as their second argument (`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). WASD or the arrows run,\n50\tSpace jumps, Shift dashes.\n51\t\n52\t**The HUD** is the page's: `engine.ui.hud(id, text, { at })` sets a line at one of six places (`top-left`, `top`,\n53\t`top-right`, `bottom-left`, `bottom`, `bottom-right`), and `null` as its text removes it.\n54\t\n55\t**Particles, sound and lights.** The sim says what happened with events; the page turns them into particles and\n56\tsound, so play stays reproducible:\n57\t\n58\t- A sim event bursts every emitter whose `events` names its type, where its payload says (`position`, or `x`, `y`,\n59\t  `z`): `w.emit('hit', { x, y: 1, z })` throws sparks. To burst others on your own event, lay them into an emitter:\n60\t  `defineEmitter('emitter:lit', { events: ['lit'], layers: [{ emitter: 'emitter:fireflies', count: 24 }] })`, then\n61\t  `w.emit('lit', { x, y: 1.2, z })`.\n62\t- Sound plays in the page: `const audio = createAudio();` once (it starts at the player's first key or click), then\n63\t  `audio.play('sfx:coin')`, or in `onFrame` each new event whose payload names one, `w.emit('lit', { sfx:\n64\t  'sfx:powerup', x, z })` in the sim: `for (const e of frame.world.events.trace()) if (e.seq > heard) { heard =\n65\t  e.seq; audio.event(e.type, e.payload); }`.\n66\t- A prop with a light (`prop:lantern`, `prop:lamp`, …) is switchable when spawned with `{ on }` or `{ group }`:\n67\t  `const id = spawnProp(w, { prop: 'prop:lantern', at: [x, 0, z], yaw: 0 }, { on: false })`, then\n68\t  `w.get(id).propLight.on = true` lights it, and `switchGroup(w, group, true)` a group.\n69\t\n70\t**Characters, physics and the rest** are in the API below, area by area, and every built-in id under [Built-in ids\n71\tand the level legend](#built-in-ids-and-the-level-legend): levels as text (`compileLevel`: a tile at column c and\n72\trow r is at x = (c + 0.5) × tile, z = (r + 0.5) × tile; `level.spawns` are `{ name, position }`, `'hero'` and `'1'`\n73\tto `'9'`) and sites of buildings on a terrain (`defineSite`),\n74\tcharacters (`createCharacter`, Apex Legends' movement with `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`: sprint, slide, mantle,\n75\tclimb; `spawnActor`), physics (`physicsOf(w)` and its `.raycast`, `.overlap`), props, ladders, ziplines, doors,\n76\twindows and switches, crowds and steering, combat, melee and firearms (`addMelee`, `defineWeapon`), materials and\n77\ttextures as data, looks and post-processing, particle emitters, sound as data, cameras (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=chase`,\n78\tthe shooter's `?cam3=shoulder`), the HUD (`engine.ui.hud`), menus and a store for saves and settings (`createMenu`,\n79\t`createStore`, `createEngine({ store, modal })`) and touch controls.\n80\t\n81\t## Rules that keep play reproducible\n82\t\n83\tThe engine runs the same seed and the same intents to the same state, bit for bit, on every machine. Keep these in\n84\tthe sim (a scene's `setup`, `step` and systems) and replays, `__engine.hash()` and capture and restore keep working:\n85\t\n86\t- No `Date`, timers, `Math.random`, DOM, network or storage in the sim. Randomness from `w.rng`, time in steps.\n87\t- Write the standard `Math` (the sim swaps in portable versions while it steps), but never keep a `Math` function in a\n88\t  variable, and write `Math.pow`, not `**`.\n89\t- Keep the sim's state in components, not in variables beside it; loop over entities in `w.query`'s order.\n90\t- Presentation never writes the sim: the camera, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change nothing a hash sees.\n91\t- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n92\t\n93\t## Make it look different\n94\t\n95\tHow a game looks is data, apart from how it plays: a _look_ is a material style, a chain of filters and a sky. The\n96\tsame game can be a storybook, a film or pixel art with nothing else changed, and no look changes a replay or a hash.\n97\t\n98\t- **Pick a look:** `?post.look=look:storybook` in the URL. To make it the game's own, set it where game.html sets its\n99\t  camera: `if (!search.has('post.look')) search.set('post.look', 'look:storybook');`. Live, from the console:\n100\t  `__engine.set('post.look', 'look:cinematic')`.\n101\t- **Lay looks on and tune them:** `post.combine` lays looks over the one drawn, each `{ look, amount, only, filters }`\n102\t  (`amount` 0 to 1, `only` some of its filters, `filters` changes to them); `post.filters` changes filters field by\n103\t  field; `post.strength` (0 to 1) fades the whole result. In a URL their values are JSON:\n104\t  `?post.combine=[{\"look\":\"look:vignette/soft\",\"amount\":0.6}]&post.filters={\"grade\":{\"scope\":\"all\",\"saturation\":1.3}}`.\n105\t- **Where a filter applies:** its `scope`: `'all'`, `'characters'` (the actors' and crowds' bodies), `'objects'`\n106\t  (props), `'environment'` (the rest: the level, the terrain, stand-in boxes, the sky), two joined by `+`\n107\t  (`'characters+objects'`), or `'off'`. Every filter has an `amount`, 0 (as if it were off) to 1.\n108\t- **Make your own:** `defineLook` before `createEngine`, then pick it like any look:\n109\t\n110\t  ```js\n111\t  defineLook('look:warm-tale', {\n112\t    description: 'A storybook with soft corners and inked heroes.',\n113\t    combine: [{ look: 'look:storybook' }, { look: 'look:vignette/soft', amount: 0.6 }],\n114\t    filters: { outline: { scope: 'characters', width: 2 }, grade: { scope: 'all', temperature: 0.2 } },\n115\t  });\n116\t  ```\n117\t\n118\t- **Read it back:** `__engine.look()` gives the look drawn now: its id, the looks laid on, the strength, and every\n119\t  filter that is on with its scope, amount and numbers; its `filters` pasted into a `defineLook` keeps the picture.\n120\t\n121\tHow they combine: a look's `combine` goes on first, layer by layer, then its own `filters`, each of which replaces a\n122\tlayer's filter of its name whole, its unwritten fields at their defaults (so `look:warm-tale` inks only the\n123\tcharacters, though `look:storybook` inks everything). A layer at an `amount` below 1 fades in a filter the chain lacks\n124\t(its `amount` scaled) and moves one the chain has that far: numbers move, colours mix, and choices (a scope, a kind)\n125\tswitch from 0.5. A filter stays off\n126\tuntil something gives it a scope: `grade: { temperature: 0.2 }` alone does nothing. A layer never turns a filter off,\n127\tnor does `scope: 'off'` in a look's own `filters`: leave a layer's filter out with `only`, or set its scope to `'off'`\n128\tin that layer's `filters`; `post.filters`, applied last, turns one off outright. Only filters are laid on: the style,\n129\tthe sky and the rest come from the look `post.look` names. Changing a filter's numbers, scope or amount, or the looks\n130\tlaid on, builds no shader. Every look, what it draws, and every filter are under [Looks and\n131\tfilters](#looks-and-filters), below game.html.\n132\t\n133\t## Look inside a running game\n134\t\n135\tThe browser console has `__engine`: `__engine.help()` lists it all; `step(n)`, `hash()`, `state('round')`,\n136\t`set('game.gems', 20)`, `seed(7)`, `camera.set('cam=iso')`, `look()`, and `describe('material')`, a kind's fields and\n137\tids. The URL takes the same: `?seed=7&game.gems=20&cam=iso`.\n138\tErrors name their fix, and docs/ERRORS.md in the engine's repository lists every code.\n139\t\n140\t## Versions\n141\t\n142\tThe head's URLs pin three.js and Rapier, and take the engine at `@main`, its latest: jsDelivr serves a new commit\n143\twithin 12 hours, and a browser may keep the copy it loaded for up to 7 days (jsDelivr's cache header). To freeze a\n144\tgame on one engine, replace `main` with a commit of https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge in its two engine URLs\n145\t(`my3dge.js`, `my3dge.css`).\n146\t\n147\tMore: the engine's repository has the full guide (docs/GAME-GUIDE.md), every export (docs/API.md) and the source. An\n148\tagent that can run commands and wants the engine's checks too can start from the repository's README instead.\n149\tLicense: MIT; `my3dge.js` carries @stdlib functions (Apache-2.0): see LICENSE beside this file.\n150\t\n151\t## game.html\n152\t\n153\t<!-- lean:game:start -->\n154\t```html\n155\t<!doctype html>\n156\t<html lang=\"en\">\n157\t  <head>\n158\t    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n159\t    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\" />\n160\t    <title>Gem run: a my-3dge game</title>\n161\t    <!--\n162\t      A whole game in one page: run round an arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out. Make your game by\n163\t      changing this file (lean/README.md says how). Open it in a browser with WebGPU: double-click it, or serve its\n164\t      folder. The block below loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr; keep it as it is.\n165\t    -->\n166\t    <!-- lean:head:start -->\n167\t    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.css\" />\n168\t    <script type=\"importmap\">\n169\t      {\n170\t        \"imports\": {\n171\t          \"my3dge\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.js\",\n172\t          \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n173\t          \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n174\t          \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.js\",\n175\t          \"three/addons/\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/examples/jsm/\",\n176\t          \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.21.0/dist/rapier.mjs\"\n177\t        }\n178\t      }\n179\t    </script>\n180\t    <!-- lean:head:end -->\n181\t    <style>\n182\t      html,\n183\t      body {\n184\t        margin: 0;\n185\t        height: 100%;\n186\t        overflow: hidden;\n187\t        background: #15171c;\n188\t      }\n189\t      canvas {\n190\t        display: block;\n191\t        width: 100vw;\n192\t        height: 100dvh;\n193\t        outline: none;\n194\t        touch-action: none;\n195\t      }\n196\t      #msg {\n197\t        position: fixed;\n198\t        inset: 0;\n199\t        display: flex;\n200\t        align-items: center;\n201\t        justify-content: center;\n202\t        padding: 24px;\n203\t        font:\n204\t          18px system-ui,\n205\t          sans-serif;\n206\t        color: #c9ccd4;\n207\t        text-align: center;\n208\t        white-space: pre-line;\n209\t        pointer-events: none;\n210\t      }\n211\t    </style>\n212\t    <script>\n213\t      // Until the engine starts, a script that fails shows here in one line, never as a blank page.\n214\t      window.__engine = { ready: false, errors: [] };\n215\t      function failed(message) {\n216\t        if (typeof window.__engine.help === 'function') return; // the engine shows its own errors\n217\t        window.__engine.errors.push({\n218\t          code: 'APP_START',\n219\t          message: message,\n220\t          fix: 'the browser console names the cause',\n221\t        });\n222\t        document.getElementById('msg').textContent = 'The game failed to start: ' + message;\n223\t      }\n224\t      addEventListener('error', (event) => failed(event.message || 'a script failed to load'));\n225\t      addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => failed(String(event.reason?.message ?? event.reason)));\n226\t    </script>\n227\t  </head>\n228\t  <body>\n229\t    <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas>\n230\t    <div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\">Loading…</div>\n231\t    <script type=\"module\">\n232\t      import {\n233\t        addLevelBodies,\n234\t        compileLevel,\n235\t        createCharacter,\n236\t        createEngine,\n237\t        defineComponent,\n238\t        defineScene,\n239\t        defineSettings,\n240\t        physicsOf,\n241\t        spawnActor,\n242\t      } from 'my3dge';\n243\t\n244\t      // ---- The sim: everything that decides play. It runs 60 steps a second, the same on every machine. ----\n245\t\n246\t      // The arena as text: # wall, . floor, P pillar, w low wall (jump it), @ the player's spawn. 1 m a character.\n247\t      const ARENA_TEXT = `---\n248\tdescription: The arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump, the player's spawn.\n249\t---\n250\t##################\n251\t#................#\n252\t#..@.............#\n253\t#.......PP.......#\n254\t#.......PP.......#\n255\t#................#\n256\t#....w......w....#\n257\t#....w......w....#\n258\t#................#\n259\t#.......PP.......#\n260\t#.......PP.......#\n261\t#................#\n262\t#................#\n263\t##################\n264\t`;\n265\t      const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'game.html', name: 'arena' });\n266\t\n267\t      // Settings: change them from the URL (?game.gems=20) or the console (__engine.set('game.gems', 20)).\n268\t      defineSettings({\n269\t        'game.gems': {\n270\t          type: 'integer', default: 10, minimum: 1, maximum: 40, when: 'scene', description: 'How many gems.',\n271\t        },\n272\t        'game.seconds': {\n273\t          type: 'number', default: 30, minimum: 5, maximum: 600, unit: 's', when: 'scene', description: 'The clock.',\n274\t        },\n275\t        'game.speed': {\n276\t          type: 'number', default: 6, minimum: 1, maximum: 20, unit: 'm/s', when: 'scene', description: 'Run speed.',\n277\t        },\n278\t      });\n279\t\n280\t      // Components: data on entities. A component with x and z in metres and a material is drawn by the page.\n281\t      defineComponent('gem', {\n282\t        description: 'A gem to collect.',\n283\t        fields: {\n284\t          x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n285\t          y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up.' },\n286\t          z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n287\t          yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n288\t          size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'Its size.' },\n289\t          material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'How it is drawn.' },\n290\t        },\n291\t      });\n292\t      defineComponent('round', {\n293\t        description: 'The round: gems collected, steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n294\t        fields: {\n295\t          gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems.' },\n296\t          collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many are collected.' },\n297\t          left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'Steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n298\t          state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n299\t        },\n300\t      });\n301\t\n302\t      const REACH = 0.8; // how close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m\n303\t      const START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero').position;\n304\t\n305\t      // Scatters the gems over floor tiles at least 3 m from the spawn, drawn from the named random stream 'gems'.\n306\t      function scatterGems(w, count) {\n307\t        const tiles = [];\n308\t        ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n309\t          const [x, z] = [((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile, (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile];\n310\t          if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= 3) tiles.push([x, z]);\n311\t        });\n312\t        const rng = w.rng('gems');\n313\t        for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n314\t          const [[x, z]] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n315\t          w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n316\t        }\n317\t      }\n318\t\n319\t      // Ends the round: the player stops, and the event says how it ended.\n320\t      function endRound(w, round, state) {\n321\t        round.state = state;\n322\t        for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n323\t          e.actor.player = false;\n324\t          e.actor.intents = {};\n325\t        }\n326\t        w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems });\n327\t      }\n328\t\n329\t      // A system: spins the gems and collects those an actor reaches (a despawn waits for the step's end).\n330\t      function collect(w) {\n331\t        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n332\t        for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n333\t        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n334\t        const taken = new Set();\n335\t        for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n336\t          for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n337\t            if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n338\t            taken.add(gem.id);\n339\t            w.despawn(gem.id);\n340\t            round.collected += 1;\n341\t            w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, collected: round.collected });\n342\t          }\n343\t        }\n344\t        if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n345\t      }\n346\t\n347\t      // A system: the clock counts whole steps, never summed seconds; at 0 the round is lost.\n348\t      function clock(w) {\n349\t        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n350\t        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n351\t        round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n352\t        if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n353\t      }\n354\t\n355\t      // The scene: the level, its physics, and what happens when it starts.\n356\t      const play = defineScene('game:play', {\n357\t        description: 'Run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n358\t        level: 'level:arena',\n359\t        physics: true,\n360\t        setup(w) {\n361\t          addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n362\t          const gems = w.settings.get('game.gems');\n363\t          w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n364\t          scatterGems(w, gems);\n365\t          const character = createCharacter({ speed: w.settings.get('game.speed'), jumpSpeed: 9 });\n366\t          const anim = { build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' }; // a humanoid that animates itself\n367\t          spawnActor(w, { at: [START[0], START[1], START[2]], character, player: true, anim });\n368\t          w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n369\t          w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n370\t        },\n371\t      });\n372\t\n373\t      // ---- The page: draws the run and shows the HUD. It only reads the sim, never changes it. ----\n374\t\n375\t      const search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n376\t      if (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase'); // the camera follows the player\n377\t      let engine = null;\n378\t      let ended = false;\n379\t\n380\t      // Each drawn frame: the gems, the clock, the keys, and how the round ended.\n381\t      function showRound({ world }) {\n382\t        const entity = world.query('round')[0];\n383\t        if (!engine || !entity) return;\n384\t        const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n385\t        engine.ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n386\t        const color = left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '';\n387\t        engine.ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color });\n388\t        engine.ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n389\t        ended = state !== 'playing';\n390\t        engine.ui.hud('end', ended ? `${state === 'won' ? 'Every gem!' : 'Time!'} Enter: play again` : null, {\n391\t          at: 'top',\n392\t        });\n393\t      }\n394\t\n395\t      engine = await createEngine({\n396\t        canvas: document.querySelector('#view'),\n397\t        scenes: [play.id],\n398\t        levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'game.html' } },\n399\t        search: `?${search}`,\n400\t        onFrame: showRound,\n401\t      });\n402\t      document.getElementById('msg').hidden = true;\n403\t\n404\t      // Enter after a round: the next round, on the next seed.\n405\t      addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n406\t        if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended) engine.inspector.seed(engine.inspector.seed() + 1);\n407\t      });\n408\t    </script>\n409\t  </body>\n410\t</html>\n411\t```\n412\t<!-- lean:game:end -->\n413\t\n414\t## Looks and filters\n415\t\n416\t<!-- lean:looks:start -->\n417\t**Whole looks**, each with what it draws (its style and sky where it names them, its filters and their scopes):\n418\t\n419\t- `look:16-bit`: The whole scene as 16-bit pixel art with a slightly vivid grade. _filters pixel, grade._\n420\t- `look:anime-action`: An action frame: comic cel bands, bold lines and speed lines. _filters cel, outline, speedlines._\n421\t- `look:arcade-cabinet`: An arcade cabinet: a curved tube's grille, glow and lines. _filters bloom, scanlines, crt, screen._\n422\t- `look:arcade-crt`: Low resolution on a curved tube with scanlines, glow and colour fringes. _filters bloom, scanlines, chroma, screen._\n423\t- `look:cartoon`: Toon shading everywhere, bold ink outlines and vivid colour. _style toon; filters outline, grade._\n424\t- `look:cinematic`: A film: teal and orange, anamorphic streaks, soft corners and widescreen bars. _filters streaks, grade, split, vignette, letterbox._\n425\t- `look:clean`: No filter: the picture as the scene draws it, in its own style. _nothing._\n426\t- `look:comic-book`: Printed dots on the world, inked heroes, punchy colour. _filters outline, stylize (environment), grade (characters+objects)._\n427\t- `look:comic-noir`: A black-and-white world; the characters and objects keep their colour, inked. _filters outline, grade (environment), screen._\n428\t- `look:flat-paper`: Flat colours, thin outlines and a faded grade, like a printed page. _style flat; filters outline, grade, grain._\n429\t- `look:game-boy`: Everything in four greens, pixelated and dithered. _filters pixel, palette._\n430\t- `look:game-boy-world`: The environment in four Game Boy greens; the characters and objects in full colour with ink outlines. _filters palette (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n431\t- `look:hacker-terminal`: The game as green characters on a curved, scanlined screen. _filters stylize, scanlines, screen._\n432\t- `look:hd-2d`: Pixel-art characters and objects in a soft, glowing 3D world with dark corners. _filters bloom, pixel (characters+objects), grade (environment), screen._\n433\t- `look:hologram-heroes`: Characters and objects flicker like holograms: cool, scanlined and fringed. The world stays clean. _filters bloom, grade (characters+objects), scanlines (characters+objects), grain (characters+objects), chroma (characters+objects)._\n434\t- `look:lamplit-fog`: A foggy night where every lamp glows in the air. _filters air, grade._\n435\t- `look:miniature`: A model railway: tilt-shift blur, crisp and bright colours. _filters tiltshift, sharpen, grade._\n436\t- `look:newspaper`: Black dots on cream paper, everything. _filters stylize._\n437\t- `look:oil-painting`: The whole scene in oil paint: flat strokes, brush streaks, canvas. _filters stylize, grade._\n438\t- `look:painted-world`: A painted backdrop; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n439\t- `look:pixel-heroes`: A clean, sharp world; the characters and objects are outlined pixel art. _filters pixel (characters+objects)._\n440\t- `look:pixel-world`: The environment is pixel art; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked. _filters pixel (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n441\t- `look:posterized-world`: The environment in a few flat shades; the characters and objects in full colour. _filters palette (environment)._\n442\t- `look:red-coat`: A grey film where only red keeps its colour, grainy and dark at the edges. _filters grade, selective, vignette, grain._\n443\t- `look:riso-print`: A risograph print: ember duotone, plates out of register, newsprint. _filters misregister, paper, duotone._\n444\t- `look:sketchbook`: Pencil hatching and contours on paper. _filters stylize._\n445\t- `look:sketched-world`: A coloured-pencil world; the characters and objects inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n446\t- `look:storybook`: A storybook: watercolour on cold-pressed paper with a thin ink line. _filters watercolor, outline, paper._\n447\t- `look:sunbeams`: Dusty air, sunlight streaming through the gaps, a soft glow. _filters air, bloom._\n448\t- `look:vhs-tape`: A home video: worn tape, soft scanlines, noise and dark corners. _filters scanlines, vhs, vignette, grain, chroma._\n449\t\n450\t**One-filter presets**, `look:<group>/<name>`: each turns its one filter on over everything, to draw alone or lay\n451\ton with `combine` (the `shading` group sets the material style instead, and `screen` the screen's own glass):\n452\t\n453\t- `aa`: smooth\n454\t- `air`: clear-air, dusty-sunbeams, lamplight, light-haze, morning-mist, pea-soup, smoky-hall\n455\t- `ao`: corners\n456\t- `bloom`: dreamy, neon, no-glow, soft\n457\t- `cel`: comic\n458\t- `crt`: arcade-tube\n459\t- `dof`: portrait\n460\t- `duotone`: dusk, ember, ocean\n461\t- `flare`: sun\n462\t- `gi`: bounce-light\n463\t- `grade`: bleach-bypass, cool, faded, lifted, neutral, night, noir, sepia, vivid, warm\n464\t- `grain`: clean, film, glitch, vhs\n465\t- `lens`: barrel, fisheye\n466\t- `letterbox`: film, widescreen\n467\t- `misregister`: risograph\n468\t- `outline`: bold, hand-drawn, heavy-ink, ink, none, thin\n469\t- `palette`: 1-bit, 512-colours, amber, cga, full-colour, game-boy, pico-8, posterize\n470\t- `paper`: canvas, kraft, newsprint, watercolour-paper\n471\t- `pixel`: 16-bit, 8-bit, bold, chunky, classic, dithered, fine, giant\n472\t- `scanlines`: arcade, fine, heavy, soft\n473\t- `screen`: crt, flat-screen, low-res, old-tv, soft-vignette\n474\t- `selective`: green, red\n475\t- `shading`: as-authored, cartoon, flat-2d, smooth-lit, toon-3-band\n476\t- `sharpen`: crisp\n477\t- `speedlines`: action\n478\t- `split`: moonlight, teal-orange\n479\t- `ssr`: metal\n480\t- `streaks`: anamorphic\n481\t- `stylize`: colour-ascii, coloured-pencil, comic-print, fine-print, gouache, loose-sketch, newsprint, off, oil-paint, pencil, terminal, thick-impasto, underpainting\n482\t- `tiltshift`: miniature\n483\t- `vhs`: worn-tape\n484\t- `vignette`: heavy, soft\n485\t- `watercolor`: wash, wet\n486\t\n487\t**The filters**, in the order the chain draws them, each `{ scope, amount, … }` in a look's `filters` or in\n488\t`post.filters`, with each field's default and range; colours are `'#rrggbb'`. A filter marked (the whole picture)\n489\ttakes only `'all'` or `'off'`; the rest also take `'characters'`, `'objects'`, `'environment'` or two joined by\n490\t`+`.\n491\t\n492\t- `ao` (the whole picture): Ambient occlusion (GTAO, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): creases and corners darken; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–2), `radius` 0.6 (0.05–4 m).\n493\t- `gi` (the whole picture): Bounce light (12 taps a pixel): lit surfaces colour their neighbours, with soft occlusion; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–4), `radius` 1.6 (0.1–6 m).\n494\t- `ssr` (the whole picture): Screen-space reflections on metal surfaces (SSRNode), blurred on rough ones; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–1), `distance` 6 (0.5–50 m), `thickness` 0.15 (0.01–2 m).\n495\t- `air` (the whole picture): Hazy air with light shafts where the sun breaks through, and halos round lamps; never scoped. `haze` 0.35 (0–3), `height` 4 (0.2–20 m), `shafts` 1 (0–3), `forward` 0.4 (-0.9–0.95), `halos` 0.3 (0–3).\n496\t- `bloom` (the whole picture): Bright parts glow (three.js's BloomNode), before the composite, never scoped. `strength` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.9 (0–4), `radius` 0.4 (0–1).\n497\t- `aa` (the whole picture): Edge smoothing over the picture before the filters (engine/gfx/post/aa.ts).\n498\t- `lens` (the whole picture): Lens distortion: barrel (above 0) or pincushion (below), zoomed so the corners stay filled. `distortion` 0.12 (-0.5–0.5).\n499\t- `dof` (the whole picture): Depth of field: sharp round the focus, blurred nearer and farther, as a camera lens draws it. `focus` 0 (0–500 m), `range` 8 (0.5–60 m), `blur` 6 (0–24 px).\n500\t- `tiltshift` (the whole picture): Tilt-shift: a sharp horizontal band, the rest blurred, so the scene looks like a miniature. `center` 0.55 (0–1), `width` 0.18 (0.02–0.8), `blur` 8 (0–24 px).\n501\t- `flare` (the whole picture): Lens flare: ghosts of the brightest parts mirrored through the centre of the screen. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.8 (0–1), `spacing` 0.25 (0.05–0.6).\n502\t- `streaks` (the whole picture): Anamorphic streaks: bright parts drawn out sideways in a tinted line. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.75 (0–1), `length` 48 (4–160 px), `tint` '#5f8dff'.\n503\t- `sharpen`: Sharpen: an unsharp mask that crisps edges and texture. `strength` 0.5 (0–2), `radius` 1 (0.5–4 px).\n504\t- `misregister`: Print misregistration: the red and blue plates offset, as on a cheap print. `offset` 1.2 (0–8 px), `angle` 30 (-180–180 °).\n505\t- `cel`: Comic cel: light in flat bands keeping each colour hue, pushed colour, ink at sharp changes. `bands` 4 (2–8), `punch` 1.3 (0–3), `ink` 0.9 (0–1), `inkWidth` 1.5 (0.5–4 px).\n506\t- `pixel`: Pixel art: whole blocks on the scoped parts and on objects tagged pixel art. `size` 4 (0–32 px), `levels` 0 (0–64), `outline` true, `snap` true, `smooth` false.\n507\t- `palette`: Colour reduction: levels per channel or a fixed palette, with ordered (Bayer) dithering. `palette` 'none' (none|gameboy|pico8|cga|1bit|amber), `levels` 8 (0–64), `dither` 0 (0–1).\n508\t- `watercolor`: Watercolour: colour bleeds, darkens where washes meet, granulates in the paper and wobbles. `wobble` 2.5 (0–8 px), `bleed` 0.3 (0–1), `edges` 0.45 (0–1), `granulation` 0.35 (0–1).\n509\t- `outline`: Outlines where depth jumps, surfaces crease or outline groups meet. `width` 1 (1–4 px), `lines` 0 (0–2160), `darken` 0.35 (0–1), `color` '', `depth` 0.02 (0.001–1), `crease` 0.6 (-1–1), `fade` 0 (0–1), `fadeStart` 25 (0–500 m), `fadeEnd` 120 (1–2000 m), `wobble` 0 (0–4 px), `boil` 0 (0–24).\n510\t- `stylize`: Painterly and print styles: oil paint, CMYK halftone, ASCII and pencil. `style` 'paint' (paint|halftone|ascii|sketch), `size` 4 (1–24 px), `mix` 1 (0–1), `color` 1 (0–1).\n511\t- `paper`: Paper or canvas under the picture: it tints and textures the light areas, ink stays dark. `kind` 'newsprint' (newsprint|coldpress|canvas|kraft), `scale` 1 (0.25–4 ×), `strength` 0.4 (0–1), `color` '#f3ead3'.\n512\t- `grade`: Colour grading. `temperature` 0 (-1–1), `tint` 0 (-1–1), `contrast` 1 (0–3), `brightness` 1 (0–3), `saturation` 1 (0–3), `lift` 0 (-0.3–0.3), `gamma` 1 (0.5–2), `vibrance` 0 (-1–1), `hue` 0 (-180–180 °), `sepia` 0 (0–1), `bleach` 0 (0–1).\n513\t- `split`: Split toning: the shadows toward one colour and the highlights toward another. `shadows` '#2e5f8a', `highlights` '#ffb46b', `balance` 0 (-1–1).\n514\t- `selective`: Selective colour: every colour but one turned grey (a red coat in a grey film). `hue` 0 (0–360 °), `range` 35 (5–120 °).\n515\t- `duotone`: Gradient map: brightness mapped onto a dark, a middle and a light colour (duotone, tritone). `dark` '#1b1442', `mid` '#b04a6f', `light` '#fff1c9'.\n516\t- `scanlines`: Dark lines across the screen, as on a tube. `strength` 0.5 (0–1), `period` 3 (1–16 px)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"d75422b4-ea0f-4559-87c5-52e8d95102ac","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:43.597Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/lean/README.md","content":"# my-3dge, lean\n\nThe my-3dge game engine as a library, the way three.js or Rapier ship: one ES module, nothing to install, build or\ntest. It makes 3D games for the browser: three.js r182 draws on WebGPU, Rapier simulates the physics, a fixed 60 Hz\nsim runs the rules, and HTML/CSS draws the UI. This file is all you need, in a chat with no sandbox too: a game is\none HTML file.\n\n## Start\n\n1. Take `game.html` (in full below): a small game that already plays. You run round an arena and collect the gems\n   before the clock runs out.\n2. Change it into the game you are asked for. Keep its head: it loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr.\n3. Open it in a browser with WebGPU (current Chrome, Edge or Safari; Firefox where it has WebGPU): double-click it, or\n   serve its folder (`npx serve`, `python3 -m http.server`). There is no WebGL fallback.\n\nA bigger game can split its code into module files that each import `my3dge`; serve the folder then, since browsers\nload module files over http, not from a double-clicked page.\n\n## How a game is made\n\n**Two halves.** The _sim_ is the rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them every\nstep, and events. The _page_ calls `createEngine`, which starts the sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into\nintents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame` for the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The\npage reads the sim and never writes it.\n\n**A scene** is a game mode: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings, setup(w), step(w, intents) })`.\n`setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. A page offers its scenes with\n`createEngine({ scenes })`, and `?scene=<id>` picks one.\n\n**The world, `w`,** is what systems get:\n\n- `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields (`number`, `integer`,\n  `string`, `boolean`) with defaults. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an id; `w.query('gem')` returns the\n  entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)` the entity (`.gem`), `w.add(id, 'gem', values)`, `w.remove(id,\n  'gem')` and `w.despawn(id)` do the rest. A component with `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box, which\n  also reads `y` (its bottom), `yaw`, `size` (its side, 0.5 m) and `material` (an id): name a place you only keep\n  otherwise.\n- Systems run in phases, in order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`:\n  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` runs after the physics moved the bodies.\n- `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)`; a page reads\n  `frame.world.events.trace()`.\n- Time is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800).\n- Randomness comes from named streams: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n- Settings are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum, when:\n  'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`, set from the URL (`?game.gems=20`).\n\n**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once a step: `move` (a direction `[x, z]`), buttons held and\npressed, and the game's own keys. A player actor (`spawnActor(w, { player: true, … })`) reads them itself; systems\nget them as their second argument (`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). WASD or the arrows run,\nSpace jumps, Shift dashes.\n\n**The HUD** is the page's: `engine.ui.hud(id, text, { at })` sets a line at one of six places (`top-left`, `top`,\n`top-right`, `bottom-left`, `bottom`, `bottom-right`), and `null` as its text removes it.\n\n**Particles, sound and lights.** The sim says what happened with events; the page turns them into particles and\nsound, so play stays reproducible:\n\n- A sim event bursts every emitter whose `events` names its type, where its payload says (`position`, or `x`, `y`,\n  `z`): `w.emit('hit', { x, y: 1, z })` throws sparks. To burst others on your own event, lay them into an emitter:\n  `defineEmitter('emitter:lit', { events: ['lit'], layers: [{ emitter: 'emitter:fireflies', count: 24 }] })`, then\n  `w.emit('lit', { x, y: 1.2, z })`.\n- Sound plays in the page: `const audio = createAudio();` once (it starts at the player's first key or click), then\n  `audio.play('sfx:coin')`, or in `onFrame` each new event whose payload names one, `w.emit('lit', { sfx:\n  'sfx:powerup', x, z })` in the sim: `for (const e of frame.world.events.trace()) if (e.seq > heard) { heard =\n  e.seq; audio.event(e.type, e.payload); }`.\n- A prop with a light (`prop:lantern`, `prop:lamp`, …) is switchable when spawned with `{ on }` or `{ group }`:\n  `const id = spawnProp(w, { prop: 'prop:lantern', at: [x, 0, z], yaw: 0 }, { on: false })`, then\n  `w.get(id).propLight.on = true` lights it, and `switchGroup(w, group, true)` a group.\n\n**Characters, physics and the rest** are in the API below, area by area, and every built-in id under [Built-in ids\nand the level legend](#built-in-ids-and-the-level-legend): levels as text (`compileLevel`: a tile at column c and\nrow r is at x = (c + 0.5) × tile, z = (r + 0.5) × tile; `level.spawns` are `{ name, position }`, `'hero'` and `'1'`\nto `'9'`) and sites of buildings on a terrain (`defineSite`),\ncharacters (`createCharacter`, Apex Legends' movement with `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`: sprint, slide, mantle,\nclimb; `spawnActor`), physics (`physicsOf(w)` and its `.raycast`, `.overlap`), props, ladders, ziplines, doors,\nwindows and switches, crowds and steering, combat, melee and firearms (`addMelee`, `defineWeapon`), materials and\ntextures as data, looks and post-processing, particle emitters, sound as data, cameras (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=chase`,\nthe shooter's `?cam3=shoulder`), the HUD (`engine.ui.hud`), menus and a store for saves and settings (`createMenu`,\n`createStore`, `createEngine({ store, modal })`) and touch controls.\n\n## Rules that keep play reproducible\n\nThe engine runs the same seed and the same intents to the same state, bit for bit, on every machine. Keep these in\nthe sim (a scene's `setup`, `step` and systems) and replays, `__engine.hash()` and capture and restore keep working:\n\n- No `Date`, timers, `Math.random`, DOM, network or storage in the sim. Randomness from `w.rng`, time in steps.\n- Write the standard `Math` (the sim swaps in portable versions while it steps), but never keep a `Math` function in a\n  variable, and write `Math.pow`, not `**`.\n- Keep the sim's state in components, not in variables beside it; loop over entities in `w.query`'s order.\n- Presentation never writes the sim: the camera, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change nothing a hash sees.\n- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n\n## Make it look different\n\nHow a game looks is data, apart from how it plays: a _look_ is a material style, a chain of filters and a sky. The\nsame game can be a storybook, a film or pixel art with nothing else changed, and no look changes a replay or a hash.\n\n- **Pick a look:** `?post.look=look:storybook` in the URL. To make it the game's own, set it where game.html sets its\n  camera: `if (!search.has('post.look')) search.set('post.look', 'look:storybook');`. Live, from the console:\n  `__engine.set('post.look', 'look:cinematic')`.\n- **Lay looks on and tune them:** `post.combine` lays looks over the one drawn, each `{ look, amount, only, filters }`\n  (`amount` 0 to 1, `only` some of its filters, `filters` changes to them); `post.filters` changes filters field by\n  field; `post.strength` (0 to 1) fades the whole result. In a URL their values are JSON:\n  `?post.combine=[{\"look\":\"look:vignette/soft\",\"amount\":0.6}]&post.filters={\"grade\":{\"scope\":\"all\",\"saturation\":1.3}}`.\n- **Where a filter applies:** its `scope`: `'all'`, `'characters'` (the actors' and crowds' bodies), `'objects'`\n  (props), `'environment'` (the rest: the level, the terrain, stand-in boxes, the sky), two joined by `+`\n  (`'characters+objects'`), or `'off'`. Every filter has an `amount`, 0 (as if it were off) to 1.\n- **Make your own:** `defineLook` before `createEngine`, then pick it like any look:\n\n  ```js\n  defineLook('look:warm-tale', {\n    description: 'A storybook with soft corners and inked heroes.',\n    combine: [{ look: 'look:storybook' }, { look: 'look:vignette/soft', amount: 0.6 }],\n    filters: { outline: { scope: 'characters', width: 2 }, grade: { scope: 'all', temperature: 0.2 } },\n  });\n  ```\n\n- **Read it back:** `__engine.look()` gives the look drawn now: its id, the looks laid on, the strength, and every\n  filter that is on with its scope, amount and numbers; its `filters` pasted into a `defineLook` keeps the picture.\n\nHow they combine: a look's `combine` goes on first, layer by layer, then its own `filters`, each of which replaces a\nlayer's filter of its name whole, its unwritten fields at their defaults (so `look:warm-tale` inks only the\ncharacters, though `look:storybook` inks everything). A layer at an `amount` below 1 fades in a filter the chain lacks\n(its `amount` scaled) and moves one the chain has that far: numbers move, colours mix, and choices (a scope, a kind)\nswitch from 0.5. A filter stays off\nuntil something gives it a scope: `grade: { temperature: 0.2 }` alone does nothing. A layer never turns a filter off,\nnor does `scope: 'off'` in a look's own `filters`: leave a layer's filter out with `only`, or set its scope to `'off'`\nin that layer's `filters`; `post.filters`, applied last, turns one off outright. Only filters are laid on: the style,\nthe sky and the rest come from the look `post.look` names. Changing a filter's numbers, scope or amount, or the looks\nlaid on, builds no shader. Every look, what it draws, and every filter are under [Looks and\nfilters](#looks-and-filters), below game.html.\n\n## Look inside a running game\n\nThe browser console has `__engine`: `__engine.help()` lists it all; `step(n)`, `hash()`, `state('round')`,\n`set('game.gems', 20)`, `seed(7)`, `camera.set('cam=iso')`, `look()`, and `describe('material')`, a kind's fields and\nids. The URL takes the same: `?seed=7&game.gems=20&cam=iso`.\nErrors name their fix, and docs/ERRORS.md in the engine's repository lists every code.\n\n## Versions\n\nThe head's URLs pin three.js and Rapier, and take the engine at `@main`, its latest: jsDelivr serves a new commit\nwithin 12 hours, and a browser may keep the copy it loaded for up to 7 days (jsDelivr's cache header). To freeze a\ngame on one engine, replace `main` with a commit of https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge in its two engine URLs\n(`my3dge.js`, `my3dge.css`).\n\nMore: the engine's repository has the full guide (docs/GAME-GUIDE.md), every export (docs/API.md) and the source. An\nagent that can run commands and wants the engine's checks too can start from the repository's README instead.\nLicense: MIT; `my3dge.js` carries @stdlib functions (Apache-2.0): see LICENSE beside this file.\n\n## game.html\n\n<!-- lean:game:start -->\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\" />\n    <title>Gem run: a my-3dge game</title>\n    <!--\n      A whole game in one page: run round an arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out. Make your game by\n      changing this file (lean/README.md says how). Open it in a browser with WebGPU: double-click it, or serve its\n      folder. The block below loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr; keep it as it is.\n    -->\n    <!-- lean:head:start -->\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.css\" />\n    <script type=\"importmap\">\n      {\n        \"imports\": {\n          \"my3dge\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.js\",\n          \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n          \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n          \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.js\",\n          \"three/addons/\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/examples/jsm/\",\n          \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.21.0/dist/rapier.mjs\"\n        }\n      }\n    </script>\n    <!-- lean:head:end -->\n    <style>\n      html,\n      body {\n        margin: 0;\n        height: 100%;\n        overflow: hidden;\n        background: #15171c;\n      }\n      canvas {\n        display: block;\n        width: 100vw;\n        height: 100dvh;\n        outline: none;\n        touch-action: none;\n      }\n      #msg {\n        position: fixed;\n        inset: 0;\n        display: flex;\n        align-items: center;\n        justify-content: center;\n        padding: 24px;\n        font:\n          18px system-ui,\n          sans-serif;\n        color: #c9ccd4;\n        text-align: center;\n        white-space: pre-line;\n        pointer-events: none;\n      }\n    </style>\n    <script>\n      // Until the engine starts, a script that fails shows here in one line, never as a blank page.\n      window.__engine = { ready: false, errors: [] };\n      function failed(message) {\n        if (typeof window.__engine.help === 'function') return; // the engine shows its own errors\n        window.__engine.errors.push({\n          code: 'APP_START',\n          message: message,\n          fix: 'the browser console names the cause',\n        });\n        document.getElementById('msg').textContent = 'The game failed to start: ' + message;\n      }\n      addEventListener('error', (event) => failed(event.message || 'a script failed to load'));\n      addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => failed(String(event.reason?.message ?? event.reason)));\n    </script>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas>\n    <div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\">Loading…</div>\n    <script type=\"module\">\n      import {\n        addLevelBodies,\n        compileLevel,\n        createCharacter,\n        createEngine,\n        defineComponent,\n        defineScene,\n        defineSettings,\n        physicsOf,\n        spawnActor,\n      } from 'my3dge';\n\n      // ---- The sim: everything that decides play. It runs 60 steps a second, the same on every machine. ----\n\n      // The arena as text: # wall, . floor, P pillar, w low wall (jump it), @ the player's spawn. 1 m a character.\n      const ARENA_TEXT = `---\ndescription: The arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump, the player's spawn.\n---\n##################\n#................#\n#..@.............#\n#.......PP.......#\n#.......PP.......#\n#................#\n#....w......w....#\n#....w......w....#\n#................#\n#.......PP.......#\n#.......PP.......#\n#................#\n#................#\n##################\n`;\n      const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'game.html', name: 'arena' });\n\n      // Settings: change them from the URL (?game.gems=20) or the console (__engine.set('game.gems', 20)).\n      defineSettings({\n        'game.gems': {\n          type: 'integer', default: 10, minimum: 1, maximum: 40, when: 'scene', description: 'How many gems.',\n        },\n        'game.seconds': {\n          type: 'number', default: 30, minimum: 5, maximum: 600, unit: 's', when: 'scene', description: 'The clock.',\n        },\n        'game.speed': {\n          type: 'number', default: 6, minimum: 1, maximum: 20, unit: 'm/s', when: 'scene', description: 'Run speed.',\n        },\n      });\n\n      // Components: data on entities. A component with x and z in metres and a material is drawn by the page.\n      defineComponent('gem', {\n        description: 'A gem to collect.',\n        fields: {\n          x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n          y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up.' },\n          z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n          yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n          size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'Its size.' },\n          material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'How it is drawn.' },\n        },\n      });\n      defineComponent('round', {\n        description: 'The round: gems collected, steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n        fields: {\n          gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems.' },\n          collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many are collected.' },\n          left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'Steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n          state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n        },\n      });\n\n      const REACH = 0.8; // how close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m\n      const START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero').position;\n\n      // Scatters the gems over floor tiles at least 3 m from the spawn, drawn from the named random stream 'gems'.\n      function scatterGems(w, count) {\n        const tiles = [];\n        ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n          const [x, z] = [((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile, (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile];\n          if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= 3) tiles.push([x, z]);\n        });\n        const rng = w.rng('gems');\n        for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n          const [[x, z]] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n          w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n        }\n      }\n\n      // Ends the round: the player stops, and the event says how it ended.\n      function endRound(w, round, state) {\n        round.state = state;\n        for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n          e.actor.player = false;\n          e.actor.intents = {};\n        }\n        w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems });\n      }\n\n      // A system: spins the gems and collects those an actor reaches (a despawn waits for the step's end).\n      function collect(w) {\n        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n        for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n        const taken = new Set();\n        for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n          for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n            if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n            taken.add(gem.id);\n            w.despawn(gem.id);\n            round.collected += 1;\n            w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, collected: round.collected });\n          }\n        }\n        if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n      }\n\n      // A system: the clock counts whole steps, never summed seconds; at 0 the round is lost.\n      function clock(w) {\n        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n        round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n        if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n      }\n\n      // The scene: the level, its physics, and what happens when it starts.\n      const play = defineScene('game:play', {\n        description: 'Run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n        level: 'level:arena',\n        physics: true,\n        setup(w) {\n          addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n          const gems = w.settings.get('game.gems');\n          w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n          scatterGems(w, gems);\n          const character = createCharacter({ speed: w.settings.get('game.speed'), jumpSpeed: 9 });\n          const anim = { build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' }; // a humanoid that animates itself\n          spawnActor(w, { at: [START[0], START[1], START[2]], character, player: true, anim });\n          w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n          w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n        },\n      });\n\n      // ---- The page: draws the run and shows the HUD. It only reads the sim, never changes it. ----\n\n      const search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n      if (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase'); // the camera follows the player\n      let engine = null;\n      let ended = false;\n\n      // Each drawn frame: the gems, the clock, the keys, and how the round ended.\n      function showRound({ world }) {\n        const entity = world.query('round')[0];\n        if (!engine || !entity) return;\n        const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n        engine.ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n        const color = left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '';\n        engine.ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color });\n        engine.ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n        ended = state !== 'playing';\n        engine.ui.hud('end', ended ? `${state === 'won' ? 'Every gem!' : 'Time!'} Enter: play again` : null, {\n          at: 'top',\n        });\n      }\n\n      engine = await createEngine({\n        canvas: document.querySelector('#view'),\n        scenes: [play.id],\n        levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'game.html' } },\n        search: `?${search}`,\n        onFrame: showRound,\n      });\n      document.getElementById('msg').hidden = true;\n\n      // Enter after a round: the next round, on the next seed.\n      addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n        if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended) engine.inspector.seed(engine.inspector.seed() + 1);\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n<!-- lean:game:end -->\n\n## Looks and filters\n\n<!-- lean:looks:start -->\n**Whole looks**, each with what it draws (its style and sky where it names them, its filters and their scopes):\n\n- `look:16-bit`: The whole scene as 16-bit pixel art with a slightly vivid grade. _filters pixel, grade._\n- `look:anime-action`: An action frame: comic cel bands, bold lines and speed lines. _filters cel, outline, speedlines._\n- `look:arcade-cabinet`: An arcade cabinet: a curved tube's grille, glow and lines. _filters bloom, scanlines, crt, screen._\n- `look:arcade-crt`: Low resolution on a curved tube with scanlines, glow and colour fringes. _filters bloom, scanlines, chroma, screen._\n- `look:cartoon`: Toon shading everywhere, bold ink outlines and vivid colour. _style toon; filters outline, grade._\n- `look:cinematic`: A film: teal and orange, anamorphic streaks, soft corners and widescreen bars. _filters streaks, grade, split, vignette, letterbox._\n- `look:clean`: No filter: the picture as the scene draws it, in its own style. _nothing._\n- `look:comic-book`: Printed dots on the world, inked heroes, punchy colour. _filters outline, stylize (environment), grade (characters+objects)._\n- `look:comic-noir`: A black-and-white world; the characters and objects keep their colour, inked. _filters outline, grade (environment), screen._\n- `look:flat-paper`: Flat colours, thin outlines and a faded grade, like a printed page. _style flat; filters outline, grade, grain._\n- `look:game-boy`: Everything in four greens, pixelated and dithered. _filters pixel, palette._\n- `look:game-boy-world`: The environment in four Game Boy greens; the characters and objects in full colour with ink outlines. _filters palette (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n- `look:hacker-terminal`: The game as green characters on a curved, scanlined screen. _filters stylize, scanlines, screen._\n- `look:hd-2d`: Pixel-art characters and objects in a soft, glowing 3D world with dark corners. _filters bloom, pixel (characters+objects), grade (environment), screen._\n- `look:hologram-heroes`: Characters and objects flicker like holograms: cool, scanlined and fringed. The world stays clean. _filters bloom, grade (characters+objects), scanlines (characters+objects), grain (characters+objects), chroma (characters+objects)._\n- `look:lamplit-fog`: A foggy night where every lamp glows in the air. _filters air, grade._\n- `look:miniature`: A model railway: tilt-shift blur, crisp and bright colours. _filters tiltshift, sharpen, grade._\n- `look:newspaper`: Black dots on cream paper, everything. _filters stylize._\n- `look:oil-painting`: The whole scene in oil paint: flat strokes, brush streaks, canvas. _filters stylize, grade._\n- `look:painted-world`: A painted backdrop; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n- `look:pixel-heroes`: A clean, sharp world; the characters and objects are outlined pixel art. _filters pixel (characters+objects)._\n- `look:pixel-world`: The environment is pixel art; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked. _filters pixel (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n- `look:posterized-world`: The environment in a few flat shades; the characters and objects in full colour. _filters palette (environment)._\n- `look:red-coat`: A grey film where only red keeps its colour, grainy and dark at the edges. _filters grade, selective, vignette, grain._\n- `look:riso-print`: A risograph print: ember duotone, plates out of register, newsprint. _filters misregister, paper, duotone._\n- `look:sketchbook`: Pencil hatching and contours on paper. _filters stylize._\n- `look:sketched-world`: A coloured-pencil world; the characters and objects inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n- `look:storybook`: A storybook: watercolour on cold-pressed paper with a thin ink line. _filters watercolor, outline, paper._\n- `look:sunbeams`: Dusty air, sunlight streaming through the gaps, a soft glow. _filters air, bloom._\n- `look:vhs-tape`: A home video: worn tape, soft scanlines, noise and dark corners. _filters scanlines, vhs, vignette, grain, chroma._\n\n**One-filter presets**, `look:<group>/<name>`: each turns its one filter on over everything, to draw alone or lay\non with `combine` (the `shading` group sets the material style instead, and `screen` the screen's own glass):\n\n- `aa`: smooth\n- `air`: clear-air, dusty-sunbeams, lamplight, light-haze, morning-mist, pea-soup, smoky-hall\n- `ao`: corners\n- `bloom`: dreamy, neon, no-glow, soft\n- `cel`: comic\n- `crt`: arcade-tube\n- `dof`: portrait\n- `duotone`: dusk, ember, ocean\n- `flare`: sun\n- `gi`: bounce-light\n- `grade`: bleach-bypass, cool, faded, lifted, neutral, night, noir, sepia, vivid, warm\n- `grain`: clean, film, glitch, vhs\n- `lens`: barrel, fisheye\n- `letterbox`: film, widescreen\n- `misregister`: risograph\n- `outline`: bold, hand-drawn, heavy-ink, ink, none, thin\n- `palette`: 1-bit, 512-colours, amber, cga, full-colour, game-boy, pico-8, posterize\n- `paper`: canvas, kraft, newsprint, watercolour-paper\n- `pixel`: 16-bit, 8-bit, bold, chunky, classic, dithered, fine, giant\n- `scanlines`: arcade, fine, heavy, soft\n- `screen`: crt, flat-screen, low-res, old-tv, soft-vignette\n- `selective`: green, red\n- `shading`: as-authored, cartoon, flat-2d, smooth-lit, toon-3-band\n- `sharpen`: crisp\n- `speedlines`: action\n- `split`: moonlight, teal-orange\n- `ssr`: metal\n- `streaks`: anamorphic\n- `stylize`: colour-ascii, coloured-pencil, comic-print, fine-print, gouache, loose-sketch, newsprint, off, oil-paint, pencil, terminal, thick-impasto, underpainting\n- `tiltshift`: miniature\n- `vhs`: worn-tape\n- `vignette`: heavy, soft\n- `watercolor`: wash, wet\n\n**The filters**, in the order the chain draws them, each `{ scope, amount, … }` in a look's `filters` or in\n`post.filters`, with each field's default and range; colours are `'#rrggbb'`. A filter marked (the whole picture)\ntakes only `'all'` or `'off'`; the rest also take `'characters'`, `'objects'`, `'environment'` or two joined by\n`+`.\n\n- `ao` (the whole picture): Ambient occlusion (GTAO, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): creases and corners darken; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–2), `radius` 0.6 (0.05–4 m).\n- `gi` (the whole picture): Bounce light (12 taps a pixel): lit surfaces colour their neighbours, with soft occlusion; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–4), `radius` 1.6 (0.1–6 m).\n- `ssr` (the whole picture): Screen-space reflections on metal surfaces (SSRNode), blurred on rough ones; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–1), `distance` 6 (0.5–50 m), `thickness` 0.15 (0.01–2 m).\n- `air` (the whole picture): Hazy air with light shafts where the sun breaks through, and halos round lamps; never scoped. `haze` 0.35 (0–3), `height` 4 (0.2–20 m), `shafts` 1 (0–3), `forward` 0.4 (-0.9–0.95), `halos` 0.3 (0–3).\n- `bloom` (the whole picture): Bright parts glow (three.js's BloomNode), before the composite, never scoped. `strength` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.9 (0–4), `radius` 0.4 (0–1).\n- `aa` (the whole picture): Edge smoothing over the picture before the filters (engine/gfx/post/aa.ts).\n- `lens` (the whole picture): Lens distortion: barrel (above 0) or pincushion (below), zoomed so the corners stay filled. `distortion` 0.12 (-0.5–0.5).\n- `dof` (the whole picture): Depth of field: sharp round the focus, blurred nearer and farther, as a camera lens draws it. `focus` 0 (0–500 m), `range` 8 (0.5–60 m), `blur` 6 (0–24 px).\n- `tiltshift` (the whole picture): Tilt-shift: a sharp horizontal band, the rest blurred, so the scene looks like a miniature. `center` 0.55 (0–1), `width` 0.18 (0.02–0.8), `blur` 8 (0–24 px).\n- `flare` (the whole picture): Lens flare: ghosts of the brightest parts mirrored through the centre of the screen. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.8 (0–1), `spacing` 0.25 (0.05–0.6).\n- `streaks` (the whole picture): Anamorphic streaks: bright parts drawn out sideways in a tinted line. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.75 (0–1), `length` 48 (4–160 px), `tint` '#5f8dff'.\n- `sharpen`: Sharpen: an unsharp mask that crisps edges and texture. `strength` 0.5 (0–2), `radius` 1 (0.5–4 px).\n- `misregister`: Print misregistration: the red and blue plates offset, as on a cheap print. `offset` 1.2 (0–8 px), `angle` 30 (-180–180 °).\n- `cel`: Comic cel: light in flat bands keeping each colour hue, pushed colour, ink at sharp changes. `bands` 4 (2–8), `punch` 1.3 (0–3), `ink` 0.9 (0–1), `inkWidth` 1.5 (0.5–4 px).\n- `pixel`: Pixel art: whole blocks on the scoped parts and on objects tagged pixel art. `size` 4 (0–32 px), `levels` 0 (0–64), `outline` true, `snap` true, `smooth` false.\n- `palette`: Colour reduction: levels per channel or a fixed palette, with ordered (Bayer) dithering. `palette` 'none' (none|gameboy|pico8|cga|1bit|amber), `levels` 8 (0–64), `dither` 0 (0–1).\n- `watercolor`: Watercolour: colour bleeds, darkens where washes meet, granulates in the paper and wobbles. `wobble` 2.5 (0–8 px), `bleed` 0.3 (0–1), `edges` 0.45 (0–1), `granulation` 0.35 (0–1).\n- `outline`: Outlines where depth jumps, surfaces crease or outline groups meet. `width` 1 (1–4 px), `lines` 0 (0–2160), `darken` 0.35 (0–1), `color` '', `depth` 0.02 (0.001–1), `crease` 0.6 (-1–1), `fade` 0 (0–1), `fadeStart` 25 (0–500 m), `fadeEnd` 120 (1–2000 m), `wobble` 0 (0–4 px), `boil` 0 (0–24).\n- `stylize`: Painterly and print styles: oil paint, CMYK halftone, ASCII and pencil. `style` 'paint' (paint|halftone|ascii|sketch), `size` 4 (1–24 px), `mix` 1 (0–1), `color` 1 (0–1).\n- `paper`: Paper or canvas under the picture: it tints and textures the light areas, ink stays dark. `kind` 'newsprint' (newsprint|coldpress|canvas|kraft), `scale` 1 (0.25–4 ×), `strength` 0.4 (0–1), `color` '#f3ead3'.\n- `grade`: Colour grading. `temperature` 0 (-1–1), `tint` 0 (-1–1), `contrast` 1 (0–3), `brightness` 1 (0–3), `saturation` 1 (0–3), `lift` 0 (-0.3–0.3), `gamma` 1 (0.5–2), `vibrance` 0 (-1–1), `hue` 0 (-180–180 °), `sepia` 0 (0–1), `bleach` 0 (0–1).\n- `split`: Split toning: the shadows toward one colour and the highlights toward another. `shadows` '#2e5f8a', `highlights` '#ffb46b', `balance` 0 (-1–1).\n- `selective`: Selective colour: every colour but one turned grey (a red coat in a grey film). `hue` 0 (0–360 °), `range` 35 (5–120 °).\n- `duotone`: Gradient map: brightness mapped onto a dark, a middle and a light colour (duotone, tritone). `dark` '#1b1442', `mid` '#b04a6f', `light` '#fff1c9'.\n- `scanlines`: Dark lines across the screen, as on a tube. `strength` 0.5 (0–1), `period` 3 (1–16 px).","numLines":516,"startLine":1,"totalLines":854,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DMDf566JcmFfwBAD3DY5HJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
