{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9K2RWfDd7bs4Kg4Xzo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TvG81eGDzrbhv4veFeQJAB","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/tools/game/template/AGENTS.md.tmpl"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31534,"cache_read_input_tokens":97752,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31534},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"8cf29eb3-cf31-4bac-9f56-59889732c419","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:52.430Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9K27QPzuJCWfG8EbQ1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TvG81eGDzrbhv4veFeQJAB":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/tools/game/template/AGENTS.md.tmpl"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TvG81eGDzrbhv4veFeQJAB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md: {{name}}\n2\t\n3\t{{name}} is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\n4\tWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\n5\tis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n6\t\n7\tBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\n8\tit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\n9\tthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n10\t\n11\t## Set up (once per machine)\n12\t\n13\t    bash scripts/setup.sh\n14\t\n15\tIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n16\t`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n17\t\n18\t## The loop\n19\t\n20\t1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n21\t   labs/game/main.ts.\n22\t2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n23\t3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n24\t4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n25\t   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n26\t   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n27\t   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n28\t5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n29\t\n30\t`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n31\t\n32\t## Rules\n33\t\n34\t- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n35\t  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n36\t  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n37\t  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n38\t- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n39\t- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n40\t  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n41\t- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n42\t  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n43\t  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n44\t- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n45\t- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n46\t  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n47\t- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n48\t\n49\t## Done means\n50\t\n51\t`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\n52\tpurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\n53\tin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"0dbd9b2c-9b25-4d52-888b-37837644aff9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:52.449Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/.research/my-3dge/tools/game/template/AGENTS.md.tmpl","content":"# AGENTS.md: {{name}}\n\n{{name}} is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\nWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\nis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n\nBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\nit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\nthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n\n## Set up (once per machine)\n\n    bash scripts/setup.sh\n\nIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n   labs/game/main.ts.\n2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n\n`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n\n## Done means\n\n`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\npurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\nin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n","numLines":54,"startLine":1,"totalLines":54}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TvG81eGDzrbhv4veFeQJAB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
