{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KHUpv2xDgFWu9T3CyHJcGc","type":"tool_result","content":"total 112\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha  4096 Nov  4 21:23 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 micha micha  4096 Nov  4 21:23 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 33330 Nov  4 21:23 arena.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 29231 Nov  4 21:23 ghost.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 33011 Nov  4 21:23 parallel.html\nREADME.md\nhub\nkit\nrapier\nthree\nthree-addons.exclude\nvendor\n---\n# Working in this repo\n\n## Finish every change on main\n\n- When a `/goal` is complete, or a prompt has changed the repo, commit and\n  push to `origin/main` right away.\n- Pushing does not deploy. Vercel usage costs too much to build every\n  change, so Git deploys are off (`\"git\": { \"deploymentEnabled\": false }`\n  in `vercel.json`) and the site runs locally: `pnpm dev`, then\n  http://127.0.0.1:7417. Deploy to Vercel only when Michael explicitly asks,\n  for a major update: push with `deploymentEnabled` set to `true` (that push\n  deploys; check it reaches READY), then set it back to `false` in the next\n  commit, which does not deploy.\n- Work on `main`. If a branch is ever needed, merge it into main and delete\n  it (locally and on GitHub) in the same session. Never leave a local\n  branch, an unpushed commit, a stash, or an open PR behind.\n- Before starting work, run `git fetch --prune` and look at\n  `gh pr list`. Anything open gets resolved first.\n\n## Pull requests from others\n\nDecide each one; do not let it sit. Accept it (merge to main, delete the\nbranch), reject it (close it with a one-line reason), or replace it (close\nit, and land a better version on main that says which PR it replaces).\n\n## Keep it simple and clean\n\n- No dependencies, no build step, no generated files committed\n  (`data/games.json` is gitignored and rebuilt by `scripts/scan.mjs`).\n- Run `pnpm test` (or `node --test \"scripts/**/*.test.mjs\"`) before\n  committing code.\n- Delete what is no longer used instead of leaving it around.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\nRoll back rather than leave half-finished work. Revert the commit\n(`git revert`) or restore the last good state and push that, so main is\nalways a working wall. Say what was rolled back and why.\n---\n267:If a game misbehaves in a frame, *Open on its own page* in its Details drawer\n348:  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n422:record, **F** favourites, **O** opens a game on its own page, **/** writes a\n489:In short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\n518:each with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\n542:node scripts/retrofit.mjs pick --wave 1 > .cache/wave1.txt   # one build of each title: readable, no WebGPU needed; smallest first\n552:  (`hub-check --draws-only`), and spends nothing on one it can't: a WebGPU-only\n553:  game in a browser without WebGPU, even when its title screen shows. On a\n564:  on its own compared with the original.\n592:After the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\n624:- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n842:  check3d.py      one 3D game in headless browsers, WebGL2 and WebGPU\n853:The games on the shared kit run on the same stack: three.js r186 with `WebGPURenderer`\n854:(WebGL2 when WebGPU is missing), loaded once from `lib/three` through an\n---\naccount.test.mjs\natlas.test.mjs\naudio-inventory.test.mjs\naudio-review-browser.mjs\naudit3d.mjs\naudit3d.test.mjs\ncatalogue.test.mjs\ncheck-libraries.mjs\ncheck3d.py\ncouch.test.mjs\ndates.test.mjs\ndeck.test.mjs\ndesktop-import.test.mjs\ndev.mjs\nexplore-browser.mjs\nexplore.test.mjs\nfill.mjs\nfill.test.mjs\nfilter.test.mjs\nframes.test.mjs\nfriends.test.mjs\nhub-check.mjs\nhub-client.mjs\nhub-client.test.mjs\nhub-pages.test.mjs\nhub.test.mjs\nimport-catalogue.test.mjs\nimport-hub-pages.mjs\nimport-matrix-runs.mjs\nimport-registry.test.mjs\ningest.mjs\nintake.test.mjs\nkit.test.mjs\nlayout.test.mjs\nlib\nlibraries.test.mjs\nlibrary-browser.mjs\nmatrix-runs.test.mjs\nmatrix3d.py\nmesh.test.mjs\nmigrate-added-dates.mjs\nnames.test.mjs\nnet.test.mjs\nparse.test.mjs\nrelease.mjs\nrelease.test.mjs\nretrofit.mjs\nretrofit.test.mjs\nroundup-boot.test.mjs\nscan.mjs\nseed-import-registry.mjs\nsession-browser.mjs\nsession-library.test.mjs\nsession.test.mjs\nshots.mjs\nsimilar.test.mjs\nsweep3d.py\ntimeline.test.mjs\nvendor-libraries.mjs\nviews.test.mjs\n32: * cannot be checked here. A WebGPU-only game without WebGPU is caught even\n35: * Chromium; on a machine without a GPU, SwiftShader gives such games a\n37: *   --browser-args \"--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\"\n81:/** Extra flags for the browser, e.g. the ones that give it WebGPU where the machine can (lib/kit/README.md). */\n121:  // What the page asked of the graphics stack: a WebGPU adapter it never got, and\n123:  const gfx = (window.__gfx = { askedForWebGPU: false, gotAdapter: false, webgl: false, twoD: false });\n130:        gfx.askedForWebGPU = true;\n289:    // A WebGL or WebGPU canvas often reads back blank from a script, so look at the screen instead:\n314:  const noWebGPU = g?.askedForWebGPU && !g.gotAdapter && !g.webgl && !g.twoD;\n315:  const asked = g ? `WebGPU ${g.askedForWebGPU ? (g.gotAdapter ? 'asked, adapter' : 'asked, no adapter') : 'not asked'}; WebGL ${g.webgl ? 'yes' : 'no'}; 2D ${g.twoD ? 'yes' : 'no'}` : 'not seen';\n316:  check('on its own: this browser can run it', !noWebGPU, noWebGPU ? `it asked for WebGPU, this browser has no adapter, and drew nothing with WebGL or 2D instead (${asked})` : asked);","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"62f16dc3-2245-4fc3-95bc-ff64eee5536a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:38.411Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 112\n