{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M92MUM5qPfT6V4ukd7DuX1","type":"tool_result","content":"340\nparallel: 183\nghost: 1\nshared-world: 0\nrace: 30\nco-op: 94\nversus: 32\ngames/Star-Parodisco-Star-Parodier-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261029-1057.html\ngames/Avow-and-Behold-Avowed-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261029-1405.html\ngames/Deep-Drake-Trouble-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261029-0753.html\ngames/Alien-Shoulder-Alien-Soldier-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261029-0820.html\ngames/Waku-Waku-Lucky-Waku-Waku-7-gpt-6-astra-xhigh-20261029-1116.html\n---\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash\n---\ntakes it off, up to twelve. They play first, in that order, whatever the\nfilters say; a pick that does not seat the players yet keeps its place until\nit does. A session hosted from a game's page in the Friends view\n(`/session?first=<slug>`) opens with that game on the list; the address drops\nit once the session has it. Everyone sees the list, and the next game's card\nsays *The host's pick*.\n\nAfter the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\nseats everyone, in an order fixed by the session's seed, and none comes round\ntwice until all have been played. The host can narrow which games are dealt,\nin the lobby before the first round or between rounds: by mode (Parallel,\nGhost, Shared world), by round length (Short, Medium, Long), by genre, and by\nwhat made the game: the model, the effort it was made at and the month it was\nadded. Each choice shows how many games it leaves, and a row with nothing\nchosen keeps every game. Each round is played from the protocol's\nstart signal to its result. The moment it ends, the results land over it\n(places, points, the game's stats and awards, the table moving) while the next\ngame is already loading underneath; a quick spin of the deck then shows the\nnext game, how to win it and its controls (or the game shows its own card, in\nits own style), and lifts on its ready prompt:\nabout half a minute from one game to the next, less when everyone is ready\n(the host sets the card's countdown in the lobby: 10, 20 or 30 seconds). The table: one point for every\nplayer beaten, then wins, then average place. When the format says so, a final\nscreen crowns the winner with a podium and the session awards (Showstopper,\nHot streak, Steady hand). The seated tab with the lowest seat hosts; if it\nleaves, the next seat takes over, and a round it was running is void.\n\n**Across devices** (`/session`):\n- The host signs in with Google, picks a name, and gets an invite link,\n  `/session#<code>`.\n- Friends open the link, pick a name and play as guests, no account needed.\n  Behind the scenes each guest gets an anonymous account.\n- Each seat shows its connection. The host can remove a player for the rest\n  of the session.\n- Every round is saved: the game, who placed where, the points, and each\n  player's stats and awards.\n- **Relay only** in the lobby sends all of a player's connections through the\n  TURN relay, so the others never see that device's IP address (remembered in\n  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n  are dealt instead, so a session always has something to play.\n- Parallel games far outnumber the ones played together, so the deal\n  alternates between the two, and a parallel round shows a live\n  scoreboard over the game, top right, folded with a tap.\n- A session nobody ended is ended after a day without activity (an hourly\n  job, `supabase/migrations/0005_end_stale_sessions.sql`).\n\nHow it works:\n- Presence, round control and the WebRTC handshake go over the session's\n  private Supabase Realtime channel.\n- Game traffic goes straight between the players' browsers (a WebRTC mesh,\n  `assets/js/session/mesh.js`), so a busy game costs the free plan nothing.\n- Where browsers can't connect directly, a Cloudflare TURN relay takes over.\n  Its short-lived credentials come from the `turn` Edge Function.\n- Every player keeps the host's time (`clock.js`), which is how rounds start\n  together.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"4f1b744b-d474-4752-80f6-247961906197","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:27:46.351Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"340\nparallel: 183\nghost: 1\nshared-world: 0\nrace: 30\nco-op: 94\nversus: 32\ngames/Star-Parodisco-Star-Parodier-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261029-1057.html\ngames/Avow-and-Behold-Avowed-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261029-1405.html\ngames/Deep-Drake-Trouble-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261029-0753.html\ngames/Alien-Shoulder-Alien-Soldier-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261029-0820.html\ngames/Waku-Waku-Lucky-Waku-Waku-7-gpt-6-astra-xhigh-20261029-1116.html\n---\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash\n---\ntakes it off, up to twelve. They play first, in that order, whatever the\nfilters say; a pick that does not seat the players yet keeps its place until\nit does. A session hosted from a game's page in the Friends view\n(`/session?first=<slug>`) opens with that game on the list; the address drops\nit once the session has it. Everyone sees the list, and the next game's card\nsays *The host's pick*.\n\nAfter the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\nseats everyone, in an order fixed by the session's seed, and none comes round\ntwice until all have been played. The host can narrow which games are dealt,\nin the lobby before the first round or between rounds: by mode (Parallel,\nGhost, Shared world), by round length (Short, Medium, Long), by genre, and by\nwhat made the game: the model, the effort it was made at and the month it was\nadded. Each choice shows how many games it leaves, and a row with nothing\nchosen keeps every game. Each round is played from the protocol's\nstart signal to its result. The moment it ends, the results land over it\n(places, points, the game's stats and awards, the table moving) while the next\ngame is already loading underneath; a quick spin of the deck then shows the\nnext game, how to win it and its controls (or the game shows its own card, in\nits own style), and lifts on its ready prompt:\nabout half a minute from one game to the next, less when everyone is ready\n(the host sets the card's countdown in the lobby: 10, 20 or 30 seconds). The table: one point for every\nplayer beaten, then wins, then average place. When the format says so, a final\nscreen crowns the winner with a podium and the session awards (Showstopper,\nHot streak, Steady hand). The seated tab with the lowest seat hosts; if it\nleaves, the next seat takes over, and a round it was running is void.\n\n**Across devices** (`/session`):\n- The host signs in with Google, picks a name, and gets an invite link,\n  `/session#<code>`.\n- Friends open the link, pick a name and play as guests, no account needed.\n  Behind the scenes each guest gets an anonymous account.\n- Each seat shows its connection. The host can remove a player for the rest\n  of the session.\n- Every round is saved: the game, who placed where, the points, and each\n  player's stats and awards.\n- **Relay only** in the lobby sends all of a player's connections through the\n  TURN relay, so the others never see that device's IP address (remembered in\n  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n  are dealt instead, so a session always has something to play.\n- Parallel games far outnumber the ones played together, so the deal\n  alternates between the two, and a parallel round shows a live\n  scoreboard over the game, top right, folded with a tap.\n- A session nobody ended is ended after a day without activity (an hourly\n  job, `supabase/migrations/0005_end_stale_sessions.sql`).\n\nHow it works:\n- Presence, round control and the WebRTC handshake go over the session's\n  private Supabase Realtime channel.\n- Game traffic goes straight between the players' browsers (a WebRTC mesh,\n  `assets/js/session/mesh.js`), so a busy game costs the free plan nothing.\n- Where browsers can't connect directly, a Cloudflare TURN relay takes over.\n  Its short-lived credentials come from the `turn` Edge Function.\n- Every player keeps the host's time (`clock.js`), which is how rounds start\n  together.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01M92MUM5qPfT6V4ukd7DuX1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvKRp8rMFEmLY3trVRnE3P","type":"tool_result","content":"/**\n * bridge.js — the hub's end of one game frame in a session.\n *\n * A game speaks the AI Hub Session Protocol over postMessage (lib/hub/README.md).\n * The bridge checks every message, enforces the limits, and routes it: control\n * messages (hello, ready, result, finish, error) to whoever coordinates the\n * round (lib/hub/round.js), game traffic straight to the other players, and a\n * game's answers on its own card to the page that holds it. What\n * comes back from the network is checked again before it reaches the game,\n * and anything from another round is dropped, so a slow packet can never land\n * in the next game.\n *\n * The network is anything with `send(toKey, envelope, {reliable})`: the\n * simulated one below (harness, tests), a BroadcastChannel between tabs, or a\n * WebRTC mesh. Envelopes:\n *   {k:'c', r, m}       to the coordinator: a message from this game\n *   {k:'h', r, m}       from the coordinator: welcome, start, leave, time-up, end\n *   {k:'g', r, f, d}    game traffic from player id f\n *   {k:'p', r, f, d}    player id f's progress, {score, label}, for the scoreboard\n */\n\nexport const LIMITS = Object.freeze({\n  maxBytes: 16_384,\n  ratePerS: 20,\n  burst: 40,\n  queueMessages: 64,\n  queueBytes: 262_144,\n  /**\n   * progress() reaches the hub about once a second. Two closer than this are a\n   * report delivered late (a long task in the game) or a client misbehaving:\n   * the later is dropped, and the next report, a second on, has the newest score.\n   */\n  progressMs: 250,\n  label: 24,\n});\n\nconst FROM_GAME = new Set(['hello', 'ready', 'send', 'result', 'finish', 'error', 'progress', 'fullscreen', 'answer']);\n\n/** A progress report, checked: a finite score and an optional short label. Null when it is not one. */\nexport function cleanProgress(p) {\n  if (!p || typeof p !== 'object') return null;\n  // A number, or text that is one: never null, '', true or [], which Number() would make 0 or 1.\n  const score = typeof p.score === 'number' || (typeof p.score === 'string' && p.score.trim() !== '') ? Number(p.score) : NaN;\n  if (!Number.isFinite(score)) return null;\n  const label = typeof p.label === 'string' ? p.label.trim().slice(0, LIMITS.label) : '';\n  return label ? { score, label } : { score };\n}\n\n/**\n * @param {object} o\n * @param {string} o.key              this player's key in the session\n * @param {number} o.round\n * @param {string} o.coordinator      key of whoever runs round.js\n * @param {(msg:object) => void} o.post            to the game frame\n * @param {{send:Function}} o.net\n * @param {{now:()=>number}} [o.clock]             the coordinator's time, as seen here\n * @param {(issue:{key:string, problem:string}) => void} [o.onIssue]\n * @param {(id:number, progress:{score:number, label?:string}) => void} [o.onProgress]   a player's\n *        progress, this one's included, for the hub's scoreboard; it never reaches the games\n * @param {(on:boolean) => void} [o.onFullscreen]   the game asked for fullscreen (or out of it): the\n....\n/**\n * round.js — one round of an AI Hub session, run by the hub that coordinates\n * it (the harness page, or the session host's tab).\n *\n * It never touches the network or a frame itself: every message for a game\n * goes out through `deliver(key, msg)`, and everything the games report comes\n * in through `receive(key, msg)` and `leave(key, reason)`. The clock is\n * injected, so `scripts/hub.test.mjs` can walk a round through every timeout\n * without waiting for one.\n *\n * Players are known by `key` (whatever the session calls them); games see\n * contiguous ids 0..N-1 in slot order.\n */\n\nexport const TIMINGS = Object.freeze({\n  helloMs: 10_000,\n  readyMs: 45_000,\n  /** Gated rounds: a game still loading when the hub releases the round gets this long more. */\n  loadGraceMs: 20_000,\n  countdownMs: 3_000,\n  graceMs: 5_000,\n  hiddenMs: 20_000,\n  maxRoundMs: 15 * 60_000,\n});\n\n/** P1 red, P2 blue, P3 green, P4 yellow: the colour follows the seat. */\nexport const SLOT_COLORS = Object.freeze(['#e8443a', '#3b82f6', '#22c55e', '#f5b700']);\n\nexport const MODES = Object.freeze(['parallel', 'ghost', 'shared-world']);\n\n/** Each mode's name on screen. */\nexport const MODE_NAMES = Object.freeze({ parallel: 'Parallel', ghost: 'Ghost', 'shared-world': 'Shared world' });\n\n/** Older tags, still played: race as ghost, co-op and versus as a shared world. */\nconst MODE_ALIASES = Object.freeze({ race: 'ghost', 'co-op': 'shared-world', versus: 'shared-world' });\n\n/** The mode a tag or a hello names, older tags included; null for anything else. */\nexport function modeOf(word) {\n  const w = String(word ?? '').trim().toLowerCase();\n  return MODES.includes(w) ? w : (MODE_ALIASES[w] ?? null);\n}\n\n/** Modes where every copy runs its own world: each reports its own result, and nothing depends on a host. */\nexport const SELF_REPORTED = Object.freeze(['parallel', 'ghost']);\n\n/**\n * Whether a game's round carries the seed in its address, so the client seeds\n * Math.random and every copy starts the same: read from the game's own tag,\n * parallel or ghost. A game still tagged race was built for an unseeded start\n * (ids it makes from Math.random must differ between its copies), so it keeps one.\n....\n    <section><h2>Round</h2><div id=\"round\" class=\"hint\">No round yet.</div></section>\n    <section id=\"checks\"><h2>Conformance</h2><ul id=\"checklist\"><li class=\"hint\">Run with <code>?auto=1</code> for the automatic checks.</li></ul></section>\n    <pre id=\"log\" aria-live=\"off\"></pre>\n  </aside>\n</main>\n<script type=\"module\">\nimport { createBridge, attachFrame, postTo, createLocalNetwork } from './bridge.js';\nimport { createRound, limitFor, modeOf, seedsRandom, SELF_REPORTED, TIMINGS } from './round.js';\n\nconst NAMES = ['Red Fox',