{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W6ntjBEh3CURFxXstGScjh","type":"tool_result","content":"/**\n * @file The terrain drawn (PLAN.md WP 10.5; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §2.2): `createTerrainMeshes(terrain, { materials })`\n * draws a terrain (engine/world/terrain.ts) in square chunks of up to `CHUNK` cells a side, each a three.js `LOD` at\n * the chunk's middle holding a mesh per level of detail: every corner, every second, every fourth (`LOD_STEPS`, those\n * that divide the chunk), shown from `LOD_DISTANCES` metres away: three's renderer picks one for each camera, by its\n * distance from a perspective camera, and for an orthographic one (which stands far back: the isometric and top-down\n * views) by what it frames, as far as a 50° lens stands to frame as much (`levelDistance`). The finest mesh is the\n * collider's surface, each cell split as Rapier splits it; coarser ones skip corners, and every mesh hangs a skirt\n * `SKIRT` metres down round its edges, so where neighbours of different detail meet no gap shows the sky. A site's cut\n * (`cut`, WP 13.15) leaves its cells out, and a chunk holding one keeps only its finest level, so the hole is exact;\n * no skirt hangs where the cells on both sides of a chunk's edge are cut (a floor covers the line there, and the\n * skirt's top in its plane drew a grass hairline across it, WP 13.37), and the terrain's own border keeps its skirt.\n * Normals are the full grid's at every level (central differences of the heights), so the light holds still as the\n * levels switch; UVs are world metres (x, z).\n *\n * Materials: the terrain's ground layer from `materials` (a registry material, so the style set re-points it), drawn\n * through the splat (engine/gfx/terrainSplat.ts, the meshes' `userData.restyle`) with its steep and high layers.\n * Names: the root `terrain:<name>`, a chunk's meshes `terrain:<name>/<col>,<row>#<level>` (its north-west cell's\n * column and row, the level from 0, the finest), with `userData.terrain`; the ID pass and the inspector show them.\n *\n * Invariants: pure in the terrain (equal inputs give equal buffers); `drawCalls(shadowMaps)` counts one mesh a chunk\n * (one level shows at a time); `register(warmup)` registers every level of every chunk, so switching builds nothing;\n * `dispose()` frees the geometries and the splat's copies, never the library's materials. Nothing reaches the sim.\n *\n * @example\n * // const meshes = createTerrainMeshes(getTerrain('terrain:hill'), { materials: createMaterialLibrary() });\n * // scene.add(meshes.root);\n * // meshes.register(gfx.warmup);\n * // meshes.drawCalls(1); // 2 a chunk: one level in colour, and into the shadow map\n * @see engine/gfx/terrain.test.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/terrain.spec.ts\n */\nimport { Group, LOD, Mesh, Vector3, type BufferGeometry, type Camera, type Material, type OrthographicCamera, type PerspectiveCamera } from 'three/webgpu'; // prettier-ignore\nimport type { Terrain } from '../world/terrain';\nimport { createWriter, type Vec3 } from './geometry/writer';\nimport { createTerrainSplat, type TerrainSplat } from './terrainSplat';\nimport type { Warmup } from './warmup';\n\n/** A chunk's side, in cells, at most. */\nexport const CHUNK = 32;\n/** The levels of detail: the corners each takes, every one, every second, every fourth. */\nexport const LOD_STEPS = [1, 2, 4] as const;\n/** From how far (m) each level shows. */\nexport const LOD_DISTANCES = [0, 48, 96] as const;\n/** How far a chunk's skirt hangs below its edge (m). */\n....\n/**\n * @file Navigation over terrain (PLAN.md WP 10.5): `terrainNavGrid(terrain, options)` is a `NavGrid`\n * (engine/world/nav.ts) over a terrain's cells (engine/world/terrain.ts), from its north-west corner. A cell is open\n * when neither of its triangles rises more than `grade` metres a metre (1, 45°, by default: walls of rock close it),\n * its floor the ground's height at its centre; a step goes to a neighbour when the ground along the line between\n * their centres never rises more than `climb` metres from one of its 8 samples to the next, as on a level\n * (engine/world/level/walk.ts). A terrace's riser is a cell's slope here (heights stand at the corners), so the grade\n * closes a high one and the climb rarely binds.\n *\n * Invariants: derived data, a pure function of the terrain and the options; paths and flow fields answer in world\n * metres, the terrain's own.\n *\n * @example\n * import { makeTerrain } from './terrain';\n * const ground = makeTerrain({ size: [8, 8], origin: [10, 0, 0], hills: [{ at: [18, 4], radius: 4, height: 8 }] });\n * const grid = terrainNavGrid(ground);\n * [grid.cellAt(10.5, 0.5), grid.open[grid.cellAt(10.5, 0.5)], grid.open[grid.cellAt(16.5, 4.5)]]; // [0, 1, 0]: the flank too steep\n * @see engine/world/terrainNav.test.ts\n */\nimport { NavGrid, type NavGridOptions, type NavSurface } from './nav';\nimport { terrainHeight, type Terrain } from './terrain';\n\n/** How many samples a step's line takes: a level's (engine/world/level/walk.ts). */\nconst STEP_SAMPLES = 8;\n\n/** What `terrainNavGrid` takes: a grid's options, and the steepest ground it walks (m a metre; 1 by default). */\nexport interface TerrainNavOptions extends NavGridOptions {\n  grade?: number;\n}\n\n/** The walkable surface of `terrain`'s cells (see the file comment). */\nexport function terrainNavSurface(terrain: Terrain, grade = 1): NavSurface {\n  const { cols, rows, cell, origin, heights } = terrain;\n  const walkable: boolean[] = [];\n  const floor: number[] = [];\n  const corner = (c: number, r: number) => heights[r * (cols + 1) + c];\n  for (let row = 0; row < rows; row++) {\n    for (let col = 0; col < cols; col++) {\n      const [h00, h10, h01, h11] = [corner(col, row), corner(col + 1, row), corner(col, row + 1), corner(col + 1, row + 1)]; // prettier-ignore\n      // Each triangle's rise a metre (the split of engine/world/terrain.ts): north-west's, then south-east's.\n....\n/**\n * @file The terrain's splat (PLAN.md WP 10.5): `createTerrainSplat(terrain, layers)` gives `wrap(material)`, what a\n * terrain chunk (engine/gfx/terrain.ts) draws with: a node material copy of `material` (engine/gfx/materials/\n * nodeCopy.ts), the ground layer's material in whatever style the style set points it at (engine/gfx/materials/\n * styles.ts calls `wrap` through the chunks' `userData.restyle`), its colour mixing three layers at every fragment:\n * - the ground: the material's own colour and map;\n * - the steep, where the ground rises `terrain.steep` metres a metre or more (read off the fragment's world normal),\n *   blending in over ±`BAND` of that grade;\n * - the high, from `terrain.high` metres over the terrain's origin up (none at 0), blending in over a metre either way;\n * each layer its material's colour times its map at the map's repeat (the chunks' UVs are world metres), the steep\n * over the high over the ground, the edges between them wavering by fractal noise over the ground (tslNoise.ts) so no\n * contour line shows, and the whole varied ±8% at the scale of 24 m, as a level's floor is.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only; one copy per source material, made on first use; a copy's own uuid and its source's\n * `userData`, so the style set re-points it by its entry; `dispose` frees the copies (the sources stay the library's).\n * A material three.js cannot copy is drawn as it is: the ground alone.\n *\n * @example\n * import { MeshLambertMaterial } from 'three/webgpu';\n * const splat = createTerrainSplat({ steep: 0.7, high: 0, origin: [0, 0, 0] }, [new MeshLambertMaterial(), new MeshLambertMaterial()]);\n * const ground = new MeshLambertMaterial();\n * [splat.wrap(ground) === splat.wrap(ground), splat.owns(splat.wrap(ground)), splat.wrap(splat.wrap(ground)) === splat.wrap(ground)]; // [true, true, true]\n * splat.dispose();\n * @see engine/gfx/terrain.test.ts\n */\nimport { float, mix, normalWorld, positionWorld, smoothstep, texture, uv, vec2, vec3, vec4 } from 'three/tsl';\nimport type { Material, MeshLambertMaterial, Node, Texture } from 'three/webgpu';\nimport type { Terrain } from '../world/terrain';\nimport { nodeCopy } from './materials/nodeCopy';\nimport { fractalNode, variationNode } from './textures/tslNoise';\n....\n10- * from the next physics step on (engine/physics/queries.ts).\n11- *\n12: * `addTerrainBody(physics, terrain)` stands a terrain (engine/world/terrain.ts) as one fixed body in `world` too: one\n13- * heightfield collider over its grid (engine/physics/heightfield.ts), its surface exactly `terrainHeight`'s; given the\n14- * cells a site cuts (`cut`), one triangle mesh of the others instead (engine/physics/trimesh.ts: a heightfield has no\n15- * holes), split as the heightfield splits them. `addSiteBodies(physics, site)` stands a site (engine/world/site.ts):\n16- * its terrain, cut, then each structure's level bodies at its place and quarter turn (`addLevelBodies`'s `place`).\n17- *\n18- * Invariants: colliders enter in the level's own order (the slab, then the descriptors as `compileLevel` lists them),\n19- * so the same level gives the same Rapier state and hash; the level is never changed. Friction is the prototype's 0.6.\n20- *\n21- * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/10-hall.js:188-201` (`hallColliders`: one fixed body, the floor slab, the\n22- * merged boxes, the hulls of the stairs and the dais and the braziers' cylinders, friction 0.6), in metres and from\n23- * descriptors.\n24- *\n25- * @example\n26- * import { compileLevel } from '../world/level/compile';\n27- * import { createPhysics } from '../physics/world';\n28- * const physics = createPhysics();\n29- * const level = compileLevel('#####\\n#@.w#\\n#####\\n', { file: 'cell.txt' });\n30- * addLevelBodies(physics, level); // 0: one fixed body, the slab and 3 boxes\n31- * @see engine/sim/levelBodies.test.ts\n32- */\n33-import type { ColliderSpec, Physics } from '../physics/world';\n34-import type { ColliderDescriptor, CompiledLevel, Point3 } from '../world/level/types';\n--\n74- * returns the body's id.\n75- */\n76:export function addTerrainBody(physics: Physics, terrain: Terrain, cut?: Uint8Array | null): number {\n77-  const { cols, rows, cell, origin, heights } = terrain;\n78-  if (cut) return physics.addBody({ type: 'fixed', group: 'world', colliders: [{ shape: cutMesh(terrain, cut), friction: LEVEL_FRICTION }] }); // prettier-ignore\n79-  const size = [cols * cell, rows * cell] as const;\n80-  const shape = { type: 'heightfield', cols, rows, size, heights } as const;\n81-  const position = [origin[0] + size[0] / 2, 0, origin[2] + size[1] / 2] as const;\n82-  return physics.addBody({ type: 'fixed', group: 'world', colliders: [{ shape, position, friction: LEVEL_FRICTION }] });\n83-}\n84-\n85-/** The triangle mesh of `terrain`'s cells but the `cut` ones: every corner, two triangles a cell split as Rapier's heightfield. */\n86-function cutMesh(terrain: Terrain, cut: Uint8Array) {\n87-  const { cols, rows, cell, origin, heights } = terrain;\n88-  const vertices: number[] = [];\n89-  for (let r = 0; r <= rows; r++) {\n90-    for (let c = 0; c <= cols; c++)\n91-      vertices.push(origin[0] + c * cell, heights[r * (cols + 1) + c], origin[2] + r * cell);\n92-  }\n93-  const indices: number[] = [];\n94-  const at = (c: number, r: number) => r * (cols + 1) + c;\n95-  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n96-    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n97-      if (cut[r * cols + c]) continue;\n98-      // North-west, south-west, north-east; north-east, south-west, south-east: facing up.\n--\n105-/** Adds a site's bodies (its terrain, cut, then each structure's) and returns their ids in that order. */\n106-export function addSiteBodies(physics: Physics, site: Site): number[] {\n107:  const ids = site.terrain ? [addTerrainBody(physics, site.terrain, site.cut)] : [];\n108-  for (const s of site.structures) ids.push(addLevelBodies(physics, s.level, { at: s.at, turn: s.turn }));\n109-  return ids;\n110-}\n\n# Templates\n\n`node x new <kind> <id>` writes a working entry of a kind from its template here: the module and its unit test,\nformatted, then its checks (types, lint, its test, docs) and the step that comes next. It never overwrites a file.\n`node x new` lists the kinds with what each id looks like; `node x new <kind> --test` writes a kind's sample, checks\nit and removes it, and `node x new --test-all` tests every kind (the full run, `node x ci --local --full`, runs it),\nso no template rots. tools/cmd/new.ts's file comment is the manual (the manifest's keys, the placeholders, the\nchecks); the `add-entry` skill (.claude/skills/add-entry/SKILL.md) is the loop round it.\n\n## The kinds\n\nEach row is a directory here; tools/cmd/new.test.ts fails a kind this table leaves out, or a path its kind refuses.\n\"Seen with\" names a command that shows or checks an entry beyond its own test; `x new` prints the rest of what to do\nafter writing one (its manifest's `next`). A kind marked \"no\" in \"In a game\" makes no entry there and says what a\ngame does instead (its manifest's `games`).\n\n| Kind           | An entry is                                                    | Its path                               | Seen with                                         | In a game |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `action`       | a multi-beat sequence a body plays (`defineAction`)            | `labs/<lab>/actions/<name>`            | its test (`actionCheck` through `animCheckBody`)  | yes       |\n| `body`         | a puppet body on the humanoid's bones (`defineBody`)           | `labs/<lab>/bodies/<name>`             | `node x gallery body body:<name>`                 | yes       |\n| `build`        | the humanoid's proportions (`defineBuild`)                     | `labs/<lab>/builds/<name>`             | its test (`animCheck` of its rest pose)           | yes       |\n| `camera`       | a camera mode of a game's own, `cam3=<name>` (`defineCamera`)  | `labs/<lab>/cameras/<name>`            | `node x shot <page> --cam cam3=<name>`            | yes       |\n| `course`       | a playground feel course (`defineCourse`)                      | `labs/playground/courses/<name>`       | `node x feel labs/playground --course <name>`     | no        |\n| `emitter`      | a particle effect as data (`defineEmitter`)                    | `labs/<lab>/emitters/<name>`           | `node x gallery emitter emitter:<name>`           | yes       |\n| `feature`      | a rendering technique behind its feature and advice            | `engine/gfx/enhanced/<name>`           | `node x perf <lab> --feature feature:<name>`      | no        |\n| `geometry`     | a shape built from the kit's builders (`defineGeometry`)       | `labs/<lab>/geometry/<name>`           | `node x qa geo --only geometry:<name>`            | yes       |\n| `interactable` | what a player uses and strikes (`defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`) | `labs/<lab>/interactables/<name>`  | its test (a player's use and punch in a yard)     | yes       |\n| `level`        | a level written as storeys (`defineLevel`)                     | `labs/<lab>/levels/<name>`             | `node x eye <scene>` (a scene that plays it)      | yes       |\n| `look`         | a material style and a filter chain (`defineLook`)             | `labs/<lab>/looks/<name>`              | `node x gallery look look:<name>`                 | yes       |\n| `material`     | three.js's classic parameters as data (`defineMaterial`)       | `labs/<lab>/materials/<name>`          | `node x gallery material material:<name>`         | yes       |\n| `module`       | a TypeScript module and its unit test                          | `<engine\\|tools\\|labs\\|tests>/<dir>/<name>` | `npm test -- <path>`                         | yes       |\n| `move`         | a named strike in metres and seconds (`defineMove`)            | `labs/<lab>/moves/<name>`              | `node x gallery move move:<name>`                 | yes       |\n| `movement`     | how a character moves (`defineMovement`)                       | `labs/<lab>/movement/<name>`           | `node x sim <scene> --feel`                       | yes       |\n| `prop`         | a thing that stands in a level (`defineProp`)                  | `labs/<lab>/props/<name>`              | `node x gallery prop prop:<name>`, `node x qa geo tex --only prop:<name>` | yes |\n| `rig`          | a procedural creature of engine/anim/proc's blocks              | `labs/<lab>/rigs/<name>`               | its test (`rigCheck`, and a walk)                 | yes       |\n| `scene`        | a box scene and its replay test (`defineScene`)                | `labs/box/scenes/<name>`               | `node x sim`, `node x replay`, `node x shot labs/box --scene <name>` | no |\n| `sfx`          | a sound effect for the DSP core (`defineSfx`)                  | `labs/<lab>/sounds/<name>`             | `node x audio sfx:<name>`                         | yes       |\n| `song`         | a tune in tracker notation (`defineSong`)                      | `labs/<lab>/sounds/<name>`             | `node x audio song:<name>`                        | yes       |\n| `telegraph`    | an attack's warning shape on the floor (`defineTelegraph`)     | `labs/<lab>/telegraphs/<name>`         | its test (a hit inside, a near miss past it)      | yes       |\n| `texture`      | a seeded, periodic texture generator (`defineTexture`)         | `labs/<lab>/textures/<name>`           | `node x qa tex --only texture:<name>`             | yes       |\n| `thing`        | what can be hit, as data (`defineThing`)                       | `labs/<lab>/things/<name>`             | its test (struck down, standing again)            | yes       |\n| `weapon`       | a firearm and the prop it is drawn as (`defineWeapon`)         | `labs/<lab>/weapons/<name>`            | `node x gallery prop prop:<name>`                 | yes       |\n\n`<lab>` is the lab that shows it (`box`, `playground`…; `game` in a game). The gallery (`node x gallery`, PLAN.md WP\n7.7) films what its page registers: its page (tests/pages/gallery.ts) imports every lab's `scenes/`, `looks/` and\n`bodies/`, so an emitter, a prop or a weapon is filmed once a scene imports its module (`--page` films a lab's own\npage instead).\n\n## Adding a kind\n\nREADME.md\naction\nbody\nbuild\ncamera\ncourse\nemitter\nfeature\ngeometry\ninteractable\nlevel\nlook\nmaterial\nmodule\nmove\nmovement\nprop\nrig\nscene\nsfx\nsong\ntelegraph\ntexture\nthing\nweapon","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"fcc94e5f-3482-4153-92c9-9a4f33acd3bf","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:29:52.314Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/**\n * @file The terrain drawn (PLAN.md WP 10.5; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §2.2): `createTerrainMeshes(terrain, { materials })`\n * draws a terrain (engine/world/terrain.ts) in square chunks of up to `CHUNK` cells a side, each a three.js `LOD` at\n * the chunk's middle holding a mesh per level of detail: every corner, every second, every fourth (`LOD_STEPS`, those\n * that divide the chunk), shown from `LOD_DISTANCES` metres away: three's renderer picks one for each camera, by its\n * distance from a perspective camera, and for an orthographic one (which stands far back: the isometric and top-down\n * views) by what it frames, as far as a 50° lens stands to frame as much (`levelDistance`). The finest mesh is the\n * collider's surface, each cell split as Rapier splits it; coarser ones skip corners, and every mesh hangs a skirt\n * `SKIRT` metres down round its edges, so where neighbours of different detail meet no gap shows the sky. A site's cut\n * (`cut`, WP 13.15) leaves its cells out, and a chunk holding one keeps only its finest level, so the hole is exact;\n * no skirt hangs where the cells on both sides of a chunk's edge are cut (a floor covers the line there, and the\n * skirt's top in its plane drew a grass hairline across it, WP 13.37), and the terrain's own border keeps its skirt.\n * Normals are the full grid's at every level (central differences of the heights), so the light holds still as the\n * levels switch; UVs are world metres (x, z).\n *\n * Materials: the terrain's ground layer from `materials` (a registry material, so the style set re-points it), drawn\n * through the splat (engine/gfx/terrainSplat.ts, the meshes' `userData.restyle`) with its steep and high layers.\n * Names: the root `terrain:<name>`, a chunk's meshes `terrain:<name>/<col>,<row>#<level>` (its north-west cell's\n * column and row, the level from 0, the finest), with `userData.terrain`; the ID pass and the inspector show them.\n *\n * Invariants: pure in the terrain (equal inputs give equal buffers); `drawCalls(shadowMaps)` counts one mesh a chunk\n * (one level shows at a time); `register(warmup)` registers every level of every chunk, so switching builds nothing;\n * `dispose()` frees the geometries and the splat's copies, never the library's materials. Nothing reaches the sim.\n *\n * @example\n * // const meshes = createTerrainMeshes(getTerrain('terrain:hill'), { materials: createMaterialLibrary() });\n * // scene.add(meshes.root);\n * // meshes.register(gfx.warmup);\n * // meshes.drawCalls(1); // 2 a chunk: one level in colour, and into the shadow map\n * @see engine/gfx/terrain.test.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/terrain.spec.ts\n */\nimport { Group, LOD, Mesh, Vector3, type BufferGeometry, type Camera, type Material, type OrthographicCamera, type PerspectiveCamera } from 'three/webgpu'; // prettier-ignore\nimport type { Terrain } from '../world/terrain';\nimport { createWriter, type Vec3 } from './geometry/writer';\nimport { createTerrainSplat, type TerrainSplat } from './terrainSplat';\nimport type { Warmup } from './warmup';\n\n/** A chunk's side, in cells, at most. */\nexport const CHUNK = 32;\n/** The levels of detail: the corners each takes, every one, every second, every fourth. */\nexport const LOD_STEPS = [1, 2, 4] as const;\n/** From how far (m) each level shows. */\nexport const LOD_DISTANCES = [0, 48, 96] as const;\n/** How far a chunk's skirt hangs below its edge (m). */\n....\n/**\n * @file Navigation over terrain (PLAN.md WP 10.5): `terrainNavGrid(terrain, options)` is a `NavGrid`\n * (engine/world/nav.ts) over a terrain's cells (engine/world/terrain.ts), from its north-west corner. A cell is open\n * when neither of its triangles rises more than `grade` metres a metre (1, 45°, by default: walls of rock close it),\n * its floor the ground's height at its centre; a step goes to a neighbour when the ground along the line between\n * their centres never rises more than `climb` metres from one of its 8 samples to the next, as on a level\n * (engine/world/level/walk.ts). A terrace's riser is a cell's slope here (heights stand at the corners), so the grade\n * closes a high one and the climb rarely binds.\n *\n * Invariants: derived data, a pure function of the terrain and the options; paths and flow fields answer in world\n * metres, the terrain's own.\n *\n * @example\n * import { makeTerrain } from './terrain';\n * const ground = makeTerrain({ size: [8, 8], origin: [10, 0, 0], hills: [{ at: [18, 4], radius: 4, height: 8 }] });\n * const grid = terrainNavGrid(ground);\n * [grid.cellAt(10.5, 0.5), grid.open[grid.cellAt(10.5, 0.5)], grid.open[grid.cellAt(16.5, 4.5)]]; // [0, 1, 0]: the flank too steep\n * @see engine/world/terrainNav.test.ts\n */\nimport { NavGrid, type NavGridOptions, type NavSurface } from './nav';\nimport { terrainHeight, type Terrain } from './terrain';\n\n/** How many samples a step's line takes: a level's (engine/world/level/walk.ts). */\nconst STEP_SAMPLES = 8;\n\n/** What `terrainNavGrid` takes: a grid's options, and the steepest ground it walks (m a metre; 1 by default). */\nexport interface TerrainNavOptions extends NavGridOptions {\n  grade?: number;\n}\n\n/** The walkable surface of `terrain`'s cells (see the file comment). */\nexport function terrainNavSurface(terrain: Terrain, grade = 1): NavSurface {\n  const { cols, rows, cell, origin, heights } = terrain;\n  const walkable: boolean[] = [];\n  const floor: number[] = [];\n  const corner = (c: number, r: number) => heights[r * (cols + 1) + c];\n  for (let row = 0; row < rows; row++) {\n    for (let col = 0; col < cols; col++) {\n      const [h00, h10, h01, h11] = [corner(col, row), corner(col + 1, row), corner(col, row + 1), corner(col + 1, row + 1)]; // prettier-ignore\n      // Each triangle's rise a metre (the split of engine/world/terrain.ts): north-west's, then south-east's.\n....\n/**\n * @file The terrain's splat (PLAN.md WP 10.5): `createTerrainSplat(terrain, layers)` gives `wrap(material)`, what a\n * terrain chunk (engine/gfx/terrain.ts) draws with: a node material copy of `material` (engine/gfx/materials/\n * nodeCopy.ts), the ground layer's material in whatever style the style set points it at (engine/gfx/materials/\n * styles.ts calls `wrap` through the chunks' `userData.restyle`), its colour mixing three layers at every fragment:\n * - the ground: the material's own colour and map;\n * - the steep, where the ground rises `terrain.steep` metres a metre or more (read off the fragment's world normal),\n *   blending in over ±`BAND` of that grade;\n * - the high, from `terrain.high` metres over the terrain's origin up (none at 0), blending in over a metre either way;\n * each layer its material's colour times its map at the map's repeat (the chunks' UVs are world metres), the steep\n * over the high over the ground, the edges between them wavering by fractal noise over the ground (tslNoise.ts) so no\n * contour line shows, and the whole varied ±8% at the scale of 24 m, as a level's floor is.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only; one copy per source material, made on first use; a copy's own uuid and its source's\n * `userData`, so the style set re-points it by its entry; `dispose` frees the copies (the sources stay the library's).\n * A material three.js cannot copy is drawn as it is: the ground alone.\n *\n * @example\n * import { MeshLambertMaterial } from 'three/webgpu';\n * const splat = createTerrainSplat({ steep: 0.7, high: 0, origin: [0, 0, 0] }, [new MeshLambertMaterial(), new MeshLambertMaterial()]);\n * const ground = new MeshLambertMaterial();\n * [splat.wrap(ground) === splat.wrap(ground), splat.owns(splat.wrap(ground)), splat.wrap(splat.wrap(ground)) === splat.wrap(ground)]; // [true, true, true]\n * splat.dispose();\n * @see engine/gfx/terrain.test.ts\n */\nimport { float, mix, normalWorld, positionWorld, smoothstep, texture, uv, vec2, vec3, vec4 } from 'three/tsl';\nimport type { Material, MeshLambertMaterial, Node, Texture } from 'three/webgpu';\nimport type { Terrain } from '../world/terrain';\nimport { nodeCopy } from './materials/nodeCopy';\nimport { fractalNode, variationNode } from './textures/tslNoise';\n....\n10- * from the next physics step on (engine/physics/queries.ts).\n11- *\n12: * `addTerrainBody(physics, terrain)` stands a terrain (engine/world/terrain.ts) as one fixed body in `world` too: one\n13- * heightfield collider over its grid (engine/physics/heightfield.ts), its surface exactly `terrainHeight`'s; given the\n14- * cells a site cuts (`cut`), one triangle mesh of the others instead (engine/physics/trimesh.ts: a heightfield has no\n15- * holes), split as the heightfield splits them. `addSiteBodies(physics, site)` stands a site (engine/world/site.ts):\n16- * its terrain, cut, then each structure's level bodies at its place and quarter turn (`addLevelBodies`'s `place`).\n17- *\n18- * Invariants: colliders enter in the level's own order (the slab, then the descriptors as `compileLevel` lists them),\n19- * so the same level gives the same Rapier state and hash; the level is never changed. Friction is the prototype's 0.6.\n20- *\n21- * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/10-hall.js:188-201` (`hallColliders`: one fixed body, the floor slab, the\n22- * merged boxes, the hulls of the stairs and the dais and the braziers' cylinders, friction 0.6), in metres and from\n23- * descriptors.\n24- *\n25- * @example\n26- * import { compileLevel } from '../world/level/compile';\n27- * import { createPhysics } from '../physics/world';\n28- * const physics = createPhysics();\n29- * const level = compileLevel('#####\\n#@.w#\\n#####\\n', { file: 'cell.txt' });\n30- * addLevelBodies(physics, level); // 0: one fixed body, the slab and 3 boxes\n31- * @see engine/sim/levelBodies.test.ts\n32- */\n33-import type { ColliderSpec, Physics } from '../physics/world';\n34-import type { ColliderDescriptor, CompiledLevel, Point3 } from '../world/level/types';\n--\n74- * returns the body's id.\n75- */\n76:export function addTerrainBody(physics: Physics, terrain: Terrain, cut?: Uint8Array | null): number {\n77-  const { cols, rows, cell, origin, heights } = terrain;\n78-  if (cut) return physics.addBody({ type: 'fixed', group: 'world', colliders: [{ shape: cutMesh(terrain, cut), friction: LEVEL_FRICTION }] }); // prettier-ignore\n79-  const size = [cols * cell, rows * cell] as const;\n80-  const shape = { type: 'heightfield', cols, rows, size, heights } as const;\n81-  const position = [origin[0] + size[0] / 2, 0, origin[2] + size[1] / 2] as const;\n82-  return physics.addBody({ type: 'fixed', group: 'world', colliders: [{ shape, position, friction: LEVEL_FRICTION }] });\n83-}\n84-\n85-/** The triangle mesh of `terrain`'s cells but the `cut` ones: every corner, two triangles a cell split as Rapier's heightfield. */\n86-function cutMesh(terrain: Terrain, cut: Uint8Array) {\n87-  const { cols, rows, cell, origin, heights } = terrain;\n88-  const vertices: number[] = [];\n89-  for (let r = 0; r <= rows; r++) {\n90-    for (let c = 0; c <= cols; c++)\n91-      vertices.push(origin[0] + c * cell, heights[r * (cols + 1) + c], origin[2] + r * cell);\n92-  }\n93-  const indices: number[] = [];\n94-  const at = (c: number, r: number) => r * (cols + 1) + c;\n95-  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n96-    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n97-      if (cut[r * cols + c]) continue;\n98-      // North-west, south-west, north-east; north-east, south-west, south-east: facing up.\n--\n105-/** Adds a site's bodies (its terrain, cut, then each structure's) and returns their ids in that order. */\n106-export function addSiteBodies(physics: Physics, site: Site): number[] {\n107:  const ids = site.terrain ? [addTerrainBody(physics, site.terrain, site.cut)] : [];\n108-  for (const s of site.structures) ids.push(addLevelBodies(physics, s.level, { at: s.at, turn: s.turn }));\n109-  return ids;\n110-}\n\n# Templates\n\n`node x new <kind> <id>` writes a working entry of a kind from its template here: the module and its unit test,\nformatted, then its checks (types, lint, its test, docs) and the step that comes next. It never overwrites a file.\n`node x new` lists the kinds with what each id looks like; `node x new <kind> --test` writes a kind's sample, checks\nit and removes it, and `node x new --test-all` tests every kind (the full run, `node x ci --local --full`, runs it),\nso no template rots. tools/cmd/new.ts's file comment is the manual (the manifest's keys, the placeholders, the\nchecks); the `add-entry` skill (.claude/skills/add-entry/SKILL.md) is the loop round it.\n\n## The kinds\n\nEach row is a directory here; tools/cmd/new.test.ts fails a kind this table leaves out, or a path its kind refuses.\n\"Seen with\" names a command that shows or checks an entry beyond its own test; `x new` prints the rest of what to do\nafter writing one (its manifest's `next`). A kind marked \"no\" in \"In a game\" makes no entry there and says what a\ngame does instead (its manifest's `games`).\n\n| Kind           | An entry is                                                    | Its path                               | Seen with                                         | In a game |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `action`       | a multi-beat sequence a body plays (`defineAction`)            | `labs/<lab>/actions/<name>`            | its test (`actionCheck` through `animCheckBody`)  | yes       |\n| `body`         | a puppet body on the humanoid's bones (`defineBody`)           | `labs/<lab>/bodies/<name>`             | `node x gallery body body:<name>`                 | yes       |\n| `build`        | the humanoid's proportions (`defineBuild`)                     | `labs/<lab>/builds/<name>`             | its test (`animCheck` of its rest pose)           | yes       |\n| `camera`       | a camera mode of a game's own, `cam3=<name>` (`defineCamera`)  | `labs/<lab>/cameras/<name>`            | `node x shot <page> --cam cam3=<name>`            | yes       |\n| `course`       | a playground feel course (`defineCourse`)                      | `labs/playground/courses/<name>`       | `node x feel labs/playground --course <name>`     | no        |\n| `emitter`      | a particle effect as data (`defineEmitter`)                    | `labs/<lab>/emitters/<name>`           | `node x gallery emitter emitter:<name>`           | yes       |\n| `feature`      | a rendering technique behind its feature and advice            | `engine/gfx/enhanced/<name>`           | `node x perf <lab> --feature feature:<name>`      | no        |\n| `geometry`     | a shape built from the kit's builders (`defineGeometry`)       | `labs/<lab>/geometry/<name>`           | `node x qa geo --only geometry:<name>`            | yes       |\n| `interactable` | what a player uses and strikes (`defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`) | `labs/<lab>/interactables/<name>`  | its test (a player's use and punch in a yard)     | yes       |\n| `level`        | a level written as storeys (`defineLevel`)                     | `labs/<lab>/levels/<name>`             | `node x eye <scene>` (a scene that plays it)      | yes       |\n| `look`         | a material style and a filter chain (`defineLook`)             | `labs/<lab>/looks/<name>`              | `node x gallery look look:<name>`                 | yes       |\n| `material`     | three.js's classic parameters as data (`defineMaterial`)       | `labs/<lab>/materials/<name>`          | `node x gallery material material:<name>`         | yes       |\n| `module`       | a TypeScript module and its unit test                          | `<engine\\|tools\\|labs\\|tests>/<dir>/<name>` | `npm test -- <path>`                         | yes       |\n| `move`         | a named strike in metres and seconds (`defineMove`)            | `labs/<lab>/moves/<name>`              | `node x gallery move move:<name>`                 | yes       |\n| `movement`     | how a character moves (`defineMovement`)                       | `labs/<lab>/movement/<name>`           | `node x sim <scene> --feel`                       | yes       |\n| `prop`         | a thing that stands in a level (`defineProp`)                  | `labs/<lab>/props/<name>`              | `node x gallery prop prop:<name>`, `node x qa geo tex --only prop:<name>` | yes |\n| `rig`          | a procedural creature of engine/anim/proc's blocks              | `labs/<lab>/rigs/<name>`               | its test (`rigCheck`, and a walk)                 | yes       |\n| `scene`        | a box scene and its replay test (`defineScene`)                | `labs/box/scenes/<name>`               | `node x sim`, `node x replay`, `node x shot labs/box --scene <name>` | no |\n| `sfx`          | a sound effect for the DSP core (`defineSfx`)                  | `labs/<lab>/sounds/<name>`             | `node x audio sfx:<name>`                         | yes       |\n| `song`         | a tune in tracker notation (`defineSong`)                      | `labs/<lab>/sounds/<name>`             | `node x audio song:<name>`                        | yes       |\n| `telegraph`    | an attack's warning shape on the floor (`defineTelegraph`)     | `labs/<lab>/telegraphs/<name>`         | its test (a hit inside, a near miss past it)      | yes       |\n| `texture`      | a seeded, periodic texture generator (`defineTexture`)         | `labs/<lab>/textures/<name>`           | `node x qa tex --only texture:<name>`             | yes       |\n| `thing`        | what can be hit, as data (`defineThing`)                       | `labs/<lab>/things/<name>`             | its test (struck down, standing again)            | yes       |\n| `weapon`       | a firearm and the prop it is drawn as (`defineWeapon`)         | `labs/<lab>/weapons/<name>`            | `node x gallery prop prop:<name>`                 | yes       |\n\n`<lab>` is the lab that shows it (`box`, `playground`…; `game` in a game). The gallery (`node x gallery`, PLAN.md WP\n7.7) films what its page registers: its page (tests/pages/gallery.ts) imports every lab's `scenes/`, `looks/` and\n`bodies/`, so an emitter, a prop or a weapon is filmed once a scene imports its module (`--page` films a lab's own\npage instead).\n\n## Adding a kind\n\nREADME.md\naction\nbody\nbuild\ncamera\ncourse\nemitter\nfeature\ngeometry\ninteractable\nlevel\nlook\nmaterial\nmodule\nmove\nmovement\nprop\nrig\nscene\nsfx\nsong\ntelegraph\ntexture\nthing\nweapon","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01W6ntjBEh3CURFxXstGScjh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
