{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjEZQ9CczZzZYmX2ReYj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017oToZTCoWHXXvhD11xzmrJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf8').read()\na='''- **Headless shell only.** Playwright's default headless works. Full Chromium dies here with \"Socket path too long\"\n  because run paths are long, so skip headed mode and xvfb.'''\nb='''- **Headless shell only.** Playwright's default headless works. Full Chromium dies here with \"Socket path too long\"\n  because run paths are long, so skip headed mode and xvfb. Never run `playwright install`; the browsers are already there.'''\nassert a in s; s=s.replace(a,b,1)\na=\"6. `AIHub.server`: no engine. `createHeadless({ scene, seed, settings:\"\nb=\"6. `AIHub.server`: no engine. `await createHeadless({ scene, seed, settings:\"\nassert a in s; s=s.replace(a,b,1)\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf8').write(s)\n\nanswers=[\n {\"question\":\"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n  \"answer\":\"It gives checkable targets instead of adjectives. The hub harness has a pass flag, testing has one required shape (one command, failures and a count), and the 'This computer' section names real tools and what is missing, with a stand-in for each. That let me test the plan's riskiest ideas on this machine before writing them down. The freedom on genre and camera, paired with a hard definition of done (no stubs, dev mode, attract mode, About screen), also gave me clear room to make design calls.\"},\n {\"question\":\"What was confusing?\",\n  \"answer\":\"Three things. First, 'multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub README says outside a session the game is not affected, so this could mean 'still runs solo' or 'has multiplayer without the hub'. I planned for both, adding tab and invite-code links, and I'm not sure the second was wanted. Second, TASK.md lists WebGPU flags that fail with this engine ('Instance dropped in popErrorScope', never ready); the engine's own flags work, and it took a probe to find that. Third, 'treat the performance budget as unlimited' sits oddly beside the harness's 20-second ready deadline on software WebGPU with four frames, which is a real load-time limit the prompt doesn't mention. I also wasn't sure whether 'leave out TASK.md's file names' covered the fixed exit-interview name, so I left that out too.\"},\n {\"question\":\"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n  \"answer\":\"Finding where a huge systemic design meets walls that the engine's docs don't flag: game code has no access to the three.js scene, first-person arms can't be posed, and weapons, melee and use only serve the actor marked as player. The last one decided the multiplayer architecture, so I had to find a design that needed no per-entity replication and then prove it. Host-sequenced lockstep passed the harness only after I fixed the ready timing, turn batching and warm-up setting. After that, the hard part was keeping the design original while still capturing what makes the source distinctive, and writing a plan that a builder of any capability can follow without me. My own numbers needed checking too: my first fire spread values were about five times too fast until I simulated them.\"},\n {\"question\":\"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n  \"answer\":\"Correct the WebGPU flags to the ones the engine uses (--use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader). In 'This computer', say that the engine's browser commands need CHROMIUM_PATH pointed at Playwright's headless shell, and that scripts/setup.sh would download Node and Chrome outside the folder. Say plainly what 'on its own' means for multiplayer. Add the hub's load deadlines beside the unlimited-performance line. Say whether a planner may run throwaway probes and where scratch files belong. Mention that labs/playground, the engine's best worked example, isn't copied into a game's workspace. I'd also give a rough target for demo length, since 'long enough to show all features' is the main driver of scope.\"},\n]\njson.dump(answers,open('planner-exit-interview.json','w',encoding='utf8'),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)\nd=json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json',encoding='utf8'))\nassert [x['question'] for x in d]==[\"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\"What was confusing?\",\"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\"What would you change in this prompt?\"]\nprint('planner-exit-interview.json parses,',len(d),'answers')\nEOF\nps -eo pid,cmd | grep -E \"\\.research|probe-out\" | grep -v grep | cut -c1-120 | head -5; rm -rf .research; ls -la; echo; grep -n -i -E \"jackal|APR\\b|UFLL|bowa|leboa|pala\\b|mosate|reuben|greaves|GameTitle|claude-|opus|haiku|sonnet|gpt-\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-120; echo \"forbidden-name scan done\"; wc -l -w PLAN.md; git -C . status --short . 2>/dev/null | head -5 | cut -c1-120","description":"Finish the plan, write the exit interview, and remove scratch files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3638,"cache_read_input_tokens":622339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3638},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"a11fb8f6-f3a0-48e5-9cf8-1995f3cbcc43","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:33:20.290Z","request_id":"req_011CgjEZPpMe1HT5MVpQsiDB","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017oToZTCoWHXXvhD11xzmrJ":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf8').read()\na='''- **Headless shell only.** Playwright's default headless works. Full Chromium dies here with \"Socket path too long\"\n  because run paths are long, so skip headed mode and xvfb.'''\nb='''- **Headless shell only.** Playwright's default headless works. Full Chromium dies here with \"Socket path too long\"\n  because run paths are long, so skip headed mode and xvfb. Never run `playwright install`; the browsers are already there.'''\nassert a in s; s=s.replace(a,b,1)\na=\"6. `AIHub.server`: no engine. `createHeadless({ scene, seed, settings:\"\nb=\"6. `AIHub.server`: no engine. `await createHeadless({ scene, seed, settings:\"\nassert a in s; s=s.replace(a,b,1)\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf8').write(s)\n\nanswers=[\n {\"question\":\"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n  \"answer\":\"It gives checkable targets instead of adjectives. The hub harness has a pass flag, testing has one required shape (one command, failures and a count), and the 'This computer' section names real tools and what is missing, with a stand-in for each. That let me test the plan's riskiest ideas on this machine before writing them down. The freedom on genre and camera, paired with a hard definition of done (no stubs, dev mode, attract mode, About screen), also gave me clear room to make design calls.\"},\n {\"question\":\"What was confusing?\",\n  \"answer\":\"Three things. First, 'multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub README says outside a session the game is not affected, so this could mean 'still runs solo' or 'has multiplayer without the hub'. I planned for both, adding tab and invite-code links, and I'm not sure the second was wanted. Second, TASK.md lists WebGPU flags that fail with this engine ('Instance dropped in popErrorScope', never ready); the engine's own flags work, and it took a probe to find that. Third, 'treat the performance budget as unlimited' sits oddly beside the harness's 20-second ready deadline on software WebGPU with four frames, which is a real load-time limit the prompt doesn't mention. I also wasn't sure whether 'leave out TASK.md's file names' covered the fixed exit-interview name, so I left that out too.\"},\n {\"question\":\"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n  \"answer\":\"Finding where a huge systemic design meets walls that the engine's docs don't flag: game code has no access to the three.js scene, first-person arms can't be posed, and weapons, melee and use only serve the actor marked as player. The last one decided the multiplayer architecture, so I had to find a design that needed no per-entity replication and then prove it. Host-sequenced lockstep passed the harness only after I fixed the ready timing, turn batching and warm-up setting. After that, the hard part was keeping the design original while still capturing what makes the source distinctive, and writing a plan that a builder of any capability can follow without me. My own numbers needed checking too: my first fire spread values were about five times too fast until I simulated them.\"},\n {\"question\":\"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n  \"answer\":\"Correct the WebGPU flags to the ones the engine uses (--use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader). In 'This computer', say that the engine's browser commands need CHROMIUM_PATH pointed at Playwright's headless shell, and that scripts/setup.sh would download Node and Chrome outside the folder. Say plainly what 'on its own' means for multiplayer. Add the hub's load deadlines beside the unlimited-performance line. Say whether a planner may run throwaway probes and where scratch files belong. Mention that labs/playground, the engine's best worked example, isn't copied into a game's workspace. I'd also give a rough target for demo length, since 'long enough to show all features' is the main driver of scope.\"},\n]\njson.dump(answers,open('planner-exit-interview.json','w',encoding='utf8'),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)\nd=json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json',encoding='utf8'))\nassert [x['question'] for x in d]==[\"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\"What was confusing?\",\"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\"What would you change in this prompt?\"]\nprint('planner-exit-interview.json parses,',len(d),'answers')\nEOF\nps -eo pid,cmd | grep -E \"\\.research|probe-out\" | grep -v grep | cut -c1-120 | head -5; rm -rf .research; ls -la; echo; grep -n -i -E \"jackal|APR\\b|UFLL|bowa|leboa|pala\\b|mosate|reuben|greaves|GameTitle|claude-|opus|haiku|sonnet|gpt-\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-120; echo \"forbidden-name scan done\"; wc -l -w PLAN.md; git -C . status --short . 2>/dev/null | head -5 | cut -c1-120","description":"Finish the plan, write the exit interview, and remove scratch files"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_017oToZTCoWHXXvhD11xzmrJ":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work"}}}
