{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjG7QdDrGwUwPT9XShwx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The boat floats stably and reaches 16 m/s without physics blow-ups, so that's the last check passed. I'm now updating PLAN.md with the brief's requirements and the probe corrections, including the reload method and the attract camera.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6468,"cache_read_input_tokens":706396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6468},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"bdb6f76f-04da-4e49-b993-bb666ae4ab42","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:53:56.454Z","request_id":"req_011CgjG7QERKwf4R8RnhRk29","narration_block_indexes":[0],"thinking_duration_ms":1,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjG7QdDrGwUwPT9XShwx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qw47P1cF8QaGwTfidewK7x","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf8').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, 'MISSING: '+a[:90]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# ---- Standing rules: the brief, restated in the plan.\nrep('''## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.**''','''## 0.1 Standing rules from the brief\n\nThese restate TASK.md so this plan stands on its own.\n\n- **Goal.** An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters,\n  themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX. It must be long enough to show **all** features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and\n  feels and what they can and cannot do (§8.4, field notes in §10.3, the coverage test in §13), with all the infrastructure for building out the rest with\n  normal designer tools (§4, §6.10).\n- **Title.** A playful riff on the source's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles (don't show the list), then pick one with a real random number generator by\n  running code; don't choose by taste. It goes in `title.ts`, on the title screen, in `<title>`, in the catalogue's `name`, and in the file and folder names your TASK.md's pattern asks for.\n- **Research.** How much is your choice. The engine and the hub are already researched here. Report whatever you did in Source Information (§15.3).\n- **Package.** Develop in separate modules in the dev folder, then bundle into one HTML file in the folder above it, named as your TASK.md says. External CDN\n  dependencies are allowed, but this plan's recipe inlines everything (§15.1).\n- **Tech.** my-3dge in its full workspace. The engine stays read-only; add whatever systems, art and animation the game needs on top. The rest of the stack is\n  your choice; this plan needs nothing beyond what the workspace ships (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright).\n- **Art, animation, audio.** The engine's native style and animation system as the base, with new assets and animations for whatever makes it feel like the source; the engine's asset\n  library is fine for generic props (§9.1, §9.2). All music, effects and voices are written in code with no audio files, voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish, and music defaults to **10%** (§9.3).\n- **Online and multiplayer.** Follow the [AI Hub session protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub) both for integration into the hub and for the game on its own (§12).\n- **Working environment.** Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` or a parent folder (the deliverable and the exit-interview file are the exceptions TASK.md sets). Don't read\n  files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside yours and share this machine's ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a\n  server (the harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. **Stop only processes you started, by PID: never `pkill`,\n  `killall`, `fuser -k`, or killing whatever holds a port.** Keep one or two browsers open at once and close them. Never run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`. The system Python has\n  Playwright and nothing else; for more, `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser\n  has been closed\" means memory ran out (earlyoom), not a bug in your game: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\n- **Performance.** Treat the budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as the scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Counts in this plan are\n  starting values for the design, not caps to protect a frame rate; raise them when a scene wants more.\n- **Testing.** Headless scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only\n  after changing the code it covers. Screenshots only for what a script can't check. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (§13).\n- **Effort.** Spend it however you see fit.\n- **Done.** No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.**''')\n\n# ---- Verified facts added to the engine table and walls.\nrep('''| Body animation | `defineAction` + `actOn` (call it after step 1, not in `setup`: verified), library clips (`node x anim find crawl`) | Credit imported clips per `data/anim/SOURCES.md` |''',\n'''| Body animation | `defineAction` + `actOn` (not in `setup`; see the spawn rule below), library clips (`node x anim find crawl`) | Credit imported clips per `data/anim/SOURCES.md` |''')\nrep('''| Data as a tool | `defineKind`/`def` for contracts, places, squads, dealer stock | Schemas check entries; `__engine.describe('contract')` lists them for designers |''',\n'''| Data as a tool | `defineKind`/`def` for contracts, places, squads, dealer stock (verified: a bad entry throws) | Schemas check entries; `__engine.describe('contract')` lists them for designers |''')\nrep('''3. **First-person arms are a closed rig.** Games can't pose them, and reloads aren't animated at all. Fakes: lower the gun\n   for a reload by setting `weapons.slot = -1` for the middle of the reload with `motion.holstered` kept false (film it to\n   check); kick by `shots`; big moments use the body camera (§10.2). Full-body actions always show in shoulder view and\n   to other players.''','''3. **First-person arms are a closed rig.** Games can't pose them, and reloads aren't animated at all. **What works (verified): a\n   flipbook.** Swap `weapons.slots[slot]` through weapon ids whose props hold the same gun tilted and lowered (`rotation` and `position` on the parts, the magazine\n   left out where it should be out). The first-person gun follows frame by frame and no pipeline is built, so reloads and jam clearing get real motion in the hands.\n   Setting `weapons.slot = -1` with `animator.weapon = 'none'` is a real holster: gun gone, raised fists; it's no reload. Kick comes from `shots`; big moments use the body\n   camera (§10.2). Full-body actions always show in shoulder view and to other players.''')\nrep('''Texture generators write `texel.color` in **sRGB bytes 0–255**, not 0–1 (verified; 0–1 draws black).\n''','''More verified traps:\n\n- Texture generators write `texel.color` in **sRGB bytes 0–255**, not 0–1 (0–1 draws black).\n- **An entity spawned in a step joins the world at that step's end.** `w.add`, `w.get` and `actOn` fail on it until then, so give components at spawn: `w.spawn({ … })`,\n  `spawnProp(w, placement, { components: { … } })`. An animated actor can take `actOn` from the next step on.\n- Dev actions are plain functions: `defineScene(id, { actions: { ignite: (w, args) => … } })`, run with `__engine.actions.run('ignite', args)`.\n- **Pre-warm while the loading screen is up.** The first holster and draw cycles built 32 pipelines and the first flame prop 2 more, stalling frames (one black frame on software WebGPU). After that,\n  holsters, flames, weapon-id swaps and fever filters built nothing. So in the scene's first moments, under the loading screen, cycle a holster twice and spawn one of each runtime prop out of sight.\n''')\n\n# ---- Guns: flipbook.\nrep('''- **Slots:** Sidearm, Primary, Special, plus machete (always), syrettes and throwables. Ammo per class, modest.''','''- **Reload and clear in the hands:** the flipbook of wall 3, 3–4 poses a gun, stepped by `arms` over the reload's time, with its sounds. Generate the ids with a helper (`gunVariants(gun)`: rust\n  tiers × poses) rather than writing them out.\n- **Slots:** Sidearm, Primary, Special, plus machete (always), syrettes and throwables. Ammo per class, modest.''')\n\n# ---- Fire: verified in the engine, and the tint.\nrep('''60 m all round. 10 m/s wind: about 150 m. So it outruns a crouch (2.0 m/s) and never a sprint (6.4 m/s).''','''60 m all round. 10 m/s wind: about 150 m. So it outruns a crouch (2.0 m/s) and never a sprint (6.4 m/s). **Verified in the engine** as one component of plain number arrays with grass patches burning\ninto scorch props: over 6 seeds, 109 m downwind, 17 m upwind and 56 m each flank in 30 s, at 3.2 ms a step with 6,459 props in the world and a 27 ms hash.''')\nrep('''**Drawing (page).** A burning cell spawns `prop:flame-patch` (parts with `flame.tongues`, an orange light spot on a few) and\nremoves it when done;''','''**Drawing (page).** A burning cell spawns `prop:flame-patch` (parts with `flame.tongues` **and a `tint`** such as `#ff7a2a`, or they draw white; an orange light spot on a few) and\nremoves it when done (verified, with no pipeline built after the first);''')\nrep('''wind's compass direction (emitter `velocity` is fixed data, so define 8 directions); ash falls near big fires.''','''wind's compass direction (emitter `velocity` is fixed data, so define 8 directions; verified with `gpu:true` smoke through `frame.emit`); ash falls near big fires.''')\n\n# ---- Vehicles: blocker and boat.\nrep('''- **People on foot vs cars** (not verified; test it): a `flyer` chassis doesn't block actors, so an unoccupied car also holds a\n  **blocker**, a kinematic body in group `crowd` moved to the chassis each step. Remove it while anyone is seated. Map shots\n  on it to the vehicle.''','''- **People on foot vs cars:** a `flyer` chassis doesn't block actors, so an unoccupied car also holds a **blocker**, a kinematic body in group `crowd` moved to the chassis each step (verified: it stops a\n  walker dead, and it doesn't touch the chassis). Remove it while anyone is seated. Map shots on it to the vehicle.''')\nrep('''- **Types:** open jeep; pickup technical with rear mounted MG (seat; `look` drives the mount, overheats, never jams); cargo truck\n  (convoys, AI only); patrol boat (same body with buoyancy springs at `waterY` instead of rays, thrust at the stern, a bow MG).''','''- **Types:** open jeep; pickup technical with rear mounted MG (seat; `look` drives the mount, overheats, never jams); cargo truck\n  (convoys, AI only); patrol boat with a bow MG.\n- **Boat (verified: floats level, 16 m/s, beaches on a bank without blowing up):** a `flyer` body, box 1.7 × 0.7 × 5 m, density 150, `linearDamping:0.05, angularDamping:1.2`. At 4 float points (±0.7, −0.3, ±2),\n  with `depth = waterY − point.y` above 0: upward `lift = max(0, 14000 × min(depth, 0.6) − 2400 × point vy)`. While any point is wet: `throttle × 5200` N forward and `−rudder × min(1, |speed|/4) × 1500` N sideways, both at the\n  stern (0, −0.3, −2.2); drag at the centre of `−speed × |speed| × 14 − speed × 40` forward and `−side × 900` sideways.''')\n\n# ---- River depth note.\nrep('''**The river** crosses west to east near z≈900 as a chain of negative `hills` (depth ≤ 1.2 m below `waterY`), drawn as one\nflat water prop at `waterY` with `texture:water`.''','''**The river** crosses west to east near z≈900 as a chain of negative `hills` (70 m apart, radius 85, as a start), drawn as one flat water prop at `waterY` with `texture:water`. Set `waterY` about\n1.1 m above the channel floor: read the floor with `terrainHeight`, so nobody needs to swim.''')\n\n# ---- Voices: a verified recipe.\nrep('''- **Voices (`sounds/voice.ts`):** Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Turn each line's text into syllables by hashing its words; each syllable is a very short formant-filtered voice (`defineSfx` per vowel and\n  consonant class, or direct Web Audio on `audio.context`) with per-character base pitch, rate and vibrato; play in sequence on the `ui` bus while the subtitle types out. NPC barks use positional\n  syllable bursts. Never real words.''','''- **Voices (`sounds/voice.ts`):** Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Turn each line's text into syllables by hashing its words; play them in sequence on the `ui` bus with per-character `pitch` while the subtitle\n  types out; NPC barks use positional syllable bursts. Never real words. A syllable per vowel (verified: renders 0.1 s at a peak near 0.2, unclipped): `defineSfx('sfx:voice-a', { voices: [{ wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.5,\n  attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F1, q:5 } }, { wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.35, attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F2, q:7 } }, { wave:'noise', dur:0.015, vol:0.05, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:2600, q:2 } }], variants:3, vary:0.05 })`, with (F1, F2) a 730, 1090; e 530, 1840; i 300, 2200; o 570, 840; u 320, 800.''')\nrep('''- **Music at 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested. Stored volume wins after the player changes it.''','''- **Music at 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested. Verified: it holds from before the first gesture (`locked`) through `running`, and the mixer reports 0.1. Stored volume wins after the player changes it.''')\n\n# ---- Controls and cameras.\nrep('''In a hub session the game ignores Options while Create is held.\n''','''In a hub session the game ignores Options while Create is held. Verified with a stubbed DualSense: a tap on Square fires only `interact` and a hold only `reload`, and Create likewise splits `ledger` and `view`, with prompts reading `Square` and `Hold Square`.\n''')\nrep('''With the gun holstered, the `first` rig shows raised fists. Where that looks wrong (the ceasefire town), a level `cam3=walk` of the same kind drops the hands out of frame; it must take `input.look` and `input.turn` as `first` does (not verified).\n''','''With the gun holstered, the `first` rig shows raised fists. In the ceasefire town, use a level `cam3=walk` of the same kind (eye height 1.52 m, 0.16 m forward, pitch 0), which keeps the hands out of frame. Verified: it turns with the stick (yaw −= `input.look[0]` + `input.turn[0]` × 2.5 × dt, pitch likewise), and the action map's `look` follows it, so moving forward goes where it faces.\n''')\nrep('''**HUD (first person):** five syrette-shaped''','''**Teaching what you can and can't do.** Each mechanic gets a one-time contextual hint when first met, and a short line on the Ledger's **Field notes** page from then on, limits included: guns can't be repaired, only replaced; pills can't be bought; nobody swims; there's no fast travel; a claimed safehouse stays yours and a checkpoint doesn't.\n\n**HUD (first person):** five syrette-shaped''')\nrep('''Ledger (a page each: debts,\nfactions, tapes, stones)''','''Ledger (a page each: debts,\nfactions, tapes, stones, field notes)''')\nrep('''If `matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)')` and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (`touch` setting `off`, no `touchLayout`).''','''If `matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)')` and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (`?touch=off` in `search`, no `touchLayout`; verified on Playwright's Pixel 7 profile, where the query is true, WebGPU runs, and a tap wakes no touch UI).''')\n\n# ---- Title and attract camera.\nrep('''`game.mode=title`: slow camera over the savanna at dusk with a fire line on the horizon (`cam3=fixed` or `rail`), title lettering in CSS, Title song.''','''`game.mode=title`: slow camera over the savanna at dusk with a fire line on the horizon (`cam3=rail`, or `cam3=fixed,x,y,z,yaw,pitch` in metres and degrees; keep it above the ground, since below it the picture draws white), title lettering in CSS, Title song. Restarting by mode is verified: `__engine.set('game.mode', 'b')`, `__engine.seed(n)`, and the scene comes up with the setting kept.''')\nrep('''Camera and look change per vignette (`__engine.camera.set`).''','''Camera and look change per vignette (`__engine.camera.set`). **The bot steers the first-person camera through `createEngine({ cameraInput: () => botView ? { view: [yaw, pitch] } : undefined })`** (verified, on a paused clock; cast it, since `view` isn't in the `CameraInput` type). `look` intents alone turn the actor but not that camera.''')\n\n# ---- Multiplayer: verified statements and the card.\nrep('''**Verified end to end:** a probe with this recipe passes the real harness in all four of its configurations, each listed in §13; four copies computed identical digests at the same step despite 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss; the `server` copy ran `createHeadless` in the browser, with no WebGPU.''','''**Verified end to end:** a probe with this recipe, the tags below and the game's own card passes the real harness in all four of its configurations (§13). In the browser, copies computed identical digests at the same step despite 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and the `server` copy ran `createHeadless` with no WebGPU. In Node, 2 and 4 seats plus a server copy, each with its own `game.me`, kept identical digests over 3,600 steps with moving seats, an NPC, a jeep and a spreading fire.''')\nrep('''The harness tests this by dispatching an Enter `keydown` in the frame, so listen on `window`. The probe ran with `card: false`, which passes; the own card itself isn't verified, so if it fights you late in the build, fall back to the hub's card with the tags above.''','''The harness tests this by dispatching an Enter `keydown` in the frame, so listen on `window` (verified: its card check passes in all four configurations). Remove the card on `start`.''')\n\n# ---- Testing: coverage test and stand-ins.\nrep('''| **Whole-game playthrough** (sim) | `bot.ts` completes prologue through every ending on seeds 1–3 with `game.god`, through intents only, using teleport-free travel for contract 1 and dev travel after. One honest run: bot clears Km-40 without god on at least 7 of 10 seeds, as a balance alarm. |''','''| **Whole-game playthrough** (sim) | `bot.ts` completes prologue through every ending on seeds 1–3 with `game.god`, through intents only, using teleport-free travel for contract 1 and dev travel after. One honest run: bot clears Km-40 without god on at least 7 of 10 seeds, as a balance alarm. **Feature coverage:** the main path must fire each of these at least once, proving the demo is long enough to show everything: jam, clear, gun break, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, mercy prompt, buddy death, fever warning, attack, pill, mirage, ignite, wind shift, ember jump, scorch, regrowth, drive, repair, vehicle explosion, boat, mounted gun, ropeway, stealth pass, holstered pass, purchase, reliability upgrade, armoury reissue, tin box, tape, sleep, save and load, standing change, alternate plan, every ending. |''')\nrep('''**Not checkable here; say so in your feedback:** real GPUs, real pads, speakers, cross-machine determinism, iOS.''','''**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (never required for real): no controller, so replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub returning a standard-mapping pad via `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`; no touch device, so use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles and tap with `page.touchscreen`; nobody to listen, so check sound by what Web Audio produces, through an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` levels; no second player, so play multiplayer with two pages or contexts; no real iPhone or iPad, so use WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest there is, though it has no WebGPU here; no TV, so judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n**Not checkable here; say so in your feedback:** real GPUs, real pads, speakers, cross-machine determinism, iOS.''')\n\n# ---- Exit interview: the full shape and questions in the plan.\nrep('''Write the exit-interview file in exactly the shape your TASK.md gives, with honest counts from your testing. Suggested catalogue values: genre `Shooter`, type `Game`, source `Far Cry 2`, series `Far Cry`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d, first-person, co-op, vehicles, exploration, low-poly`. Plan field: `PLAN.md`. Research: engine and hub source reading, this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did. Validate with `JSON.parse`, rebuild so About shows it, and put Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply too, using \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm, and Usage as \"added on import\". Say plainly what you did not verify.''','''**Critical, from the brief:** put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply **and** on the About/Credits screen, writing \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Say plainly what you did not verify.\n\n| Source Information | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Date | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\n| Source | Far Cry 2 |\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given: `PLAN.md` |\n| Genre and Perspective | Open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure; first person, with shoulder and chase options and body-camera moments |\n| Tech | my-3dge at the commit `engine-lock.json` names; TypeScript; three.js r182 on WebGPU; Rapier 0.21; HTML/CSS UI; Vite, Vitest, Playwright; what else you add, with versions |\n| Art Direction | The engine's native low-poly style, sun-bleached, with new procedural art (§9.1) |\n| Animation | The engine's animation system (procedural humanoid, actions, library clips) with new actions and flipbook guns |\n| Audio | All in code: tracker songs and synthesized effects through the engine's audio; Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (the client version you bundled), shared-world host-sequenced lockstep; on its own, a same-computer tab link and WebRTC invite codes |\n| Testing | The testing parameters allowed: from your TASK.md, as you used them |\n| Research | Engine and hub source reading and this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did, or none |\n| Environment, Model | Exactly as your TASK.md gives them |\n| Usage | Write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records it, and the import puts it on the About screen |\n\n**The exit-interview file,** in exactly this shape, every value filled in as plain text (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm), checked with `JSON.parse`. Tools read it. Rebuild afterwards so About shows it.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor this game: type `Game`, genre `Shooter`, source `Far Cry 2`, series `Far Cry`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d, first-person, co-op, vehicles, exploration, low-poly`. The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked here.\n\n- **Testing Feedback:** How many issues did testing catch? How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking? What was the most helpful test you ran? What was the least helpful test you ran?\n- **Engine Feedback:** What did you like most about the engine? Did you get stuck or guess wrong? What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? What was the hardest part about using the engine? What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing) If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n- **Prompt Feedback:** What did you like most about this prompt? What was confusing? What was the hardest thing to do? What would you change in this prompt?''')\n\n# ---- Build order: pre-warm; title.\nrep('''| 0 | §2 setup. Pick the title as TASK.md says (brainstorm silently, real RNG), into `title.ts`.''','''| 0 | §2 setup. Pick the title by §0.1's procedure, into `title.ts`.''')\n\n# ---- Coverage table: point at content, not at TASK.md.\na=s.index('### 15.4 Coverage: TASK.md → this plan')\ns=s[:a]+'''### 15.4 Coverage: TASK.md → this plan\n\nEvery line below is stated in this plan at the section named, not only referred to.\n\n| TASK.md item | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Source: Far Cry 2; original game inspired by it | §0.1, §1 |\n| Genre, perspective | §1 |\n| Title: riff, 50 brainstormed silently, real RNG | §0.1, §14 step 0 |\n| Research: your choice, reported | §0.1, §15.3 |\n| Single HTML in root, modules in a subfolder, CDN allowed | §0.1, §15.1 |\n| Full workspace; engine read-only; systems, art and animations on top; rest of stack free | §0.1, §2, §3, §15.2 |\n| Art direction | §0.1, §9.1 |\n| Animation | §0.1, §9.2, §3 wall 3, §10.2 |\n| Audio: code only, gibberish voices, music 10% | §0.1, §9.3, §13 page tier |\n| Online and multiplayer: hub and on its own | §0.1, §12 |\n| Shared machine: files, reads, ports, PIDs, browsers, Python, memory | §0.1, §2 |\n| Performance unlimited, FPS and optimization out of scope | §0.1 |\n| Testing: headless, one command, failures and count, playthrough, screenshots rarely, no debate agents | §0.1, §13 |\n| Stand-ins: pad, touch, audio, second player, iPhone, 1080p | §13 |\n| Feature-complete demo; long enough to show all features; what you can and can't do | §0.1, §8.4, §10.3, §13 feature coverage |\n| World, characters, themes, tone | §1, §5, §8 |\n| Infrastructure for later content with normal tools | §4, §6.10 |\n| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | §11.3 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, looping, cheats, any input exits | §11.2 |\n| Narrative tone | §8 |\n| 10-foot, DualSense or any standard-mapping pad; native keyboard and mouse on the same screens; hover focus, world clicks; prompts by last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §10 |\n| Touch-only: message, settings button, attract runs | §10.4 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §0, §0.1, §14 step 9 |\n| Source Information, in chat and About; every field | §11.4, §15.3 |\n| Exit-interview file: shape, every field, catalogue values | §15.3 |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback questions | §15.3 |\n'''\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf8').write(s)\nprint(len(s.split()),'words',s.count('\\n'),'lines')\nEOF\ngrep -n \"not verified\\|Not verified\\|unverified\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-140; grep -n \"§15 maps\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Write the brief's requirements and probe results into the plan"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6468,"cache_read_input_tokens":706396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6468},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"91c0523c-d056-4662-80fd-f1d5d896285f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:55:17.189Z","request_id":"req_011CgjG7QERKwf4R8RnhRk29","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Qw47P1cF8QaGwTfidewK7x":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf8').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, 'MISSING: '+a[:90]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# ---- Standing rules: the brief, restated in the plan.\nrep('''## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.**''','''## 0.1 Standing rules from the brief\n\nThese restate TASK.md so this plan stands on its own.\n\n- **Goal.** An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters,\n  themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX. It must be long enough to show **all** features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and\n  feels and what they can and cannot do (§8.4, field notes in §10.3, the coverage test in §13), with all the infrastructure for building out the rest with\n  normal designer tools (§4, §6.10).\n- **Title.** A playful riff on the source's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles (don't show the list), then pick one with a real random number generator by\n  running code; don't choose by taste. It goes in `title.ts`, on the title screen, in `<title>`, in the catalogue's `name`, and in the file and folder names your TASK.md's pattern asks for.\n- **Research.** How much is your choice. The engine and the hub are already researched here. Report whatever you did in Source Information (§15.3).\n- **Package.** Develop in separate modules in the dev folder, then bundle into one HTML file in the folder above it, named as your TASK.md says. External CDN\n  dependencies are allowed, but this plan's recipe inlines everything (§15.1).\n- **Tech.** my-3dge in its full workspace. The engine stays read-only; add whatever systems, art and animation the game needs on top. The rest of the stack is\n  your choice; this plan needs nothing beyond what the workspace ships (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright).\n- **Art, animation, audio.** The engine's native style and animation system as the base, with new assets and animations for whatever makes it feel like the source; the engine's asset\n  library is fine for generic props (§9.1, §9.2). All music, effects and voices are written in code with no audio files, voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish, and music defaults to **10%** (§9.3).\n- **Online and multiplayer.** Follow the [AI Hub session protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub) both for integration into the hub and for the game on its own (§12).\n- **Working environment.** Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` or a parent folder (the deliverable and the exit-interview file are the exceptions TASK.md sets). Don't read\n  files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside yours and share this machine's ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a\n  server (the harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. **Stop only processes you started, by PID: never `pkill`,\n  `killall`, `fuser -k`, or killing whatever holds a port.** Keep one or two browsers open at once and close them. Never run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`. The system Python has\n  Playwright and nothing else; for more, `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser\n  has been closed\" means memory ran out (earlyoom), not a bug in your game: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\n- **Performance.** Treat the budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as the scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Counts in this plan are\n  starting values for the design, not caps to protect a frame rate; raise them when a scene wants more.\n- **Testing.** Headless scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only\n  after changing the code it covers. Screenshots only for what a script can't check. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (§13).\n- **Effort.** Spend it however you see fit.\n- **Done.** No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.**''')\n\n# ---- Verified facts added to the engine table and walls.\nrep('''| Body animation | `defineAction` + `actOn` (call it after step 1, not in `setup`: verified), library clips (`node x anim find crawl`) | Credit imported clips per `data/anim/SOURCES.md` |''',\n'''| Body animation | `defineAction` + `actOn` (not in `setup`; see the spawn rule below), library clips (`node x anim find crawl`) | Credit imported clips per `data/anim/SOURCES.md` |''')\nrep('''| Data as a tool | `defineKind`/`def` for contracts, places, squads, dealer stock | Schemas check entries; `__engine.describe('contract')` lists them for designers |''',\n'''| Data as a tool | `defineKind`/`def` for contracts, places, squads, dealer stock (verified: a bad entry throws) | Schemas check entries; `__engine.describe('contract')` lists them for designers |''')\nrep('''3. **First-person arms are a closed rig.** Games can't pose them, and reloads aren't animated at all. Fakes: lower the gun\n   for a reload by setting `weapons.slot = -1` for the middle of the reload with `motion.holstered` kept false (film it to\n   check); kick by `shots`; big moments use the body camera (§10.2). Full-body actions always show in shoulder view and\n   to other players.''','''3. **First-person arms are a closed rig.** Games can't pose them, and reloads aren't animated at all. **What works (verified): a\n   flipbook.** Swap `weapons.slots[slot]` through weapon ids whose props hold the same gun tilted and lowered (`rotation` and `position` on the parts, the magazine\n   left out where it should be out). The first-person gun follows frame by frame and no pipeline is built, so reloads and jam clearing get real motion in the hands.\n   Setting `weapons.slot = -1` with `animator.weapon = 'none'` is a real holster: gun gone, raised fists; it's no reload. Kick comes from `shots`; big moments use the body\n   camera (§10.2). Full-body actions always show in shoulder view and to other players.''')\nrep('''Texture generators write `texel.color` in **sRGB bytes 0–255**, not 0–1 (verified; 0–1 draws black).\n''','''More verified traps:\n\n- Texture generators write `texel.color` in **sRGB bytes 0–255**, not 0–1 (0–1 draws black).\n- **An entity spawned in a step joins the world at that step's end.** `w.add`, `w.get` and `actOn` fail on it until then, so give components at spawn: `w.spawn({ … })`,\n  `spawnProp(w, placement, { components: { … } })`. An animated actor can take `actOn` from the next step on.\n- Dev actions are plain functions: `defineScene(id, { actions: { ignite: (w, args) => … } })`, run with `__engine.actions.run('ignite', args)`.\n- **Pre-warm while the loading screen is up.** The first holster and draw cycles built 32 pipelines and the first flame prop 2 more, stalling frames (one black frame on software WebGPU). After that,\n  holsters, flames, weapon-id swaps and fever filters built nothing. So in the scene's first moments, under the loading screen, cycle a holster twice and spawn one of each runtime prop out of sight.\n''')\n\n# ---- Guns: flipbook.\nrep('''- **Slots:** Sidearm, Primary, Special, plus machete (always), syrettes and throwables. Ammo per class, modest.''','''- **Reload and clear in the hands:** the flipbook of wall 3, 3–4 poses a gun, stepped by `arms` over the reload's time, with its sounds. Generate the ids with a helper (`gunVariants(gun)`: rust\n  tiers × poses) rather than writing them out.\n- **Slots:** Sidearm, Primary, Special, plus machete (always), syrettes and throwables. Ammo per class, modest.''')\n\n# ---- Fire: verified in the engine, and the tint.\nrep('''60 m all round. 10 m/s wind: about 150 m. So it outruns a crouch (2.0 m/s) and never a sprint (6.4 m/s).''','''60 m all round. 10 m/s wind: about 150 m. So it outruns a crouch (2.0 m/s) and never a sprint (6.4 m/s). **Verified in the engine** as one component of plain number arrays with grass patches burning\ninto scorch props: over 6 seeds, 109 m downwind, 17 m upwind and 56 m each flank in 30 s, at 3.2 ms a step with 6,459 props in the world and a 27 ms hash.''')\nrep('''**Drawing (page).** A burning cell spawns `prop:flame-patch` (parts with `flame.tongues`, an orange light spot on a few) and\nremoves it when done;''','''**Drawing (page).** A burning cell spawns `prop:flame-patch` (parts with `flame.tongues` **and a `tint`** such as `#ff7a2a`, or they draw white; an orange light spot on a few) and\nremoves it when done (verified, with no pipeline built after the first);''')\nrep('''wind's compass direction (emitter `velocity` is fixed data, so define 8 directions); ash falls near big fires.''','''wind's compass direction (emitter `velocity` is fixed data, so define 8 directions; verified with `gpu:true` smoke through `frame.emit`); ash falls near big fires.''')\n\n# ---- Vehicles: blocker and boat.\nrep('''- **People on foot vs cars** (not verified; test it): a `flyer` chassis doesn't block actors, so an unoccupied car also holds a\n  **blocker**, a kinematic body in group `crowd` moved to the chassis each step. Remove it while anyone is seated. Map shots\n  on it to the vehicle.''','''- **People on foot vs cars:** a `flyer` chassis doesn't block actors, so an unoccupied car also holds a **blocker**, a kinematic body in group `crowd` moved to the chassis each step (verified: it stops a\n  walker dead, and it doesn't touch the chassis). Remove it while anyone is seated. Map shots on it to the vehicle.''')\nrep('''- **Types:** open jeep; pickup technical with rear mounted MG (seat; `look` drives the mount, overheats, never jams); cargo truck\n  (convoys, AI only); patrol boat (same body with buoyancy springs at `waterY` instead of rays, thrust at the stern, a bow MG).''','''- **Types:** open jeep; pickup technical with rear mounted MG (seat; `look` drives the mount, overheats, never jams); cargo truck\n  (convoys, AI only); patrol boat with a bow MG.\n- **Boat (verified: floats level, 16 m/s, beaches on a bank without blowing up):** a `flyer` body, box 1.7 × 0.7 × 5 m, density 150, `linearDamping:0.05, angularDamping:1.2`. At 4 float points (±0.7, −0.3, ±2),\n  with `depth = waterY − point.y` above 0: upward `lift = max(0, 14000 × min(depth, 0.6) − 2400 × point vy)`. While any point is wet: `throttle × 5200` N forward and `−rudder × min(1, |speed|/4) × 1500` N sideways, both at the\n  stern (0, −0.3, −2.2); drag at the centre of `−speed × |speed| × 14 − speed × 40` forward and `−side × 900` sideways.''')\n\n# ---- River depth note.\nrep('''**The river** crosses west to east near z≈900 as a chain of negative `hills` (depth ≤ 1.2 m below `waterY`), drawn as one\nflat water prop at `waterY` with `texture:water`.''','''**The river** crosses west to east near z≈900 as a chain of negative `hills` (70 m apart, radius 85, as a start), drawn as one flat water prop at `waterY` with `texture:water`. Set `waterY` about\n1.1 m above the channel floor: read the floor with `terrainHeight`, so nobody needs to swim.''')\n\n# ---- Voices: a verified recipe.\nrep('''- **Voices (`sounds/voice.ts`):** Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Turn each line's text into syllables by hashing its words; each syllable is a very short formant-filtered voice (`defineSfx` per vowel and\n  consonant class, or direct Web Audio on `audio.context`) with per-character base pitch, rate and vibrato; play in sequence on the `ui` bus while the subtitle types out. NPC barks use positional\n  syllable bursts. Never real words.''','''- **Voices (`sounds/voice.ts`):** Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Turn each line's text into syllables by hashing its words; play them in sequence on the `ui` bus with per-character `pitch` while the subtitle\n  types out; NPC barks use positional syllable bursts. Never real words. A syllable per vowel (verified: renders 0.1 s at a peak near 0.2, unclipped): `defineSfx('sfx:voice-a', { voices: [{ wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.5,\n  attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F1, q:5 } }, { wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.35, attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F2, q:7 } }, { wave:'noise', dur:0.015, vol:0.05, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:2600, q:2 } }], variants:3, vary:0.05 })`, with (F1, F2) a 730, 1090; e 530, 1840; i 300, 2200; o 570, 840; u 320, 800.''')\nrep('''- **Music at 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested. Stored volume wins after the player changes it.''','''- **Music at 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested. Verified: it holds from before the first gesture (`locked`) through `running`, and the mixer reports 0.1. Stored volume wins after the player changes it.''')\n\n# ---- Controls and cameras.\nrep('''In a hub session the game ignores Options while Create is held.\n''','''In a hub session the game ignores Options while Create is held. Verified with a stubbed DualSense: a tap on Square fires only `interact` and a hold only `reload`, and Create likewise splits `ledger` and `view`, with prompts reading `Square` and `Hold Square`.\n''')\nrep('''With the gun holstered, the `first` rig shows raised fists. Where that looks wrong (the ceasefire town), a level `cam3=walk` of the same kind drops the hands out of frame; it must take `input.look` and `input.turn` as `first` does (not verified).\n''','''With the gun holstered, the `first` rig shows raised fists. In the ceasefire town, use a level `cam3=walk` of the same kind (eye height 1.52 m, 0.16 m forward, pitch 0), which keeps the hands out of frame. Verified: it turns with the stick (yaw −= `input.look[0]` + `input.turn[0]` × 2.5 × dt, pitch likewise), and the action map's `look` follows it, so moving forward goes where it faces.\n''')\nrep('''**HUD (first person):** five syrette-shaped''','''**Teaching what you can and can't do.** Each mechanic gets a one-time contextual hint when first met, and a short line on the Ledger's **Field notes** page from then on, limits included: guns can't be repaired, only replaced; pills can't be bought; nobody swims; there's no fast travel; a claimed safehouse stays yours and a checkpoint doesn't.\n\n**HUD (first person):** five syrette-shaped''')\nrep('''Ledger (a page each: debts,\nfactions, tapes, stones)''','''Ledger (a page each: debts,\nfactions, tapes, stones, field notes)''')\nrep('''If `matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)')` and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (`touch` setting `off`, no `touchLayout`).''','''If `matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)')` and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (`?touch=off` in `search`, no `touchLayout`; verified on Playwright's Pixel 7 profile, where the query is true, WebGPU runs, and a tap wakes no touch UI).''')\n\n# ---- Title and attract camera.\nrep('''`game.mode=title`: slow camera over the savanna at dusk with a fire line on the horizon (`cam3=fixed` or `rail`), title lettering in CSS, Title song.''','''`game.mode=title`: slow camera over the savanna at dusk with a fire line on the horizon (`cam3=rail`, or `cam3=fixed,x,y,z,yaw,pitch` in metres and degrees; keep it above the ground, since below it the picture draws white), title lettering in CSS, Title song. Restarting by mode is verified: `__engine.set('game.mode', 'b')`, `__engine.seed(n)`, and the scene comes up with the setting kept.''')\nrep('''Camera and look change per vignette (`__engine.camera.set`).''','''Camera and look change per vignette (`__engine.camera.set`). **The bot steers the first-person camera through `createEngine({ cameraInput: () => botView ? { view: [yaw, pitch] } : undefined })`** (verified, on a paused clock; cast it, since `view` isn't in the `CameraInput` type). `look` intents alone turn the actor but not that camera.''')\n\n# ---- Multiplayer: verified statements and the card.\nrep('''**Verified end to end:** a probe with this recipe passes the real harness in all four of its configurations, each listed in §13; four copies computed identical digests at the same step despite 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss; the `server` copy ran `createHeadless` in the browser, with no WebGPU.''','''**Verified end to end:** a probe with this recipe, the tags below and the game's own card passes the real harness in all four of its configurations (§13). In the browser, copies computed identical digests at the same step despite 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and the `server` copy ran `createHeadless` with no WebGPU. In Node, 2 and 4 seats plus a server copy, each with its own `game.me`, kept identical digests over 3,600 steps with moving seats, an NPC, a jeep and a spreading fire.''')\nrep('''The harness tests this by dispatching an Enter `keydown` in the frame, so listen on `window`. The probe ran with `card: false`, which passes; the own card itself isn't verified, so if it fights you late in the build, fall back to the hub's card with the tags above.''','''The harness tests this by dispatching an Enter `keydown` in the frame, so listen on `window` (verified: its card check passes in all four configurations). Remove the card on `start`.''')\n\n# ---- Testing: coverage test and stand-ins.\nrep('''| **Whole-game playthrough** (sim) | `bot.ts` completes prologue through every ending on seeds 1–3 with `game.god`, through intents only, using teleport-free travel for contract 1 and dev travel after. One honest run: bot clears Km-40 without god on at least 7 of 10 seeds, as a balance alarm. |''','''| **Whole-game playthrough** (sim) | `bot.ts` completes prologue through every ending on seeds 1–3 with `game.god`, through intents only, using teleport-free travel for contract 1 and dev travel after. One honest run: bot clears Km-40 without god on at least 7 of 10 seeds, as a balance alarm. **Feature coverage:** the main path must fire each of these at least once, proving the demo is long enough to show everything: jam, clear, gun break, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, mercy prompt, buddy death, fever warning, attack, pill, mirage, ignite, wind shift, ember jump, scorch, regrowth, drive, repair, vehicle explosion, boat, mounted gun, ropeway, stealth pass, holstered pass, purchase, reliability upgrade, armoury reissue, tin box, tape, sleep, save and load, standing change, alternate plan, every ending. |''')\nrep('''**Not checkable here; say so in your feedback:** real GPUs, real pads, speakers, cross-machine determinism, iOS.''','''**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (never required for real): no controller, so replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub returning a standard-mapping pad via `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`; no touch device, so use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles and tap with `page.touchscreen`; nobody to listen, so check sound by what Web Audio produces, through an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` levels; no second player, so play multiplayer with two pages or contexts; no real iPhone or iPad, so use WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest there is, though it has no WebGPU here; no TV, so judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n**Not checkable here; say so in your feedback:** real GPUs, real pads, speakers, cross-machine determinism, iOS.''')\n\n# ---- Exit interview: the full shape and questions in the plan.\nrep('''Write the exit-interview file in exactly the shape your TASK.md gives, with honest counts from your testing. Suggested catalogue values: genre `Shooter`, type `Game`, source `Far Cry 2`, series `Far Cry`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d, first-person, co-op, vehicles, exploration, low-poly`. Plan field: `PLAN.md`. Research: engine and hub source reading, this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did. Validate with `JSON.parse`, rebuild so About shows it, and put Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply too, using \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm, and Usage as \"added on import\". Say plainly what you did not verify.''','''**Critical, from the brief:** put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply **and** on the About/Credits screen, writing \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Say plainly what you did not verify.\n\n| Source Information | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Date | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\n| Source | Far Cry 2 |\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given: `PLAN.md` |\n| Genre and Perspective | Open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure; first person, with shoulder and chase options and body-camera moments |\n| Tech | my-3dge at the commit `engine-lock.json` names; TypeScript; three.js r182 on WebGPU; Rapier 0.21; HTML/CSS UI; Vite, Vitest, Playwright; what else you add, with versions |\n| Art Direction | The engine's native low-poly style, sun-bleached, with new procedural art (§9.1) |\n| Animation | The engine's animation system (procedural humanoid, actions, library clips) with new actions and flipbook guns |\n| Audio | All in code: tracker songs and synthesized effects through the engine's audio; Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (the client version you bundled), shared-world host-sequenced lockstep; on its own, a same-computer tab link and WebRTC invite codes |\n| Testing | The testing parameters allowed: from your TASK.md, as you used them |\n| Research | Engine and hub source reading and this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did, or none |\n| Environment, Model | Exactly as your TASK.md gives them |\n| Usage | Write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records it, and the import puts it on the About screen |\n\n**The exit-interview file,** in exactly this shape, every value filled in as plain text (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm), checked with `JSON.parse`. Tools read it. Rebuild afterwards so About shows it.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor this game: type `Game`, genre `Shooter`, source `Far Cry 2`, series `Far Cry`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d, first-person, co-op, vehicles, exploration, low-poly`. The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked here.\n\n- **Testing Feedback:** How many issues did testing catch? How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking? What was the most helpful test you ran? What was the least helpful test you ran?\n- **Engine Feedback:** What did you like most about the engine? Did you get stuck or guess wrong? What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? What was the hardest part about using the engine? What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing) If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n- **Prompt Feedback:** What did you like most about this prompt? What was confusing? What was the hardest thing to do? What would you change in this prompt?''')\n\n# ---- Build order: pre-warm; title.\nrep('''| 0 | §2 setup. Pick the title as TASK.md says (brainstorm silently, real RNG), into `title.ts`.''','''| 0 | §2 setup. Pick the title by §0.1's procedure, into `title.ts`.''')\n\n# ---- Coverage table: point at content, not at TASK.md.\na=s.index('### 15.4 Coverage: TASK.md → this plan')\ns=s[:a]+'''### 15.4 Coverage: TASK.md → this plan\n\nEvery line below is stated in this plan at the section named, not only referred to.\n\n| TASK.md item | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Source: Far Cry 2; original game inspired by it | §0.1, §1 |\n| Genre, perspective | §1 |\n| Title: riff, 50 brainstormed silently, real RNG | §0.1, §14 step 0 |\n| Research: your choice, reported | §0.1, §15.3 |\n| Single HTML in root, modules in a subfolder, CDN allowed | §0.1, §15.1 |\n| Full workspace; engine read-only; systems, art and animations on top; rest of stack free | §0.1, §2, §3, §15.2 |\n| Art direction | §0.1, §9.1 |\n| Animation | §0.1, §9.2, §3 wall 3, §10.2 |\n| Audio: code only, gibberish voices, music 10% | §0.1, §9.3, §13 page tier |\n| Online and multiplayer: hub and on its own | §0.1, §12 |\n| Shared machine: files, reads, ports, PIDs, browsers, Python, memory | §0.1, §2 |\n| Performance unlimited, FPS and optimization out of scope | §0.1 |\n| Testing: headless, one command, failures and count, playthrough, screenshots rarely, no debate agents | §0.1, §13 |\n| Stand-ins: pad, touch, audio, second player, iPhone, 1080p | §13 |\n| Feature-complete demo; long enough to show all features; what you can and can't do | §0.1, §8.4, §10.3, §13 feature coverage |\n| World, characters, themes, tone | §1, §5, §8 |\n| Infrastructure for later content with normal tools | §4, §6.10 |\n| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | §11.3 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, looping, cheats, any input exits | §11.2 |\n| Narrative tone | §8 |\n| 10-foot, DualSense or any standard-mapping pad; native keyboard and mouse on the same screens; hover focus, world clicks; prompts by last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §10 |\n| Touch-only: message, settings button, attract runs | §10.4 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §0, §0.1, §14 step 9 |\n| Source Information, in chat and About; every field | §11.4, §15.3 |\n| Exit-interview file: shape, every field, catalogue values | §15.3 |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback questions | §15.3 |\n'''\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf8').write(s)\nprint(len(s.split()),'words',s.count('\\n'),'lines')\nEOF\ngrep -n \"not verified\\|Not verified\\|unverified\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-140; grep -n \"§15 maps\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Write the brief's requirements and probe results into the plan"}}}
