{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VJeNDwkVgFBPsLj6bMQuH6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: Far Cry 2\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## This computer\n33\tChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n34\t\n35\t- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n36\t- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n37\t- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n38\t- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n39\t  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n40\t  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n41\t  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n42\t- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n43\t- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n44\t- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n45\t- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n46\t- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n47\t\n48\t### What this computer doesn't have\n49\t\n50\tNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n51\t\n52\t- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n53\t- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n54\t- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n55\t- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n56\t- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n57\t- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n58\t\n59\t### Sharing this computer\n60\t\n61\t- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n62\t- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n63\t- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n64\t- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n65\t\n66\t## Goal\n67\t\n68\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n69\t\n70\tScope\n71\t\n72\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73\t\n74\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75\t\n76\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n77\t\n78\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n79\t\n80\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n81\t\n82\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n83\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n84\t\n85\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n86\t\n87\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n88\t\n89\t## Done\n90\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"40428a63-8350-45c8-95d6-bba3819ce097","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:56:40.043Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: Far Cry 2\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.","numLines":90,"startLine":1,"totalLines":152}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VJeNDwkVgFBPsLj6bMQuH6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjGM9zJQr27JLYL8CbEJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019c657cvPdgXZmrdjmZHXHT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":130},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":734312,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1250},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"1a481eac-78c5-417a-8e5f-16981d5b78b9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:56:41.362Z","request_id":"req_011CgjGM9fDDS9kT79dKx8Mi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019c657cvPdgXZmrdjmZHXHT":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":130}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019c657cvPdgXZmrdjmZHXHT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: an open-world first-person mercenary game in a burning country\n2\t\n3\tInspired by Far Cry 2. Original world, cast, story, art and music. You build it from this plan and your TASK.md,\n4\twithout the plan's author. Paths are relative to your dev folder. \"The deliverable\" means the `.html` your TASK.md\n5\tnames, in the folder above the dev folder (`..`); \"the exit-interview file\" is the JSON file your TASK.md names, beside it.\n6\t\n7\t## 0. How to use this plan\n8\t\n9\t- **The design is decided.** Build it; don't reopen it. Every number is a starting value and most are dev sliders:\n10\t  tune by playtest scripts. Where you must deviate, do the simplest thing that keeps the feature, and write the call in\n11\t  `labs/game/README.md`.\n12\t- **\"Verified\"** means the plan's author ran it on this machine on 2026-11-04, on engine commit `e7a1243` and hub client\n13\t  1.4.0, with throwaway probes on software WebGPU. If a verified recipe fails for you, look for what changed; don't redesign around it. What couldn't be checked here is listed in §13.\n14\t- **Your TASK.md wins** over this plan on anything it states. §15.4 maps each TASK.md requirement to the section here that states it.\n15\t- **Features beat content.** No feature on the never-cut list may be stubbed. If you run short, shrink content, never\n16\t  below the floors:\n17\t\n18\t| Never cut | May shrink, never below |\n19\t|---|---|\n20\t| Gun wear, jams and clearing; wounds, syrettes and field surgery; fire with wind; fever and pills; buddies with rescue, debt and mercy; drivable vehicles with repair; map in hand; two factions' contracts and an ending; stones, locator and dealer; safehouses and saves; stealth in grass; day and night; dev mode with sliders; attract mode; hub session passing the harness; co-op on its own; touch notice; About/Credits; code-only audio with gibberish voices | Places: 7 (town + 6). Contracts: prologue + 5. Weapons: 8. Tapes: 4. Stone caches: 5. Buddies: 3. Enemy types: 4. Vehicles: pickup + boat. Songs: 3 |\n21\t\n22\t## 0.1 Standing rules from the brief\n23\t\n24\tThese restate TASK.md so this plan stands on its own.\n25\t\n26\t- **Goal.** An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters,\n27\t  themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX. It must be long enough to show **all** features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and\n28\t  feels and what they can and cannot do (§8.4, field notes in §10.3, the coverage test in §13), with all the infrastructure for building out the rest with\n29\t  normal designer tools (§4, §6.10).\n30\t- **Title.** A playful riff on the source's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles (don't show the list), then pick one with a real random number generator by\n31\t  running code; don't choose by taste. It goes in `title.ts`, on the title screen, in `<title>`, in the catalogue's `name`, and in the file and folder names your TASK.md's pattern asks for.\n32\t- **Research.** How much is your choice. The engine and the hub are already researched here. Report whatever you did in Source Information (§15.3).\n33\t- **Package.** Develop in separate modules in the dev folder, then bundle into one HTML file in the folder above it, named as your TASK.md says. External CDN\n34\t  dependencies are allowed, but this plan's recipe inlines everything (§15.1).\n35\t- **Tech.** my-3dge in its full workspace. The engine stays read-only; add whatever systems, art and animation the game needs on top. The rest of the stack is\n36\t  your choice; this plan needs nothing beyond what the workspace ships (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright).\n37\t- **Art, animation, audio.** The engine's native style and animation system as the base, with new assets and animations for whatever makes it feel like the source; the engine's asset\n38\t  library is fine for generic props (§9.1, §9.2). All music, effects and voices are written in code with no audio files, voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish, and music defaults to **10%** (§9.3).\n39\t- **Online and multiplayer.** Follow the [AI Hub session protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub) both for integration into the hub and for the game on its own (§12).\n40\t- **Working environment.** Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` or a parent folder (the deliverable and the exit-interview file are the exceptions TASK.md sets). Don't read\n41\t  files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside yours and share this machine's ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a\n42\t  server (the harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. **Stop only processes you started, by PID: never `pkill`,\n43\t  `killall`, `fuser -k`, or killing whatever holds a port.** Keep one or two browsers open at once and close them. Never run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`. The system Python has\n44\t  Playwright and nothing else; for more, `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser\n45\t  has been closed\" means memory ran out (earlyoom), not a bug in your game: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\n46\t- **Performance.** Treat the budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as the scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Counts in this plan are\n47\t  starting values for the design, not caps to protect a frame rate; raise them when a scene wants more.\n48\t- **Testing.** Headless scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only\n49\t  after changing the code it covers. Screenshots only for what a script can't check. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (§13).\n50\t- **Effort.** Spend it however you see fit.\n51\t- **Done.** No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n52\t\n53\t## 1. The game\n54\t\n55\t**Pitch.** You come up the river at the end of the dry season to serve a warrant on the Auctioneer, the arms dealer who\n56\tkeeps a civil war solvent by selling to both sides. You have a fever, a rusting rifle, debts to people who may or may not\n57\tdrag you out of the next fire, and a wind that is the only honest thing in the country.\n58\t\n59\t**Genre:** open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure. **Perspective:** first person, with\n60\tan over-the-shoulder option in Settings, a chase option while driving, and short eye-level \"body camera\" moments (§10.2).\n61\t\n62\t**Pillars**\n63\t\n64\t1. **Everything wears out.** Guns, vehicles, your body, your friends. Reliability is the real economy.\n65\t2. **Fire is your best weapon and you don't control it.** Wind decides. Burn scars stay and change the map.\n66\t3. **No clean hands.** Two factions, no good side, debts kept in a ledger. The writing never winks.\n67\t4. **The body is the interface.** Map in the hands, pills from the bottle, a bullet dug out of the arm, a wrench on the\n68\t   engine, all in real time. Only the pause menu stops the world.\n69\t5. **The land is the antagonist.** Long sightlines, chest-high grass, checkpoints on every road, heat haze.\n70\t\n71\t**What we take from the source, and what we make of it.** Mechanics are fair game; names, characters, places, story\n72\tbeats, art and music are not.\n73\t\n74\t| Source's idea | This game's version |\n75\t|---|---|\n76\t| Fire spreads with the wind | Same verb, deeper: a forecastable wind, ember spotting, fuel types, firebreaks, smoke that blinds AI, scars that strip stealth cover and regrow |\n77\t| Guns degrade and jam | Same, and condition shows on the gun in three rust tiers; enemy guns are junk; reliability upgrades at the dealer |\n78\t| Malaria and pills | Blackwater fever and quinine; attacks can show harmless mirages of people you failed |\n79\t| Buddies rescue you | A **ledger of debts**: each rescue is owed, and overdrawn buddies stop coming; mercy or a syrette when they fall |\n80\t| Map held in the hands | Paper map and tick-box locator on a diegetic CSS layer; you can walk or drive with it up |\n81\t| Diamonds | Rough stones in dead prospectors' tin boxes, found by a Geiger-style tick-box |\n82\t| Arms dealer villain with tapes | The **Auctioneer**: cassette \"lots\" in the patter of a sale. Every contract you do adds to his catalogue |\n83\t| Two rotten factions, ceasefire town | The Provisional Authority and the Basin Front; ceasefire in Sainte-Aurore |\n84\t\n85\t**Original-work rules.** Use nothing named or written in the source: no character, faction, place or mission names, no\n86\ttaxi or hotel opening, no tapes of a villain quoting philosophers, no music, logos or screens that copy it.\n87\tThe title is the one riff on the source's name, as TASK.md asks; everything else stands alone. Don't call the game a\n88\tsequel. The country, its war and its factions are invented: no real conflict, ethnic group or leader is named or caricatured,\n89\tand its people are written as individuals on every side. The hired mercs come from real countries and are written as individuals too.\n90\t\n91\t## 2. Setup and verified ground truth\n92\t\n93\tRun from the empty dev folder:\n94\t\n95\t```sh\n96\tmkdir -p .cache && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge .cache/engine-src   # stays: the engine's examples, to read\n97\tnode .cache/engine-src/tools/game/new-game.mjs . --name <slug> && rm -rf .cache/engine-src/.git\n98\tnpm ci --no-audit --no-fund                     # 6 s. Do NOT run scripts/setup.sh: it downloads Node and Chrome outside your folder\n99\texport CHROMIUM_PATH=$(ls ~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-*/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell | head -1)\n100\tnpm test && npm run check && node x shot labs/game   # all green on the starter (verified)\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tRead `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` once. **`labs/playground/` is not in a game's workspace**, but it is the best\n104\tworked example of nearly everything here (first person, guns, bodies, doors, sites, neighbours, looks, front end, attract\n105\tmode, sound): read it at `.cache/engine-src/labs/playground/` as needed. Grep `docs/API.md`; don't read it whole.\n106\t\n107\tVerified facts that will save you hours:\n108\t\n109\t- **Browser flags.** TASK.md's listed flags (`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan\n110\t  --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) alone **fail with this engine** (\"Instance dropped in popErrorScope\", never ready).\n111\t  Use `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Adding\n112\t  `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` is harmless. With these the starter is `ready` in 3.6 s from `file://`.\n113\t- **WebGPU needs a secure page:** `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n114\t- **Headless shell only.** Playwright's default headless works. Full Chromium dies here with \"Socket path too long\"\n115\t  because run paths are long, so skip headed mode and xvfb. Never run `playwright install`; the browsers are already there.\n116\t- **`CHROMIUM_PATH`** (above) makes `node x shot`, `x film`, `x inspect` and `x replay --browser` work. Node 24.22.1 is\n117\t  fine. In Node drivers: `chromium.launch({ executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH, args })` from `@playwright/test`.\n118\t- **Ports.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` piped to a script never prints its port (buffered): use\n119\t  `python3 -u`, or Node's `http` on port 0. Confirm the page's marker before trusting a result. Stop only your own PID.\n120\t- **What the checks scan.** `npm run check` fails on any PNG or base64 blob under the dev folder, except in git-ignored\n121\t  places: `out/` and `.cache/` are skipped by ESLint, Prettier, the asset scan and Vitest. Screenshots and temp bundles go\n122\t  in `out/`, hub files in `.cache/hub/`, the deliverable outside the dev folder.\n123\t- **Git.** The dev folder sits inside a larger repository that isn't yours. Make no commits there; `git init` inside the\n124\t  dev folder if you want history.\n125\t- **Sim costs on this machine.** A 1536 m terrain at 4 m cells adds 0.35 ms a step. An actor costs about 0.15 ms. A prop\n126\t  with **no** body costs about nothing a step. A prop with **its own body** costs about 1.2 µs a step (3,000 trees 3.9 ms),\n127\t  so trees are `body:'none'` props plus **one** fixed body holding all their colliders (verified). A site of 40 buildings\n128\t  on that terrain starts in 1.1 s and steps 24 actors in 1.8 ms.\n129\t- **`capture()` is 35–80 MB of JSON** at this scale. Never use it for saves or network resync.\n130\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"f5275bb8-9d0d-4362-9c49-83697ae1ce79","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:56:41.372Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: an open-world first-person mercenary game in a burning country\n\nInspired by Far Cry 2. Original world, cast, story, art and music. You build it from this plan and your TASK.md,\nwithout the plan's author. Paths are relative to your dev folder. \"The deliverable\" means the `.html` your TASK.md\nnames, in the folder above the dev folder (`..`); \"the exit-interview file\" is the JSON file your TASK.md names, beside it.\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **The design is decided.** Build it; don't reopen it. Every number is a starting value and most are dev sliders:\n  tune by playtest scripts. Where you must deviate, do the simplest thing that keeps the feature, and write the call in\n  `labs/game/README.md`.\n- **\"Verified\"** means the plan's author ran it on this machine on 2026-11-04, on engine commit `e7a1243` and hub client\n  1.4.0, with throwaway probes on software WebGPU. If a verified recipe fails for you, look for what changed; don't redesign around it. What couldn't be checked here is listed in §13.\n- **Your TASK.md wins** over this plan on anything it states. §15.4 maps each TASK.md requirement to the section here that states it.\n- **Features beat content.** No feature on the never-cut list may be stubbed. If you run short, shrink content, never\n  below the floors:\n\n| Never cut | May shrink, never below |\n|---|---|\n| Gun wear, jams and clearing; wounds, syrettes and field surgery; fire with wind; fever and pills; buddies with rescue, debt and mercy; drivable vehicles with repair; map in hand; two factions' contracts and an ending; stones, locator and dealer; safehouses and saves; stealth in grass; day and night; dev mode with sliders; attract mode; hub session passing the harness; co-op on its own; touch notice; About/Credits; code-only audio with gibberish voices | Places: 7 (town + 6). Contracts: prologue + 5. Weapons: 8. Tapes: 4. Stone caches: 5. Buddies: 3. Enemy types: 4. Vehicles: pickup + boat. Songs: 3 |\n\n## 0.1 Standing rules from the brief\n\nThese restate TASK.md so this plan stands on its own.\n\n- **Goal.** An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters,\n  themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX. It must be long enough to show **all** features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and\n  feels and what they can and cannot do (§8.4, field notes in §10.3, the coverage test in §13), with all the infrastructure for building out the rest with\n  normal designer tools (§4, §6.10).\n- **Title.** A playful riff on the source's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles (don't show the list), then pick one with a real random number generator by\n  running code; don't choose by taste. It goes in `title.ts`, on the title screen, in `<title>`, in the catalogue's `name`, and in the file and folder names your TASK.md's pattern asks for.\n- **Research.** How much is your choice. The engine and the hub are already researched here. Report whatever you did in Source Information (§15.3).\n- **Package.** Develop in separate modules in the dev folder, then bundle into one HTML file in the folder above it, named as your TASK.md says. External CDN\n  dependencies are allowed, but this plan's recipe inlines everything (§15.1).\n- **Tech.** my-3dge in its full workspace. The engine stays read-only; add whatever systems, art and animation the game needs on top. The rest of the stack is\n  your choice; this plan needs nothing beyond what the workspace ships (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright).\n- **Art, animation, audio.** The engine's native style and animation system as the base, with new assets and animations for whatever makes it feel like the source; the engine's asset\n  library is fine for generic props (§9.1, §9.2). All music, effects and voices are written in code with no audio files, voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish, and music defaults to **10%** (§9.3).\n- **Online and multiplayer.** Follow the [AI Hub session protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub) both for integration into the hub and for the game on its own (§12).\n- **Working environment.** Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` or a parent folder (the deliverable and the exit-interview file are the exceptions TASK.md sets). Don't read\n  files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside yours and share this machine's ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a\n  server (the harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. **Stop only processes you started, by PID: never `pkill`,\n  `killall`, `fuser -k`, or killing whatever holds a port.** Keep one or two browsers open at once and close them. Never run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`. The system Python has\n  Playwright and nothing else; for more, `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser\n  has been closed\" means memory ran out (earlyoom), not a bug in your game: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\n- **Performance.** Treat the budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as the scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Counts in this plan are\n  starting values for the design, not caps to protect a frame rate; raise them when a scene wants more.\n- **Testing.** Headless scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only\n  after changing the code it covers. Screenshots only for what a script can't check. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (§13).\n- **Effort.** Spend it however you see fit.\n- **Done.** No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You come up the river at the end of the dry season to serve a warrant on the Auctioneer, the arms dealer who\nkeeps a civil war solvent by selling to both sides. You have a fever, a rusting rifle, debts to people who may or may not\ndrag you out of the next fire, and a wind that is the only honest thing in the country.\n\n**Genre:** open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure. **Perspective:** first person, with\nan over-the-shoulder option in Settings, a chase option while driving, and short eye-level \"body camera\" moments (§10.2).\n\n**Pillars**\n\n1. **Everything wears out.** Guns, vehicles, your body, your friends. Reliability is the real economy.\n2. **Fire is your best weapon and you don't control it.** Wind decides. Burn scars stay and change the map.\n3. **No clean hands.** Two factions, no good side, debts kept in a ledger. The writing never winks.\n4. **The body is the interface.** Map in the hands, pills from the bottle, a bullet dug out of the arm, a wrench on the\n   engine, all in real time. Only the pause menu stops the world.\n5. **The land is the antagonist.** Long sightlines, chest-high grass, checkpoints on every road, heat haze.\n\n**What we take from the source, and what we make of it.** Mechanics are fair game; names, characters, places, story\nbeats, art and music are not.\n\n| Source's idea | This game's version |\n|---|---|\n| Fire spreads with the wind | Same verb, deeper: a forecastable wind, ember spotting, fuel types, firebreaks, smoke that blinds AI, scars that strip stealth cover and regrow |\n| Guns degrade and jam | Same, and condition shows on the gun in three rust tiers; enemy guns are junk; reliability upgrades at the dealer |\n| Malaria and pills | Blackwater fever and quinine; attacks can show harmless mirages of people you failed |\n| Buddies rescue you | A **ledger of debts**: each rescue is owed, and overdrawn buddies stop coming; mercy or a syrette when they fall |\n| Map held in the hands | Paper map and tick-box locator on a diegetic CSS layer; you can walk or drive with it up |\n| Diamonds | Rough stones in dead prospectors' tin boxes, found by a Geiger-style tick-box |\n| Arms dealer villain with tapes | The **Auctioneer**: cassette \"lots\" in the patter of a sale. Every contract you do adds to his catalogue |\n| Two rotten factions, ceasefire town | The Provisional Authority and the Basin Front; ceasefire in Sainte-Aurore |\n\n**Original-work rules.** Use nothing named or written in the source: no character, faction, place or mission names, no\ntaxi or hotel opening, no tapes of a villain quoting philosophers, no music, logos or screens that copy it.\nThe title is the one riff on the source's name, as TASK.md asks; everything else stands alone. Don't call the game a\nsequel. The country, its war and its factions are invented: no real conflict, ethnic group or leader is named or caricatured,\nand its people are written as individuals on every side. The hired mercs come from real countries and are written as individuals too.\n\n## 2. Setup and verified ground truth\n\nRun from the empty dev folder:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p .cache && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge .cache/engine-src   # stays: the engine's examples, to read\nnode .cache/engine-src/tools/game/new-game.mjs . --name <slug> && rm -rf .cache/engine-src/.git\nnpm ci --no-audit --no-fund                     # 6 s. Do NOT run scripts/setup.sh: it downloads Node and Chrome outside your folder\nexport CHROMIUM_PATH=$(ls ~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-*/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell | head -1)\nnpm test && npm run check && node x shot labs/game   # all green on the starter (verified)\n```\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` once. **`labs/playground/` is not in a game's workspace**, but it is the best\nworked example of nearly everything here (first person, guns, bodies, doors, sites, neighbours, looks, front end, attract\nmode, sound): read it at `.cache/engine-src/labs/playground/` as needed. Grep `docs/API.md`; don't read it whole.\n\nVerified facts that will save you hours:\n\n- **Browser flags.** TASK.md's listed flags (`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan\n  --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) alone **fail with this engine** (\"Instance dropped in popErrorScope\", never ready).\n  Use `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Adding\n  `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` is harmless. With these the starter is `ready` in 3.6 s from `file://`.\n- **WebGPU needs a secure page:** `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n- **Headless shell only.** Playwright's default headless works. Full Chromium dies here with \"Socket path too long\"\n  because run paths are long, so skip headed mode and xvfb. Never run `playwright install`; the browsers are already there.\n- **`CHROMIUM_PATH`** (above) makes `node x shot`, `x film`, `x inspect` and `x replay --browser` work. Node 24.22.1 is\n  fine. In Node drivers: `chromium.launch({ executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH, args })` from `@playwright/test`.\n- **Ports.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` piped to a script never prints its port (buffered): use\n  `python3 -u`, or Node's `http` on port 0. Confirm the page's marker before trusting a result. Stop only your own PID.\n- **What the checks scan.** `npm run check` fails on any PNG or base64 blob under the dev folder, except in git-ignored\n  places: `out/` and `.cache/` are skipped by ESLint, Prettier, the asset scan and Vitest. Screenshots and temp bundles go\n  in `out/`, hub files in `.cache/hub/`, the deliverable outside the dev folder.\n- **Git.** The dev folder sits inside a larger repository that isn't yours. Make no commits there; `git init` inside the\n  dev folder if you want history.\n- **Sim costs on this machine.** A 1536 m terrain at 4 m cells adds 0.35 ms a step. An actor costs about 0.15 ms. A prop\n  with **no** body costs about nothing a step. A prop with **its own body** costs about 1.2 µs a step (3,000 trees 3.9 ms),\n  so trees are `body:'none'` props plus **one** fixed body holding all their colliders (verified). A site of 40 buildings\n  on that terrain starts in 1.1 s and steps 24 actors in 1.8 ms.\n- **`capture()` is 35–80 MB of JSON** at this scale. Never use it for saves or network resync.\n","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":917}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019c657cvPdgXZmrdjmZHXHT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjGM9zJQr27JLYL8CbEJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JfjkqnA4joe4mpA6PPGm6s","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/PLAN.md","offset":590,"limit":330},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":734312,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1250},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"c69adf2b-e66f-4826-b938-acffb6e9ed76","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:56:42.663Z","request_id":"req_011CgjGM9fDDS9kT79dKx8Mi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JfjkqnA4joe4mpA6PPGm6s":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/PLAN.md","offset":590,"limit":330}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JfjkqnA4joe4mpA6PPGm6s","type":"tool_result","content":"590\t### 9.3 Audio: code only, no audio files\n591\t\n592\t- **Music at 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested. Verified: it holds from before the first gesture (`locked`) through `running`, and the mixer reports 0.1. Stored volume wins after the player changes it.\n593\t- **Songs (`defineSong`, original, 3–5):** sparse desert-blues over plucked pentatonic arpeggios and hand drums: Title, Roam (low and tense, intensity layers), Combat\n594\t  (`audio.intensity` by alert state), Fever (detuned, slow), Ending. Nothing that echoes the source's score.\n595\t- **SFX (`defineSfx`):** each gun class with distance tail (`sound` on the weapon), dry click, jam rack, reload parts, machete, grenade, rocket, flare, fire crackle in three sizes, flare-up whoosh,\n596\t  barrel explosion, cook-off pops, engines by rpm (pitch on a loop), gears, skid, wrench, car door, boat, footsteps by surface (`SURFACE_STEPS`, add grass rustle), wind by speed, insects day\n597\t  and night, thunder, rain, tick-box click, tape hiss and clunk, syrette, pill rattle, surgery wince, heartbeat, UI cues.\n598\t- **Voices (`sounds/voice.ts`):** Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Turn each line's text into syllables by hashing its words; play them in sequence on the `ui` bus with per-character `pitch` while the subtitle\n599\t  types out; NPC barks use positional syllable bursts. Never real words. A syllable per vowel (verified: renders 0.1 s at a peak near 0.2, unclipped): `defineSfx('sfx:voice-a', { voices: [{ wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.5,\n600\t  attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F1, q:5 } }, { wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.35, attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F2, q:7 } }, { wave:'noise', dur:0.015, vol:0.05, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:2600, q:2 } }], variants:3, vary:0.05 })`, with (F1, F2) a 730, 1090; e 530, 1840; i 300, 2200; o 570, 840; u 320, 800.\n601\t- **Mix:** `room` reverb for interiors, `duck` during dialogue, `update(pose, queries)` each frame for 3D and occlusion. Settings sliders: Master, Music, Effects, Voices, Ambience.\n602\t\n603\t## 10. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n604\t\n605\t### 10.1 Controls\n606\t\n607\tEvery device gets the same game on the same screens; no desktop layout. Own action maps (`defineActionMap`, `look:true, aimWith:'centre', lock:true` for first person; a second for vehicles). Bindings rebindable with `input.listen`.\n608\t\n609\t| Action | DualSense | Keyboard and mouse |\n610\t|---|---|---|\n611\t| Move / Look | L stick / R stick | WASD / mouse |\n612\t| Fire / Aim | R2 / L2 | LMB / RMB |\n613\t| Jump, mantle | Cross | Space |\n614\t| Crouch | Circle | C (never Ctrl: Ctrl+W closes the tab) |\n615\t| Sprint | L3 | Left Shift |\n616\t| Use (tap), Reload and clear jam (hold) | Square, `Hold:PadWest` | E, R |\n617\t| Next weapon / holster (hold) | Triangle | 1 2 3, wheel / X |\n618\t| Melee | R3 | V |\n619\t| Throw | R1 | G |\n620\t| Syrette (surgery when critical) | L1 | H |\n621\t| Map / Tick-box / Pills / Fire-gun quick-draw | D-pad up, down, left, right | M / B / P / F |\n622\t| Ledger | Create | L |\n623\t| Camera view toggle | Hold Create | T |\n624\t| Pause | Options | Esc |\n625\t| Drive: throttle, brake, steer, handbrake, exit, seat | R2, L2, L stick, Cross, Square, Triangle | W, S, A D, Space, E, Q |\n626\t\n627\tIn a hub session the game ignores Options while Create is held. Verified with a stubbed DualSense: a tap on Square fires only `interact` and a hold only `reload`, and Create likewise splits `ledger` and `view`, with prompts reading `Square` and `Hold Square`.\n628\t\n629\t**Mouse is native:** pointer lock in play; in menus hover moves focus and click picks (`createMenu`; custom panels too, `createPadCursor` for pad). **Things in the world:** under lock, the crosshair is the\n630\tpointer: looking at something usable highlights it with a world-anchored label, and with no weapon out a left click uses it, as well as E.\n631\t\n632\t**Prompts follow the last input.** `ui/glyphs.ts`: inline SVG for PlayStation shapes (✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1 L2 R2 L3 R3 Options Create, D-pad) and Xbox (A B X Y LB RB LT RT LS RS Menu View). Pick by `input.lastDevice`: `gamepad` → PlayStation\n633\tglyphs for any pad, or Xbox if Settings says so; keyboard → key caps and mouse icons with the actual binding (`input.bindingsFor`, `codeLabel`, `readKeyboardLayout`). Every prompt element carries `data-glyph` for tests, and they swap at once when the device changes. Verified: with a\n634\tstubbed DualSense, `input.lastDevice` turns `gamepad`, `input.held`/`strength` work, and `input.prompt('jump')` is `Cross`.\n635\t\n636\t### 10.2 First person without a closed door\n637\t\n638\tThe default camera is `cam3=first`. For **body moments** (surgery, syrette on critical, repair, rescue from the ground) blend (`__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`) to custom `cam3=body` (`defineCamera`, `followCamera`, `makePose`):\n639\tat the eye about 0.1 m forward, pitch about −50°, body drawn whole because only `first` hides it. **Verified:** this shows the player's own sleeves, hands and gun in frame during a `defineAction`; framing needs tuning (holster the gun, arms farther out). The head's back faces don't block\n640\tit. `cam3=driver` is the same idea with a level pitch at the driver's eye. The world keeps running throughout.\n641\t\n642\tWith the gun holstered, the `first` rig shows raised fists. In the ceasefire town, use a level `cam3=walk` of the same kind (eye height 1.52 m, 0.16 m forward, pitch 0), which keeps the hands out of frame. Verified: it turns with the stick (yaw −= `input.look[0]` + `input.turn[0]` × 2.5 × dt, pitch likewise), and the action map's `look` follows it, so moving forward goes where it faces.\n643\t\n644\t### 10.3 Screens and HUD\n645\t\n646\t**10-foot rules:** type scales with `vh`; body text ≥ 3.2vh (34 px at 1080p), headings 6vh, subtitles 3.4vh on a dark band; 5% safe margins; one decision a screen; at most 7 visible list items; no tables of data: the dealer and ledger show one thing large, stepped with\n647\tleft/right. System font stacks only (stencil-ish headings via `Impact, \"Arial Narrow\"`, typed notes via `\"Courier New\"`); no font files. Visual language: field notebook, stencil, carbon-copy paper, rust.\n648\t\n649\t**Teaching what you can and can't do.** Each mechanic gets a one-time contextual hint when first met, and a short line on the Ledger's **Field notes** page from then on, limits included: guns can't be repaired, only replaced; pills can't be bought; nobody swims; there's no fast travel; a claimed safehouse stays yours and a checkpoint doesn't.\n650\t\n651\t**HUD (first person):** five syrette-shaped health segments, ammo and gun condition as a small rust bar, syrette and pill counts only when they change or are low; centre dot crosshair (none down the sights); goal line for 5 s when it changes; wind ribbon as per §6.5; damage arcs; prompts near centre-bottom. No\n652\tminimap, ever.\n653\t\n654\t**Map in hand (`ui/map.ts`), the world doesn't pause.** A paper map on a CSS 3D layer (`perspective`, `rotateX`, slides up with an arm-raise ease, sways with the camera's turn), SVG hands gripping its edges in the merc's skin tone. Contour lines baked once to a canvas from `terrain.heights`; places as ink labels once discovered; roads, river; burn scars as hatch from fire state; an arrow for you; goal marker;\n655\twind arrow. R stick or pointer moves a pencil cursor, Cross or click drops a waypoint (a world-anchored marker). You walk at half speed with it up, and you can drive with it up (it sits low and to the right). The tick-box shows beside it as a small device with lamp and needle.\n656\t\n657\t**Screens:** Title (Continue, New Game, Co-op, Settings, Controls, About); merc pick; Pause (Resume, Map, Ledger, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About, Quit to title); Settings; Controls with rebinding (a picture of the pad and a short list); Dialogue (subtitle band with portrait name; choices as a short `createMenu`); Sleep (dawn, noon, dusk); Dealer; Ledger (a page each: debts,\n658\tfactions, tapes, stones, field notes); Co-op lobby; Game over; Ending; Credits.\n659\t\n660\t**Settings:** Master, Music (10%), Effects, Voices, Ambience; Glyphs (PlayStation / Xbox); Camera (first / shoulder); FOV (`cam.fov`); mouse and stick sensitivity and invert (`input.*`), aim-assist-free; Crouch hold/toggle; Fever intensity; Screen shake; Subtitle size; Quality (`quality`); Fullscreen (`requestFullscreen`). Keep with `createStore`.\n661\t\n662\t### 10.4 Touch-only devices\n663\t\n664\tIf `matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)')` and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (`?touch=off` in `search`, no `touchLayout`; verified on Playwright's Pixel 7 profile, where the query is true, WebGPU runs, and a tap wakes no touch UI). Show a full-screen message exactly **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** and a **Settings** button over the attract mode, which keeps running. A pad press or key press dismisses it for good. Test with Playwright's phone profile.\n665\t\n666\tIf WebGPU is missing on any device, show a plain message naming a browser that has it; the engine has no WebGL fallback, and the sim still runs.\n667\t\n668\t## 11. Title, attract mode, dev mode, About\n669\t\n670\t### 11.1 Title\n671\t\n672\t`game.mode=title`: slow camera over the savanna at dusk with a fire line on the horizon (`cam3=rail`, or `cam3=fixed,x,y,z,yaw,pitch` in metres and degrees; keep it above the ground, since below it the picture draws white), title lettering in CSS, Title song. Restarting by mode is verified: `__engine.set('game.mode', 'b')`, `__engine.seed(n)`, and the scene comes up with the setting kept. The view lights and shadows round the player actor, so park it near the camera's target, out of frame. Starting play sets the mode and calls `inspector.seed(n)`.\n673\t\n674\t### 11.2 Attract mode\n675\t\n676\tAfter **10 s without input** on the title or any menu off the title (keys, mouse move or click, pad buttons or sticks past deadzone all reset the timer), switch to `game.mode=attract` and restart. A page-side director (`attract.ts`) plays a loop of vignettes by placing the bot with dev actions and feeding `inspector.step(1, botIntents)` on a paused clock, with cheats on (`game.god`, infinite ammo; teleporting\n677\tand scripted fires are fine). Each runs 15–25 s with a banner: title + \"Press any button\":\n678\t\n679\t1. Chase camera: a technical tearing down the road at dusk. 2. First person: firefight at Km-40. 3. Flare into grass; wind carries it; soldiers panic. 4. A jam at the worst moment, cleared. 5. Fever attack with mirages, pill. 6. Down, buddy rescue, ledger line. 7. Map in hand and tick-box, finding a tin box. 8. Boat with mounted gun past the burning estate. 9. Dealer and tape caption, then loop.\n680\t\n681\tCamera and look change per vignette (`__engine.camera.set`). **The bot steers the first-person camera through `createEngine({ cameraInput: () => botView ? { view: [yaw, pitch] } : undefined })`** (verified, on a paused clock; cast it, since `view` isn't in the `CameraInput` type). `look` intents alone turn the actor but not that camera. **Any input exits at once** to the title (restart). It runs on touch-only devices too, and off-line: nothing is fetched. Never start it when automation should test the title unless asked: a setting `game.attract=auto|off|on` for tests.\n682\t\n683\t### 11.3 Dev mode: Pause → Dev Mode (not in hub sessions)\n684\t\n685\tPad and mouse friendly, big rows, one category at a time:\n686\t\n687\t- **Balance:** live sliders on `scenes/settings.ts`, each with its value and a per-row and global **Reset to defaults**: wear per shot, jam multiplier, player damage, enemy damage, enemy accuracy, reaction, sight, hearing, attackers, respawn hours; fire spread, wind bias, burn time, fire damage, regrow; wind speed, shift interval; fever interval, severity; HP, regen, syrette heal; car engine, grip, spring, damper; prices, stone payouts; day length; `movement.*`. Settings use `when:'now'` and systems read them at each use. `__engine.set` applies a value; reset writes each default from the table back the same way.\n688\t- **Cheats:** god, infinite ammo, no wear/jam, no fever, invisible to AI, noclip, one-shot kills.\n689\t- **Teleport:** every place, the crosshair point, a chosen map point.\n690\t- **Spawn:** any gun with chosen condition, vehicle, squad, buddy, tin box.\n691\t- **World:** hour, time scale (`time.scale`), wind dial, rain, ignite at crosshair, burn all, regrow all, fever now.\n692\t- **Story:** jump to any contract or step, set flags, debts, standing, give stones.\n693\t- **Debug overlays:** fire grid, AI state labels (`engine.ui.label`), detection meters, nav paths; `__engine.stats()` numbers; digest; quick save/load.\n694\t\n695\tSliders survive across resume, not across New Game unless \"Keep\" is on.\n696\t\n697\t### 11.4 About/Credits\n698\t\n699\tReachable from title and pause; pad and mouse scrollable pages. Pages: Source Information (all fields in TASK.md), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, Credits (engine, three.js, Rapier, @stdlib, motion clips per `data/anim/SOURCES.md`, source note: \"inspired by Far Cry 2, not affiliated\"). **One source of truth:** `scripts/bundle.mjs` reads the exit-interview file and injects it as data, so the screen and the file can't differ. Usage reads literally \"added on import\".\n700\t\n701\t## 12. Multiplayer\n702\t\n703\t**Decision: `shared-world`, host-sequenced deterministic lockstep.** The engine's one superpower is bit-exact determinism, so instead of replicating fire cells, ragdolls and NPCs, every copy runs the same scene from the same seed, and the host sequences everyone's input. No per-entity networking code.\n704\t\n705\t**Verified end to end:** a probe with this recipe, the tags below and the game's own card passes the real harness in all four of its configurations (§13). In the browser, copies computed identical digests at the same step despite 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and the `server` copy ran `createHeadless` with no WebGPU. In Node, 2 and 4 seats plus a server copy, each with its own `game.me`, kept identical digests over 3,600 steps with moving seats, an NPC, a jeep and a spreading fire.\n706\t\n707\t### 12.1 The mode: **Contract Run** (`game.mode=contract`)\n708\t\n709\t1–4 mercs, about 5 minutes, in the Pump Station region: reach the depot, burn it (flare, sprayer or charges at 3 points, all driven by wind), survive the counter-attack, get out by the river boat or pickups. Revive teammates (hold use 3 s; bleed-out 40 s). Friendly fire and friendly fire-fire are on. All down, or time up without getting out: team loses. `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })`: score = objective 500 + kills 50 + rescues 150 + extracted 200 − downs 100 − teammates burned 200; awards like \"Best employee of the wind\". The hub deals by these tags:\n710\t\n711\t```html\n712\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n713\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n714\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n715\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Burn the depot, then get out alive together\">\n716\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick or WASD moves, R2 or click fires, Square or E uses\">\n717\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n718\t<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">\n719\t```\n720\t\n721\tSolo in-hub (1 seat) still works. Names and colours come from `info.players`; set names with `textContent`, armbands by seat colour. No chat, emotes or custom names, no `alert`, no new tabs, no menus before the round, and pause opens only Settings without stopping the sim. Quit does nothing.\n722\t\n723\t### 12.2 The session flow (order matters)\n724\t\n725\t1. `session.js` is the first script after `<meta charset>`, unmodified (bundler does this).\n726\t2. In `main.ts`, first thing: `if (AIHub.active) info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. `null` means play on as standalone.\n727\t3. Start: `createEngine` with `search: ?seed=${info.seed}&game.mode=contract&game.seats=${n}&game.me=${info.me}&cam3=first&gfx.warmUp=asked`. **`gfx.warmUp=asked` is required:** with the default `all`, four copies sharing one main thread blocked past the deadlines. On a software adapter also lower `game.detail` (one fixed value across copies; pass through the host if adapters differ, or simply use a fixed contract-mode density).\n728\t4. **Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the sim exists, not when the first frame draws.** Poll `typeof window.__engine?.step === 'function'` every 20 ms (reached in about 1 s); then `__engine.pause()`, start the pump, `ready()`. The harness allows all copies 20 s, and rendering took 5–25 s on software WebGPU with 4 frames; the view catches up by itself.\n729\t5. **The pump:** a 16 ms `setInterval` alone passes the harness (verified); once frames flow, step from `onFrame` for smooth motion, and keep the timer for when frames stop. All copies run on the paused clock through `inspector.step`. **While paused, the camera still turns with mouse and stick** (verified: look is view state), so aim feels immediate on every copy.\n730\t6. `AIHub.server`: no engine. `await createHeadless({ scene, seed, settings: { 'game.seats': n, 'game.me': -1, 'game.mode': 'contract' } })`, pump on the timer, call `finish`. If `navigator.gpu` is missing on a playing copy: `AIHub.fail('WebGPU is required')`.\n731\t7. `time-up` → host `finish` at once from its handler. `end` → stop input, stop sound (`audio.mute(true)`), stop stepping, send nothing. Host leaving voids the round by protocol; do nothing special.\n732\t\n733\t### 12.3 The lockstep core (`net/lockstep.ts`)\n734\t\n735\t```ts\n736\tconst TURN = 4;                         // steps a turn: 15 messages a second, under the hub's 20\n737\t// Local wire, rebuilt each frame in onFrame from engine.input (held/strength), frame.camera (yaw, pitch) and an aim ray:\n738\t//   { m:[x,z], l:[yaw,pitch], a:[x,y,z], b:[...buttons pressed or held since last sent] }   rounded to 3 decimals\n739\t// client, every 66 ms:  net.send({ k:'i', w: wire }, { to: host, reliable: true })\n740\t// host, every 66 ms, ONE message however many turns are due (a burst per turn breaks the rate limit; verified):\n741\t//   const due = Math.floor(net.time() / (1000 / 60));\n742\t//   const n = Math.min(Math.floor((due - sent) / TURN), 30); if (n > 0) {\n743\t//     latest[me] = wire; const f = { k:'f', s: sent, n, i: { ...latest }, x: seatEvents };\n744\t//     net.send(f, { reliable: true }); queue.push(f); sent += TURN * n; }\n745\t// every copy: for each frame in order, for each of TURN*n steps, when due:\n746\t//   engine.step(1, { ...toIntents(f.i[me]), 'game:seats': othersAsIntents(f.i, me), ...(first ? { 'game:events': f.x } : {}) });\n747\t// Playout: run max(1, dt*60) steps a frame while frames wait, up to 12 when far behind, so motion is smooth at 60 Hz.\n748\t```\n749\t\n750\t**Rules that keep copies identical:**\n751\t\n752\t- **Everything the sim sees comes through the host's frames.** A `leave` event, a pick in a dialogue, a revive: all are host-sequenced (`x`, `game:events`, or the wire). Never feed a local `leave` straight to the sim: copies hear it at different times.\n753\t- `game.me` differs per copy, so `actor.player` differs, so **`engine.hash()` differs across copies by design**. Compare `digest.ts` (seats, NPCs, vehicles' positions and HP, fire state counts, as a string) instead. Clients send `{ k:'d', s, h }` every 2 s; the host logs mismatches and shows a small \"out of sync\" note; tests fail on it. No resync: `capture()` is far too big, and determinism holds by construction.\n754\t- Keep sim settings identical across copies (from the host via URL), dev mode off, and sanitize wire data (finite numbers, known buttons) before it reaches intents: `normalizeIntents` throws on junk.\n755\t- A tap shorter than a turn must survive: OR presses into `b` until the wire is sent.\n756\t\n757\t### 12.4 The game on its own\n758\t\n759\tOutside the hub, Title → Co-op runs the same Contract Run with no server, through the same `Net` interface (`send`, `on('message')`, `time`, `players`, `isHost`):\n760\t\n761\t- **`net/tabs.ts`: \"Same computer\":** `BroadcastChannel`; host lists itself, others join; a second tab, window or screen. Verified from `file://` between two pages.\n762\t- **`net/rtc.ts`: \"Invite code\":** one `RTCPeerConnection` and ordered data channel per guest (star on the host), non-trickle ICE, `iceServers:[{urls:'stun:stun.l.google.com:19302'}]`, base64 of the description as a copy-and-paste code with a Copy button and a Paste field: host's code to guest, guest's reply to host. Verified from `file://` in two separate browser contexts with no STUN: connects in 0.3 s with 650-byte descriptions.\n763\t\n764\tUse the hub's seat colours and a local 3 s countdown. Rule: none of this runs while `AIHub.active` (network only through the hub).\n765\t\n766\t### 12.5 Own card\n767\t\n768\tBecause the game has its own world, use `card: true`: HTML/CSS card over the loading scene showing the game's name, players in colours with ready ticks, how to win, this player's controls, time left from the `card` event. Enter, Cross or a click: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle, a named key or a click: `AIHub.answer('more')`. No menus; nothing changes the round. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, after `start`. The harness tests this by dispatching an Enter `keydown` in the frame, so listen on `window` (verified: its card check passes in all four configurations). Remove the card on `start`.\n769\t\n770\t## 13. Testing\n771\t\n772\t**One command:** `node tests/game/verify.mjs [sim|page|hub]` (add `\"verify\"` to `package.json`). It prints only failures and a final one-line count (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Vitest with `--reporter=json` and parsed. Rerun a tier only after changing what it covers. Screenshots only for what a script can't judge; save them in `out/`. Keep one or two browsers open at a time. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n773\t\n774\t| Tier | What it proves |\n775\t|---|---|\n776\t| **sim** (Vitest, Node; seconds to minutes) | Starter's four kinds kept: golden replay (1), capture/restore, bot, rules. Wear lowers condition and swaps tier ids; jam rate at given conditions over many shots; clearing works. HP, regen, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, debt, overdraw. Fever timeline, pills, collapse. **Fire front metric** (§6.5), firebreaks hold, embers jump, rain stops it, regrowth. Wind shifts on schedule with forecast. Stealth: same patrol detects standing in the open and misses crouched in grass; burned grass exposes. AI squad clears cover and flanks; jam window. Checkpoint repopulation; holstered pass with standing. Vehicle: drives a route, stays on terrain, repairs, explodes; `placeActor` seat; run-over. Economy purchases, caches, armoury. Save → reload → same state fields. Contract engine: every contract and alt completes by script. **Lockstep digest:** 1, 2, 4 headless copies with different `game.me` fed the same frames keep equal digests over 3,600 steps with a fight, a fire and a car in them. |\n777\t| **Whole-game playthrough** (sim) | `bot.ts` completes prologue through every ending on seeds 1–3 with `game.god`, through intents only, using teleport-free travel for contract 1 and dev travel after. One honest run: bot clears Km-40 without god on at least 7 of 10 seeds, as a balance alarm. **Feature coverage:** the main path must fire each of these at least once, proving the demo is long enough to show everything: jam, clear, gun break, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, mercy prompt, buddy death, fever warning, attack, pill, mirage, ignite, wind shift, ember jump, scorch, regrowth, drive, repair, vehicle explosion, boat, mounted gun, ropeway, stealth pass, holstered pass, purchase, reliability upgrade, armoury reissue, tin box, tape, sleep, save and load, standing change, alternate plan, every ending. |\n778\t| **page** (Playwright on the deliverable over `file://`) | Boots `ready` with no errors. Title → attract at 10 s; any key, mouse or pad input exits it (virtual clock not needed; use `game.attract` and a short setting for the wait, and one real 10 s test). Keyboard-only and pad-only (stubbed `navigator.getGamepads`) routes from title to first control. Prompts swap device on input; Xbox option; `data-glyph` values. Music bus reads 0.1 at first run; volume persists. `renderSong`, `renderSfx` and an `OfflineAudioContext` run of a voice line produce non-silent, unclipped audio (`node x qa audio`). Pause and Dev Mode: a slider changes behaviour, reset restores. Map opens without pausing the tick count. Mouse hover moves menu focus. About matches the exit-interview file. Phone profile shows the exact touch message, Settings button, no touch controls, attract running. WebKit and Firefox have no WebGPU here, so Chromium only; say so. |\n779\t| **hub** | Fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` to `.cache/hub/`; copy the deliverable beside them; serve on an OS-picked 127.0.0.1 port; confirm the page marker. All must give `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` (`document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` when finished): `players=2`; `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; the same with `scenario=host-leave`; `players=2&scenario=server`; all with `&auto=1`. Launch args include the WebGPU flags of §2. Then standalone: same deliverable alone, solo; two pages over tabs link; two contexts over invite codes, digests equal. |\n780\t| **look** (rare) | `node x shot` numbers and `node x gallery prop` for art; 1920×1080 screenshots of each UI screen for the 10-foot judgement. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot; check state instead when it does. |\n781\t\n782\t**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (never required for real): no controller, so replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub returning a standard-mapping pad via `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`; no touch device, so use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles and tap with `page.touchscreen`; nobody to listen, so check sound by what Web Audio produces, through an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` levels; no second player, so play multiplayer with two pages or contexts; no real iPhone or iPad, so use WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest there is, though it has no WebGPU here; no TV, so judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n783\t\n784\t**Not checkable here; say so in your feedback:** real GPUs, real pads, speakers, cross-machine determinism, iOS.\n785\t\n786\t## 14. Build order\n787\t\n788\tBuild a walking skeleton first, then keep the deliverable working at every step. Run `npm run check` and the relevant `verify` tier before moving on.\n789\t\n790\t| # | Do | Done when |\n791\t|---|---|---|\n792\t| 0 | §2 setup. Pick the title by §0.1's procedure, into `title.ts`. Write `scripts/bundle.mjs` (§15.1), `fetch-hub.mjs`, `verify.mjs` skeleton. Add hub metas and a stub session | One-file loads over `file://`; harness passes with the starter + stub |\n793\t| 1 | Site, terrain, river, roads, scatter, looks, day cycle; `movement:merc`; `seats.ts`; action maps; first person; HUD shell | Walk the map in first person; prompts follow device |\n794\t| 2 | `arms.ts`, wear and jam, `health.ts`, one gun, Rifleman, perception, Km-40. **Now `lockstep.ts` + digest test + `net/hub.ts`**, so co-op stays honest from here | Bot clears Km-40; digests equal; harness passes with guns |\n795\t| 3 | Fire, wind, flare, grass burning, scars, panic | Fire metric test; looks like fire in a shot |\n796\t| 4 | Vehicles, driver AI, patrols, repair, boat, mounted guns | Bot drives a route; vehicle tests |\n797\t| 5 | Fever, buddies, ledger, stones, tick-box, dealer, safehouses, saves, rest of the guns and enemy types | Tier tests green |\n798\t| 6 | Contract engine, all story content, dialogue, voices, tapes, map in hand, endings | Playthrough bot finishes the game |\n799\t| 7 | Contract Run, own card, tabs and invite links | Full hub tier green |\n800\t| 8 | Title, pause, settings, controls, dev mode, attract, touch notice, About | Page tier green |\n801\t| 9 | Art, animation, audio passes across everything; balance by sliders and bot | No placeholder boxes or stand-ins anywhere; a search for TODO, FIXME, placeholder finds nothing |\n802\t| 10 | Finish: §15 | All below |\n803\t\n804\t## 15. Finish line\n805\t\n806\t### 15.1 Packaging: `scripts/bundle.mjs` (verified: 6.7 MB, loads from `file://`, harness passes)\n807\t\n808\t```js\n809\timport { build } from 'vite'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { join } from 'node:path';\n810\tconst root = process.cwd();\n811\tconst result = await build({ root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',\n812\t  resolve: { alias: [{ find: /^three$/, replacement: 'three/webgpu' }] },\n813\t  build: { write: false, modulePreload: false, cssCodeSplit: false, assetsInlineLimit: () => true, chunkSizeWarningLimit: Infinity,\n814\t    rollupOptions: { input: { game: join(root, 'labs/game/index.html') }, output: { inlineDynamicImports: true } } } });\n815\tconst files = {}; let html;\n816\tfor (const item of [result].flat().flatMap((o) => o.output)) {\n817\t  const text = item.type === 'chunk' ? item.code : typeof item.source === 'string' ? item.source : new TextDecoder().decode(item.source);\n818\t  if (item.fileName.endsWith('.html')) html = text; else files[item.fileName] = text; }\n819\tconst find = (ref) => files[Object.keys(files).find((f) => ref.endsWith('/' + f) || ref === f || ref === './' + f)];\n820\thtml = html.replace(/<script type=\"module\" crossorigin src=\"([^\"]+)\"><\\/script>|<link rel=\"stylesheet\" crossorigin href=\"([^\"]+)\">/g, (_t, s, c) => {\n821\t  if (c) return `<style>${find(c)}</style>`;\n822\t  const code = find(s).replaceAll('</script', '<\\\\/script');\n823\t  if (code.includes('<!--')) throw new Error('script holds <!--: split it in the source');\n824\t  return `<script type=\"module\">${code}</script>`; });\n825\tconst session = readFileSync('.cache/hub/session.js', 'utf8'); // the unmodified client: scripts/fetch-hub.mjs downloads it\n826\thtml = html.replace('<meta charset=\"utf-8\" />', () => `<meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\\n<script>${session}</script>\\n${HUB_METAS}`);\n827\twriteFileSync(DELIVERABLE, html); // DELIVERABLE: the name your TASK.md gives, in '..'; inject the exit-interview file as data too\n828\t```\n829\t\n830\t`fetch-hub.mjs` downloads `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/` into `.cache/hub/`, where lint and format can't touch them: the client must stay byte-identical. The protocol's full text is `README.md` at the same address; read it once. Everything in the deliverable is inline, so nothing depends on a CDN.\n831\t\n832\t### 15.2 Your own feedback\n833\t\n834\tKeep `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` current as AGENTS.md asks. Starting points, if they bite you too: arms rig closed to games; weapons, melee and use only for the `player` actor; no scene access for game meshes (raw geometry through `.constructor` is a hack); TASK.md's browser flags vs the engine's; `capture` size; `setup.sh` downloads outside the folder; one scene per page. Don't patch the engine (it stays read-only); `npm run check` proves it. Keep `labs/game/README.md` true: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n835\t\n836\t### 15.3 Exit interview\n837\t\n838\t**Critical, from the brief:** put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply **and** on the About/Credits screen, writing \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Say plainly what you did not verify.\n839\t\n840\t| Source Information | What to write |\n841\t|---|---|\n842\t| Date | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\n843\t| Source | Far Cry 2 |\n844\t| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given: `PLAN.md` |\n845\t| Genre and Perspective | Open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure; first person, with shoulder and chase options and body-camera moments |\n846\t| Tech | my-3dge at the commit `engine-lock.json` names; TypeScript; three.js r182 on WebGPU; Rapier 0.21; HTML/CSS UI; Vite, Vitest, Playwright; what else you add, with versions |\n847\t| Art Direction | The engine's native low-poly style, sun-bleached, with new procedural art (§9.1) |\n848\t| Animation | The engine's animation system (procedural humanoid, actions, library clips) with new actions and flipbook guns |\n849\t| Audio | All in code: tracker songs and synthesized effects through the engine's audio; Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\n850\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (the client version you bundled), shared-world host-sequenced lockstep; on its own, a same-computer tab link and WebRTC invite codes |\n851\t| Testing | The testing parameters allowed: from your TASK.md, as you used them |\n852\t| Research | Engine and hub source reading and this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did, or none |\n853\t| Environment, Model | Exactly as your TASK.md gives them |\n854\t| Usage | Write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records it, and the import puts it on the About screen |\n855\t\n856\t**The exit-interview file,** in exactly this shape, every value filled in as plain text (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm), checked with `JSON.parse`. Tools read it. Rebuild afterwards so About shows it.\n857\t\n858\t```json\n859\t{\n860\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n861\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n862\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n863\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n864\t  \"aiHub\": {\n865\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n866\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n867\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n868\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n869\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n870\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on\",\n871\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, or empty\",\n872\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n873\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n874\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags\"]\n875\t  }\n876\t}\n877\t```\n878\t\n879\tFor this game: type `Game`, genre `Shooter`, source `Far Cry 2`, series `Far Cry`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d, first-person, co-op, vehicles, exploration, low-poly`. The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked here.\n880\t\n881\t- **Testing Feedback:** How many issues did testing catch? How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking? What was the most helpful test you ran? What was the least helpful test you ran?\n882\t- **Engine Feedback:** What did you like most about the engine? Did you get stuck or guess wrong? What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? What was the hardest part about using the engine? What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing) If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n883\t- **Prompt Feedback:** What did you like most about this prompt? What was confusing? What was the hardest thing to do? What would you change in this prompt?\n884\t\n885\t### 15.4 Coverage: TASK.md → this plan\n886\t\n887\tEvery line below is stated in this plan at the section named, not only referred to.\n888\t\n889\t| TASK.md item | Where |\n890\t|---|---|\n891\t| Source: Far Cry 2; original game inspired by it | §0.1, §1 |\n892\t| Genre, perspective | §1 |\n893\t| Title: riff, 50 brainstormed silently, real RNG | §0.1, §14 step 0 |\n894\t| Research: your choice, reported | §0.1, §15.3 |\n895\t| Single HTML in root, modules in a subfolder, CDN allowed | §0.1, §15.1 |\n896\t| Full workspace; engine read-only; systems, art and animations on top; rest of stack free | §0.1, §2, §3, §15.2 |\n897\t| Art direction | §0.1, §9.1 |\n898\t| Animation | §0.1, §9.2, §3 wall 3, §10.2 |\n899\t| Audio: code only, gibberish voices, music 10% | §0.1, §9.3, §13 page tier |\n900\t| Online and multiplayer: hub and on its own | §0.1, §12 |\n901\t| Shared machine: files, reads, ports, PIDs, browsers, Python, memory | §0.1, §2 |\n902\t| Performance unlimited, FPS and optimization out of scope | §0.1 |\n903\t| Testing: headless, one command, failures and count, playthrough, screenshots rarely, no debate agents | §0.1, §13 |\n904\t| Stand-ins: pad, touch, audio, second player, iPhone, 1080p | §13 |\n905\t| Feature-complete demo; long enough to show all features; what you can and can't do | §0.1, §8.4, §10.3, §13 feature coverage |\n906\t| World, characters, themes, tone | §1, §5, §8 |\n907\t| Infrastructure for later content with normal tools | §4, §6.10 |\n908\t| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | §11.3 |\n909\t| Attract mode at 10 s, looping, cheats, any input exits | §11.2 |\n910\t| Narrative tone | §8 |\n911\t| 10-foot, DualSense or any standard-mapping pad; native keyboard and mouse on the same screens; hover focus, world clicks; prompts by last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §10 |\n912\t| Touch-only: message, settings button, attract runs | §10.4 |\n913\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §0, §0.1, §14 step 9 |\n914\t| Source Information, in chat and About; every field | §11.4, §15.3 |\n915\t| Exit-interview file: shape, every field, catalogue values | §15.3 |\n916\t| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback questions | §15.3 |\n917\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"601fafe8-2344-49f9-95de-5b839f33c702","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:56:42.860Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 9.3 Audio: code only, no audio files\n\n- **Music at 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested. Verified: it holds from before the first gesture (`locked`) through `running`, and the mixer reports 0.1. Stored volume wins after the player changes it.\n- **Songs (`defineSong`, original, 3–5):** sparse desert-blues over plucked pentatonic arpeggios and hand drums: Title, Roam (low and tense, intensity layers), Combat\n  (`audio.intensity` by alert state), Fever (detuned, slow), Ending. Nothing that echoes the source's score.\n- **SFX (`defineSfx`):** each gun class with distance tail (`sound` on the weapon), dry click, jam rack, reload parts, machete, grenade, rocket, flare, fire crackle in three sizes, flare-up whoosh,\n  barrel explosion, cook-off pops, engines by rpm (pitch on a loop), gears, skid, wrench, car door, boat, footsteps by surface (`SURFACE_STEPS`, add grass rustle), wind by speed, insects day\n  and night, thunder, rain, tick-box click, tape hiss and clunk, syrette, pill rattle, surgery wince, heartbeat, UI cues.\n- **Voices (`sounds/voice.ts`):** Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Turn each line's text into syllables by hashing its words; play them in sequence on the `ui` bus with per-character `pitch` while the subtitle\n  types out; NPC barks use positional syllable bursts. Never real words. A syllable per vowel (verified: renders 0.1 s at a peak near 0.2, unclipped): `defineSfx('sfx:voice-a', { voices: [{ wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.5,\n  attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F1, q:5 } }, { wave:'saw', freq:170, to:150, dur:0.1, vol:0.35, attack:0.008, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:F2, q:7 } }, { wave:'noise', dur:0.015, vol:0.05, filter:{ type:'bandpass', freq:2600, q:2 } }], variants:3, vary:0.05 })`, with (F1, F2) a 730, 1090; e 530, 1840; i 300, 2200; o 570, 840; u 320, 800.\n- **Mix:** `room` reverb for interiors, `duck` during dialogue, `update(pose, queries)` each frame for 3D and occlusion. Settings sliders: Master, Music, Effects, Voices, Ambience.\n\n## 10. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n\n### 10.1 Controls\n\nEvery device gets the same game on the same screens; no desktop layout. Own action maps (`defineActionMap`, `look:true, aimWith:'centre', lock:true` for first person; a second for vehicles). Bindings rebindable with `input.listen`.\n\n| Action | DualSense | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move / Look | L stick / R stick | WASD / mouse |\n| Fire / Aim | R2 / L2 | LMB / RMB |\n| Jump, mantle | Cross | Space |\n| Crouch | Circle | C (never Ctrl: Ctrl+W closes the tab) |\n| Sprint | L3 | Left Shift |\n| Use (tap), Reload and clear jam (hold) | Square, `Hold:PadWest` | E, R |\n| Next weapon / holster (hold) | Triangle | 1 2 3, wheel / X |\n| Melee | R3 | V |\n| Throw | R1 | G |\n| Syrette (surgery when critical) | L1 | H |\n| Map / Tick-box / Pills / Fire-gun quick-draw | D-pad up, down, left, right | M / B / P / F |\n| Ledger | Create | L |\n| Camera view toggle | Hold Create | T |\n| Pause | Options | Esc |\n| Drive: throttle, brake, steer, handbrake, exit, seat | R2, L2, L stick, Cross, Square, Triangle | W, S, A D, Space, E, Q |\n\nIn a hub session the game ignores Options while Create is held. Verified with a stubbed DualSense: a tap on Square fires only `interact` and a hold only `reload`, and Create likewise splits `ledger` and `view`, with prompts reading `Square` and `Hold Square`.\n\n**Mouse is native:** pointer lock in play; in menus hover moves focus and click picks (`createMenu`; custom panels too, `createPadCursor` for pad). **Things in the world:** under lock, the crosshair is the\npointer: looking at something usable highlights it with a world-anchored label, and with no weapon out a left click uses it, as well as E.\n\n**Prompts follow the last input.** `ui/glyphs.ts`: inline SVG for PlayStation shapes (✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1 L2 R2 L3 R3 Options Create, D-pad) and Xbox (A B X Y LB RB LT RT LS RS Menu View). Pick by `input.lastDevice`: `gamepad` → PlayStation\nglyphs for any pad, or Xbox if Settings says so; keyboard → key caps and mouse icons with the actual binding (`input.bindingsFor`, `codeLabel`, `readKeyboardLayout`). Every prompt element carries `data-glyph` for tests, and they swap at once when the device changes. Verified: with a\nstubbed DualSense, `input.lastDevice` turns `gamepad`, `input.held`/`strength` work, and `input.prompt('jump')` is `Cross`.\n\n### 10.2 First person without a closed door\n\nThe default camera is `cam3=first`. For **body moments** (surgery, syrette on critical, repair, rescue from the ground) blend (`__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`) to custom `cam3=body` (`defineCamera`, `followCamera`, `makePose`):\nat the eye about 0.1 m forward, pitch about −50°, body drawn whole because only `first` hides it. **Verified:** this shows the player's own sleeves, hands and gun in frame during a `defineAction`; framing needs tuning (holster the gun, arms farther out). The head's back faces don't block\nit. `cam3=driver` is the same idea with a level pitch at the driver's eye. The world keeps running throughout.\n\nWith the gun holstered, the `first` rig shows raised fists. In the ceasefire town, use a level `cam3=walk` of the same kind (eye height 1.52 m, 0.16 m forward, pitch 0), which keeps the hands out of frame. Verified: it turns with the stick (yaw −= `input.look[0]` + `input.turn[0]` × 2.5 × dt, pitch likewise), and the action map's `look` follows it, so moving forward goes where it faces.\n\n### 10.3 Screens and HUD\n\n**10-foot rules:** type scales with `vh`; body text ≥ 3.2vh (34 px at 1080p), headings 6vh, subtitles 3.4vh on a dark band; 5% safe margins; one decision a screen; at most 7 visible list items; no tables of data: the dealer and ledger show one thing large, stepped with\nleft/right. System font stacks only (stencil-ish headings via `Impact, \"Arial Narrow\"`, typed notes via `\"Courier New\"`); no font files. Visual language: field notebook, stencil, carbon-copy paper, rust.\n\n**Teaching what you can and can't do.** Each mechanic gets a one-time contextual hint when first met, and a short line on the Ledger's **Field notes** page from then on, limits included: guns can't be repaired, only replaced; pills can't be bought; nobody swims; there's no fast travel; a claimed safehouse stays yours and a checkpoint doesn't.\n\n**HUD (first person):** five syrette-shaped health segments, ammo and gun condition as a small rust bar, syrette and pill counts only when they change or are low; centre dot crosshair (none down the sights); goal line for 5 s when it changes; wind ribbon as per §6.5; damage arcs; prompts near centre-bottom. No\nminimap, ever.\n\n**Map in hand (`ui/map.ts`), the world doesn't pause.** A paper map on a CSS 3D layer (`perspective`, `rotateX`, slides up with an arm-raise ease, sways with the camera's turn), SVG hands gripping its edges in the merc's skin tone. Contour lines baked once to a canvas from `terrain.heights`; places as ink labels once discovered; roads, river; burn scars as hatch from fire state; an arrow for you; goal marker;\nwind arrow. R stick or pointer moves a pencil cursor, Cross or click drops a waypoint (a world-anchored marker). You walk at half speed with it up, and you can drive with it up (it sits low and to the right). The tick-box shows beside it as a small device with lamp and needle.\n\n**Screens:** Title (Continue, New Game, Co-op, Settings, Controls, About); merc pick; Pause (Resume, Map, Ledger, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About, Quit to title); Settings; Controls with rebinding (a picture of the pad and a short list); Dialogue (subtitle band with portrait name; choices as a short `createMenu`); Sleep (dawn, noon, dusk); Dealer; Ledger (a page each: debts,\nfactions, tapes, stones, field notes); Co-op lobby; Game over; Ending; Credits.\n\n**Settings:** Master, Music (10%), Effects, Voices, Ambience; Glyphs (PlayStation / Xbox); Camera (first / shoulder); FOV (`cam.fov`); mouse and stick sensitivity and invert (`input.*`), aim-assist-free; Crouch hold/toggle; Fever intensity; Screen shake; Subtitle size; Quality (`quality`); Fullscreen (`requestFullscreen`). Keep with `createStore`.\n\n### 10.4 Touch-only devices\n\nIf `matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)')` and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (`?touch=off` in `search`, no `touchLayout`; verified on Playwright's Pixel 7 profile, where the query is true, WebGPU runs, and a tap wakes no touch UI). Show a full-screen message exactly **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** and a **Settings** button over the attract mode, which keeps running. A pad press or key press dismisses it for good. Test with Playwright's phone profile.\n\nIf WebGPU is missing on any device, show a plain message naming a browser that has it; the engine has no WebGL fallback, and the sim still runs.\n\n## 11. Title, attract mode, dev mode, About\n\n### 11.1 Title\n\n`game.mode=title`: slow camera over the savanna at dusk with a fire line on the horizon (`cam3=rail`, or `cam3=fixed,x,y,z,yaw,pitch` in metres and degrees; keep it above the ground, since below it the picture draws white), title lettering in CSS, Title song. Restarting by mode is verified: `__engine.set('game.mode', 'b')`, `__engine.seed(n)`, and the scene comes up with the setting kept. The view lights and shadows round the player actor, so park it near the camera's target, out of frame. Starting play sets the mode and calls `inspector.seed(n)`.\n\n### 11.2 Attract mode\n\nAfter **10 s without input** on the title or any menu off the title (keys, mouse move or click, pad buttons or sticks past deadzone all reset the timer), switch to `game.mode=attract` and restart. A page-side director (`attract.ts`) plays a loop of vignettes by placing the bot with dev actions and feeding `inspector.step(1, botIntents)` on a paused clock, with cheats on (`game.god`, infinite ammo; teleporting\nand scripted fires are fine). Each runs 15–25 s with a banner: title + \"Press any button\":\n\n1. Chase camera: a technical tearing down the road at dusk. 2. First person: firefight at Km-40. 3. Flare into grass; wind carries it; soldiers panic. 4. A jam at the worst moment, cleared. 5. Fever attack with mirages, pill. 6. Down, buddy rescue, ledger line. 7. Map in hand and tick-box, finding a tin box. 8. Boat with mounted gun past the burning estate. 9. Dealer and tape caption, then loop.\n\nCamera and look change per vignette (`__engine.camera.set`). **The bot steers the first-person camera through `createEngine({ cameraInput: () => botView ? { view: [yaw, pitch] } : undefined })`** (verified, on a paused clock; cast it, since `view` isn't in the `CameraInput` type). `look` intents alone turn the actor but not that camera. **Any input exits at once** to the title (restart). It runs on touch-only devices too, and off-line: nothing is fetched. Never start it when automation should test the title unless asked: a setting `game.attract=auto|off|on` for tests.\n\n### 11.3 Dev mode: Pause → Dev Mode (not in hub sessions)\n\nPad and mouse friendly, big rows, one category at a time:\n\n- **Balance:** live sliders on `scenes/settings.ts`, each with its value and a per-row and global **Reset to defaults**: wear per shot, jam multiplier, player damage, enemy damage, enemy accuracy, reaction, sight, hearing, attackers, respawn hours; fire spread, wind bias, burn time, fire damage, regrow; wind speed, shift interval; fever interval, severity; HP, regen, syrette heal; car engine, grip, spring, damper; prices, stone payouts; day length; `movement.*`. Settings use `when:'now'` and systems read them at each use. `__engine.set` applies a value; reset writes each default from the table back the same way.\n- **Cheats:** god, infinite ammo, no wear/jam, no fever, invisible to AI, noclip, one-shot kills.\n- **Teleport:** every place, the crosshair point, a chosen map point.\n- **Spawn:** any gun with chosen condition, vehicle, squad, buddy, tin box.\n- **World:** hour, time scale (`time.scale`), wind dial, rain, ignite at crosshair, burn all, regrow all, fever now.\n- **Story:** jump to any contract or step, set flags, debts, standing, give stones.\n- **Debug overlays:** fire grid, AI state labels (`engine.ui.label`), detection meters, nav paths; `__engine.stats()` numbers; digest; quick save/load.\n\nSliders survive across resume, not across New Game unless \"Keep\" is on.\n\n### 11.4 About/Credits\n\nReachable from title and pause; pad and mouse scrollable pages. Pages: Source Information (all fields in TASK.md), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, Credits (engine, three.js, Rapier, @stdlib, motion clips per `data/anim/SOURCES.md`, source note: \"inspired by Far Cry 2, not affiliated\"). **One source of truth:** `scripts/bundle.mjs` reads the exit-interview file and injects it as data, so the screen and the file can't differ. Usage reads literally \"added on import\".\n\n## 12. Multiplayer\n\n**Decision: `shared-world`, host-sequenced deterministic lockstep.** The engine's one superpower is bit-exact determinism, so instead of replicating fire cells, ragdolls and NPCs, every copy runs the same scene from the same seed, and the host sequences everyone's input. No per-entity networking code.\n\n**Verified end to end:** a probe with this recipe, the tags below and the game's own card passes the real harness in all four of its configurations (§13). In the browser, copies computed identical digests at the same step despite 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and the `server` copy ran `createHeadless` with no WebGPU. In Node, 2 and 4 seats plus a server copy, each with its own `game.me`, kept identical digests over 3,600 steps with moving seats, an NPC, a jeep and a spreading fire.\n\n### 12.1 The mode: **Contract Run** (`game.mode=contract`)\n\n1–4 mercs, about 5 minutes, in the Pump Station region: reach the depot, burn it (flare, sprayer or charges at 3 points, all driven by wind), survive the counter-attack, get out by the river boat or pickups. Revive teammates (hold use 3 s; bleed-out 40 s). Friendly fire and friendly fire-fire are on. All down, or time up without getting out: team loses. `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })`: score = objective 500 + kills 50 + rescues 150 + extracted 200 − downs 100 − teammates burned 200; awards like \"Best employee of the wind\". The hub deals by these tags:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Burn the depot, then get out alive together\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick or WASD moves, R2 or click fires, Square or E uses\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">\n```\n\nSolo in-hub (1 seat) still works. Names and colours come from `info.players`; set names with `textContent`, armbands by seat colour. No chat, emotes or custom names, no `alert`, no new tabs, no menus before the round, and pause opens only Settings without stopping the sim. Quit does nothing.\n\n### 12.2 The session flow (order matters)\n\n1. `session.js` is the first script after `<meta charset>`, unmodified (bundler does this).\n2. In `main.ts`, first thing: `if (AIHub.active) info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. `null` means play on as standalone.\n3. Start: `createEngine` with `search: ?seed=${info.seed}&game.mode=contract&game.seats=${n}&game.me=${info.me}&cam3=first&gfx.warmUp=asked`. **`gfx.warmUp=asked` is required:** with the default `all`, four copies sharing one main thread blocked past the deadlines. On a software adapter also lower `game.detail` (one fixed value across copies; pass through the host if adapters differ, or simply use a fixed contract-mode density).\n4. **Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the sim exists, not when the first frame draws.** Poll `typeof window.__engine?.step === 'function'` every 20 ms (reached in about 1 s); then `__engine.pause()`, start the pump, `ready()`. The harness allows all copies 20 s, and rendering took 5–25 s on software WebGPU with 4 frames; the view catches up by itself.\n5. **The pump:** a 16 ms `setInterval` alone passes the harness (verified); once frames flow, step from `onFrame` for smooth motion, and keep the timer for when frames stop. All copies run on the paused clock through `inspector.step`. **While paused, the camera still turns with mouse and stick** (verified: look is view state), so aim feels immediate on every copy.\n6. `AIHub.server`: no engine. `await createHeadless({ scene, seed, settings: { 'game.seats': n, 'game.me': -1, 'game.mode': 'contract' } })`, pump on the timer, call `finish`. If `navigator.gpu` is missing on a playing copy: `AIHub.fail('WebGPU is required')`.\n7. `time-up` → host `finish` at once from its handler. `end` → stop input, stop sound (`audio.mute(true)`), stop stepping, send nothing. Host leaving voids the round by protocol; do nothing special.\n\n### 12.3 The lockstep core (`net/lockstep.ts`)\n\n```ts\nconst TURN = 4;                         // steps a turn: 15 messages a second, under the hub's 20\n// Local wire, rebuilt each frame in onFrame from engine.input (held/strength), frame.camera (yaw, pitch) and an aim ray:\n//   { m:[x,z], l:[yaw,pitch], a:[x,y,z], b:[...buttons pressed or held since last sent] }   rounded to 3 decimals\n// client, every 66 ms:  net.send({ k:'i', w: wire }, { to: host, reliable: true })\n// host, every 66 ms, ONE message however many turns are due (a burst per turn breaks the rate limit; verified):\n//   const due = Math.floor(net.time() / (1000 / 60));\n//   const n = Math.min(Math.floor((due - sent) / TURN), 30); if (n > 0) {\n//     latest[me] = wire; const f = { k:'f', s: sent, n, i: { ...latest }, x: seatEvents };\n//     net.send(f, { reliable: true }); queue.push(f); sent += TURN * n; }\n// every copy: for each frame in order, for each of TURN*n steps, when due:\n//   engine.step(1, { ...toIntents(f.i[me]), 'game:seats': othersAsIntents(f.i, me), ...(first ? { 'game:events': f.x } : {}) });\n// Playout: run max(1, dt*60) steps a frame while frames wait, up to 12 when far behind, so motion is smooth at 60 Hz.\n```\n\n**Rules that keep copies identical:**\n\n- **Everything the sim sees comes through the host's frames.** A `leave` event, a pick in a dialogue, a revive: all are host-sequenced (`x`, `game:events`, or the wire). Never feed a local `leave` straight to the sim: copies hear it at different times.\n- `game.me` differs per copy, so `actor.player` differs, so **`engine.hash()` differs across copies by design**. Compare `digest.ts` (seats, NPCs, vehicles' positions and HP, fire state counts, as a string) instead. Clients send `{ k:'d', s, h }` every 2 s; the host logs mismatches and shows a small \"out of sync\" note; tests fail on it. No resync: `capture()` is far too big, and determinism holds by construction.\n- Keep sim settings identical across copies (from the host via URL), dev mode off, and sanitize wire data (finite numbers, known buttons) before it reaches intents: `normalizeIntents` throws on junk.\n- A tap shorter than a turn must survive: OR presses into `b` until the wire is sent.\n\n### 12.4 The game on its own\n\nOutside the hub, Title → Co-op runs the same Contract Run with no server, through the same `Net` interface (`send`, `on('message')`, `time`, `players`, `isHost`):\n\n- **`net/tabs.ts`: \"Same computer\":** `BroadcastChannel`; host lists itself, others join; a second tab, window or screen. Verified from `file://` between two pages.\n- **`net/rtc.ts`: \"Invite code\":** one `RTCPeerConnection` and ordered data channel per guest (star on the host), non-trickle ICE, `iceServers:[{urls:'stun:stun.l.google.com:19302'}]`, base64 of the description as a copy-and-paste code with a Copy button and a Paste field: host's code to guest, guest's reply to host. Verified from `file://` in two separate browser contexts with no STUN: connects in 0.3 s with 650-byte descriptions.\n\nUse the hub's seat colours and a local 3 s countdown. Rule: none of this runs while `AIHub.active` (network only through the hub).\n\n### 12.5 Own card\n\nBecause the game has its own world, use `card: true`: HTML/CSS card over the loading scene showing the game's name, players in colours with ready ticks, how to win, this player's controls, time left from the `card` event. Enter, Cross or a click: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle, a named key or a click: `AIHub.answer('more')`. No menus; nothing changes the round. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, after `start`. The harness tests this by dispatching an Enter `keydown` in the frame, so listen on `window` (verified: its card check passes in all four configurations). Remove the card on `start`.\n\n## 13. Testing\n\n**One command:** `node tests/game/verify.mjs [sim|page|hub]` (add `\"verify\"` to `package.json`). It prints only failures and a final one-line count (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Vitest with `--reporter=json` and parsed. Rerun a tier only after changing what it covers. Screenshots only for what a script can't judge; save them in `out/`. Keep one or two browsers open at a time. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n| Tier | What it proves |\n|---|---|\n| **sim** (Vitest, Node; seconds to minutes) | Starter's four kinds kept: golden replay (1), capture/restore, bot, rules. Wear lowers condition and swaps tier ids; jam rate at given conditions over many shots; clearing works. HP, regen, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, debt, overdraw. Fever timeline, pills, collapse. **Fire front metric** (§6.5), firebreaks hold, embers jump, rain stops it, regrowth. Wind shifts on schedule with forecast. Stealth: same patrol detects standing in the open and misses crouched in grass; burned grass exposes. AI squad clears cover and flanks; jam window. Checkpoint repopulation; holstered pass with standing. Vehicle: drives a route, stays on terrain, repairs, explodes; `placeActor` seat; run-over. Economy purchases, caches, armoury. Save → reload → same state fields. Contract engine: every contract and alt completes by script. **Lockstep digest:** 1, 2, 4 headless copies with different `game.me` fed the same frames keep equal digests over 3,600 steps with a fight, a fire and a car in them. |\n| **Whole-game playthrough** (sim) | `bot.ts` completes prologue through every ending on seeds 1–3 with `game.god`, through intents only, using teleport-free travel for contract 1 and dev travel after. One honest run: bot clears Km-40 without god on at least 7 of 10 seeds, as a balance alarm. **Feature coverage:** the main path must fire each of these at least once, proving the demo is long enough to show everything: jam, clear, gun break, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, mercy prompt, buddy death, fever warning, attack, pill, mirage, ignite, wind shift, ember jump, scorch, regrowth, drive, repair, vehicle explosion, boat, mounted gun, ropeway, stealth pass, holstered pass, purchase, reliability upgrade, armoury reissue, tin box, tape, sleep, save and load, standing change, alternate plan, every ending. |\n| **page** (Playwright on the deliverable over `file://`) | Boots `ready` with no errors. Title → attract at 10 s; any key, mouse or pad input exits it (virtual clock not needed; use `game.attract` and a short setting for the wait, and one real 10 s test). Keyboard-only and pad-only (stubbed `navigator.getGamepads`) routes from title to first control. Prompts swap device on input; Xbox option; `data-glyph` values. Music bus reads 0.1 at first run; volume persists. `renderSong`, `renderSfx` and an `OfflineAudioContext` run of a voice line produce non-silent, unclipped audio (`node x qa audio`). Pause and Dev Mode: a slider changes behaviour, reset restores. Map opens without pausing the tick count. Mouse hover moves menu focus. About matches the exit-interview file. Phone profile shows the exact touch message, Settings button, no touch controls, attract running. WebKit and Firefox have no WebGPU here, so Chromium only; say so. |\n| **hub** | Fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` to `.cache/hub/`; copy the deliverable beside them; serve on an OS-picked 127.0.0.1 port; confirm the page marker. All must give `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` (`document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` when finished): `players=2`; `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; the same with `scenario=host-leave`; `players=2&scenario=server`; all with `&auto=1`. Launch args include the WebGPU flags of §2. Then standalone: same deliverable alone, solo; two pages over tabs link; two contexts over invite codes, digests equal. |\n| **look** (rare) | `node x shot` numbers and `node x gallery prop` for art; 1920×1080 screenshots of each UI screen for the 10-foot judgement. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot; check state instead when it does. |\n\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (never required for real): no controller, so replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub returning a standard-mapping pad via `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`; no touch device, so use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles and tap with `page.touchscreen`; nobody to listen, so check sound by what Web Audio produces, through an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` levels; no second player, so play multiplayer with two pages or contexts; no real iPhone or iPad, so use WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest there is, though it has no WebGPU here; no TV, so judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n**Not checkable here; say so in your feedback:** real GPUs, real pads, speakers, cross-machine determinism, iOS.\n\n## 14. Build order\n\nBuild a walking skeleton first, then keep the deliverable working at every step. Run `npm run check` and the relevant `verify` tier before moving on.\n\n| # | Do | Done when |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | §2 setup. Pick the title by §0.1's procedure, into `title.ts`. Write `scripts/bundle.mjs` (§15.1), `fetch-hub.mjs`, `verify.mjs` skeleton. Add hub metas and a stub session | One-file loads over `file://`; harness passes with the starter + stub |\n| 1 | Site, terrain, river, roads, scatter, looks, day cycle; `movement:merc`; `seats.ts`; action maps; first person; HUD shell | Walk the map in first person; prompts follow device |\n| 2 | `arms.ts`, wear and jam, `health.ts`, one gun, Rifleman, perception, Km-40. **Now `lockstep.ts` + digest test + `net/hub.ts`**, so co-op stays honest from here | Bot clears Km-40; digests equal; harness passes with guns |\n| 3 | Fire, wind, flare, grass burning, scars, panic | Fire metric test; looks like fire in a shot |\n| 4 | Vehicles, driver AI, patrols, repair, boat, mounted guns | Bot drives a route; vehicle tests |\n| 5 | Fever, buddies, ledger, stones, tick-box, dealer, safehouses, saves, rest of the guns and enemy types | Tier tests green |\n| 6 | Contract engine, all story content, dialogue, voices, tapes, map in hand, endings | Playthrough bot finishes the game |\n| 7 | Contract Run, own card, tabs and invite links | Full hub tier green |\n| 8 | Title, pause, settings, controls, dev mode, attract, touch notice, About | Page tier green |\n| 9 | Art, animation, audio passes across everything; balance by sliders and bot | No placeholder boxes or stand-ins anywhere; a search for TODO, FIXME, placeholder finds nothing |\n| 10 | Finish: §15 | All below |\n\n## 15. Finish line\n\n### 15.1 Packaging: `scripts/bundle.mjs` (verified: 6.7 MB, loads from `file://`, harness passes)\n\n```js\nimport { build } from 'vite'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { join } from 'node:path';\nconst root = process.cwd();\nconst result = await build({ root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',\n  resolve: { alias: [{ find: /^three$/, replacement: 'three/webgpu' }] },\n  build: { write: false, modulePreload: false, cssCodeSplit: false, assetsInlineLimit: () => true, chunkSizeWarningLimit: Infinity,\n    rollupOptions: { input: { game: join(root, 'labs/game/index.html') }, output: { inlineDynamicImports: true } } } });\nconst files = {}; let html;\nfor (const item of [result].flat().flatMap((o) => o.output)) {\n  const text = item.type === 'chunk' ? item.code : typeof item.source === 'string' ? item.source : new TextDecoder().decode(item.source);\n  if (item.fileName.endsWith('.html')) html = text; else files[item.fileName] = text; }\nconst find = (ref) => files[Object.keys(files).find((f) => ref.endsWith('/' + f) || ref === f || ref === './' + f)];\nhtml = html.replace(/<script type=\"module\" crossorigin src=\"([^\"]+)\"><\\/script>|<link rel=\"stylesheet\" crossorigin href=\"([^\"]+)\">/g, (_t, s, c) => {\n  if (c) return `<style>${find(c)}</style>`;\n  const code = find(s).replaceAll('</script', '<\\\\/script');\n  if (code.includes('<!--')) throw new Error('script holds <!--: split it in the source');\n  return `<script type=\"module\">${code}</script>`; });\nconst session = readFileSync('.cache/hub/session.js', 'utf8'); // the unmodified client: scripts/fetch-hub.mjs downloads it\nhtml = html.replace('<meta charset=\"utf-8\" />', () => `<meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\\n<script>${session}</script>\\n${HUB_METAS}`);\nwriteFileSync(DELIVERABLE, html); // DELIVERABLE: the name your TASK.md gives, in '..'; inject the exit-interview file as data too\n```\n\n`fetch-hub.mjs` downloads `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/` into `.cache/hub/`, where lint and format can't touch them: the client must stay byte-identical. The protocol's full text is `README.md` at the same address; read it once. Everything in the deliverable is inline, so nothing depends on a CDN.\n\n### 15.2 Your own feedback\n\nKeep `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` current as AGENTS.md asks. Starting points, if they bite you too: arms rig closed to games; weapons, melee and use only for the `player` actor; no scene access for game meshes (raw geometry through `.constructor` is a hack); TASK.md's browser flags vs the engine's; `capture` size; `setup.sh` downloads outside the folder; one scene per page. Don't patch the engine (it stays read-only); `npm run check` proves it. Keep `labs/game/README.md` true: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n\n### 15.3 Exit interview\n\n**Critical, from the brief:** put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply **and** on the About/Credits screen, writing \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Say plainly what you did not verify.\n\n| Source Information | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Date | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\n| Source | Far Cry 2 |\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given: `PLAN.md` |\n| Genre and Perspective | Open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure; first person, with shoulder and chase options and body-camera moments |\n| Tech | my-3dge at the commit `engine-lock.json` names; TypeScript; three.js r182 on WebGPU; Rapier 0.21; HTML/CSS UI; Vite, Vitest, Playwright; what else you add, with versions |\n| Art Direction | The engine's native low-poly style, sun-bleached, with new procedural art (§9.1) |\n| Animation | The engine's animation system (procedural humanoid, actions, library clips) with new actions and flipbook guns |\n| Audio | All in code: tracker songs and synthesized effects through the engine's audio; Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (the client version you bundled), shared-world host-sequenced lockstep; on its own, a same-computer tab link and WebRTC invite codes |\n| Testing | The testing parameters allowed: from your TASK.md, as you used them |\n| Research | Engine and hub source reading and this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did, or none |\n| Environment, Model | Exactly as your TASK.md gives them |\n| Usage | Write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records it, and the import puts it on the About screen |\n\n**The exit-interview file,** in exactly this shape, every value filled in as plain text (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm), checked with `JSON.parse`. Tools read it. Rebuild afterwards so About shows it.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor this game: type `Game`, genre `Shooter`, source `Far Cry 2`, series `Far Cry`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d, first-person, co-op, vehicles, exploration, low-poly`. The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked here.\n\n- **Testing Feedback:** How many issues did testing catch? How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking? What was the most helpful test you ran? What was the least helpful test you ran?\n- **Engine Feedback:** What did you like most about the engine? Did you get stuck or guess wrong? What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? What was the hardest part about using the engine? What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing) If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n- **Prompt Feedback:** What did you like most about this prompt? What was confusing? What was the hardest thing to do? What would you change in this prompt?\n\n### 15.4 Coverage: TASK.md → this plan\n\nEvery line below is stated in this plan at the section named, not only referred to.\n\n| TASK.md item | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Source: Far Cry 2; original game inspired by it | §0.1, §1 |\n| Genre, perspective | §1 |\n| Title: riff, 50 brainstormed silently, real RNG | §0.1, §14 step 0 |\n| Research: your choice, reported | §0.1, §15.3 |\n| Single HTML in root, modules in a subfolder, CDN allowed | §0.1, §15.1 |\n| Full workspace; engine read-only; systems, art and animations on top; rest of stack free | §0.1, §2, §3, §15.2 |\n| Art direction | §0.1, §9.1 |\n| Animation | §0.1, §9.2, §3 wall 3, §10.2 |\n| Audio: code only, gibberish voices, music 10% | §0.1, §9.3, §13 page tier |\n| Online and multiplayer: hub and on its own | §0.1, §12 |\n| Shared machine: files, reads, ports, PIDs, browsers, Python, memory | §0.1, §2 |\n| Performance unlimited, FPS and optimization out of scope | §0.1 |\n| Testing: headless, one command, failures and count, playthrough, screenshots rarely, no debate agents | §0.1, §13 |\n| Stand-ins: pad, touch, audio, second player, iPhone, 1080p | §13 |\n| Feature-complete demo; long enough to show all features; what you can and can't do | §0.1, §8.4, §10.3, §13 feature coverage |\n| World, characters, themes, tone | §1, §5, §8 |\n| Infrastructure for later content with normal tools | §4, §6.10 |\n| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | §11.3 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, looping, cheats, any input exits | §11.2 |\n| Narrative tone | §8 |\n| 10-foot, DualSense or any standard-mapping pad; native keyboard and mouse on the same screens; hover focus, world clicks; prompts by last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §10 |\n| Touch-only: message, settings button, attract runs | §10.4 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §0, §0.1, §14 step 9 |\n| Source Information, in chat and About; every field | §11.4, §15.3 |\n| Exit-interview file: shape, every field, catalogue values | §15.3 |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback questions | §15.3 |\n","numLines":328,"startLine":590,"totalLines":917}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JfjkqnA4joe4mpA6PPGm6s","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
