{"type":"system","subtype":"thinking_tokens","estimated_tokens":243,"estimated_tokens_delta":175,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"3d056345-f7b9-4efb-8222-0e373fbd789a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjGaNy1hxTK26wi2n6Ym","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The engine change only affects camera wall cut-away, so the plan is unaffected, though I'll note the commit difference. The screenshot also shows the WebGPU-missing notice needs an opaque background, and I'm now closing the gaps from the read-through.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5445,"cache_read_input_tokens":774038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5445},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"699d12f4-ebf6-48ea-951f-06a089d96aa5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:59:27.747Z","request_id":"req_011CgjGaNdvADXUYP65zP7xk","narration_block_indexes":[0],"thinking_duration_ms":2,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjGaNy1hxTK26wi2n6Ym","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018pJe6iZz21PLYnUkrmBvg4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf8').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, 'MISSING: '+a[:90]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# What the plan is and isn't.\nrep('''## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **The design is decided.**''','''## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **This is the plan, not the game.** Building the game, choosing its title, making the deliverable and writing the exit-interview file are the builder's job, using §0.1, §14 and §15. Nothing here has been built except throwaway probes.\n- **The design is decided.**''')\nrep('''1.4.0, with throwaway probes on software WebGPU. If a verified recipe fails for you, look for what changed; don't redesign around it. What couldn't be checked here is listed in §13.''','''1.4.0, with throwaway probes on software WebGPU. The engine's `main` moved once while this was written (`2dbd829`, a change to how building walls cut away for cameras, touching nothing here), and `new-game` pins whatever you get in `engine-lock.json`. If a verified recipe fails for you, look for what changed; don't redesign around it. What couldn't be checked here is listed in §13.''')\n\n# Node Playwright vs the version note in TASK.md.\nrep('''- **`CHROMIUM_PATH`** (above) makes `node x shot`, `x film`, `x inspect` and `x replay --browser` work. Node 24.22.1 is\n  fine. In Node drivers: `chromium.launch({ executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH, args })` from `@playwright/test`.''','''- **`CHROMIUM_PATH`** (above) makes `node x shot`, `x film`, `x inspect` and `x replay --browser` work. Node 24.22.1 is\n  fine. In Node drivers: `chromium.launch({ executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH, args })` from `@playwright/test`. The workspace pins 1.64.1, not the 1.63.0 the browsers were downloaded for, so always pass\n  `executablePath`; then nothing downloads, and you don't need `npm install playwright@1.63.0`. The system Python's Playwright 1.63.0 also works with no path. Both verified. Leave `PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE` as it is set.''')\n\n# Designer tools: proved, not asserted.\nrep('''**Data-driven so designers can extend it:** places, squads, contracts, dealer stock and weapons are registry kinds or\ntables with schemas. Adding a contract or a checkpoint takes no new code.''','''**Data-driven so designers can extend it:** places, squads, contracts, dealer stock and weapons are registry kinds or\ntables with schemas; all text is data; levels are text maps; props, materials and sounds are data entries; dev mode tunes and jumps to anything. Adding a contract or a checkpoint takes no new code, and a test proves it (§13).''')\n\n# Pacing.\nrep('''After an ending: credits, then the world stays open for free roam from the title's Continue.''','''**Pacing.** Quiet and violence alternate: a tense drive or stalk of 1–3 minutes, then a fight of about 1 minute that ends in a fire, a jam or a rescue, then a breath at a safehouse, in town or on the river. Music follows by intensity and drops to wind and insects between fights. Each new mechanic arrives in a low-stakes moment before a contract leans on it, in the order of the Teaches column. Attacks of fever and wind shifts are what break up routine, so a safe road never stays safe.\n\nAfter an ending: credits, then the world stays open for free roam from the title's Continue.''')\n\n# Polish list.\nrep('''## 10. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n\n### 10.1 Controls\n\nEvery device gets the same game on the same screens; no desktop layout.''','''### 9.4 Polish: what \"complete and polished\" means here\n\nDo this pass before calling anything done: hit confirmation by sound and a small marker; muzzle light and flash, shell casings, dust puffs by surface; camera shake scaled by distance, with its setting; ragdolls on every death; tyre dust, suspension travel and engine note by speed; screen transitions eased, never popped; a loading screen with a line of field advice; subtitles timed to their voices; pause fading the world without stopping its sound abruptly; no stand-in boxes, no default engine colours or body on anything that matters, and no silent action anywhere.\n\n## 10. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n\nDesign for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping, played on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse get the same game on the same screens, and must feel native rather than like stand-ins for controller buttons: mouse look is direct under pointer lock with no stick imitation or acceleration, number keys and the wheel pick weapons outright, menus and the map follow the pointer, and keys are the ones PC players expect.\n\n### 10.1 Controls\n\nEvery device gets the same game on the same screens; no desktop layout.''')\n\n# Prompts: own glyphs for pads.\nrep('''Every prompt element carries `data-glyph` for tests, and they swap at once when the device changes.''','''Don't use `input.prompt` for pads: it names buttons by the pad's brand (verified: `A` on a pad reporting as Xbox, `Cross` on a DualSense), while the brief wants PlayStation glyphs by default on any pad. Every prompt element carries `data-glyph` for tests, and they swap at once when the device changes.''')\n\n# Aim assist.\nrep('''**Prompts follow the last input.** `ui/glyphs.ts`:''','''**Pad aim assist.** A shooter on a TV needs it. Pad only, on by default, a Settings toggle and a dev slider: while the stick is moving, turn slower near an enemy's chest and pull gently toward it, and snap about 2° when the sights come up. It's camera input, never sim: `cameraInput: () => ({ look: [right, up] })` adds to the stick's turn (verified: the two combine), and the aim that results goes out as the wire's look, so co-op stays in step.\n\n**Prompts follow the last input.** `ui/glyphs.ts`:''')\nrep('''mouse and stick sensitivity and invert (`input.*`), aim-assist-free; Crouch hold/toggle;''','''mouse and stick sensitivity and invert (`input.*`); pad aim assist; Crouch hold/toggle;''')\n\n# Touch notice before the engine, with the WebKit fact.\nrep('''Show a full-screen message exactly **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** and a **Settings** button over the attract mode, which keeps running. A pad press or key press dismisses it for good. Test with Playwright's phone profile.\n\nIf WebGPU is missing on any device, show a plain message naming a browser that has it; the engine has no WebGL fallback, and the sim still runs.''','''Show a full-screen message exactly **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** and a **Settings** button over the attract mode, which keeps running. A pad press or key press dismisses it for good.\n\n**Build the notice in plain HTML before `createEngine` starts,** so it never depends on the renderer. Verified both ways: on Chromium's Pixel 7 profile it sits on top of a running engine, and on WebKit's iPhone profile, which has no WebGPU here, it still shows. There the attract mode can't draw and the engine's own error text shows through, so make the notice's background opaque when `navigator.gpu` is missing.\n\nIf WebGPU is missing on any other device, show a plain message naming a browser that has it; the engine has no WebGL fallback, and the sim still runs.''')\n\n# Attract's job.\nrep('''Each runs 15–25 s with a banner: title + \"Press any button\":''','''Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible; it plays no part in balance. It's arcade-style and may cheat freely: infinite health and ammo, no clip. Each vignette runs 15–25 s with a banner: title + \"Press any button\":''')\n\n# Card pad input.\nrep('''Enter, Cross or a click: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle, a named key or a click: `AIHub.answer('more')`.''','''Enter, Cross (button 0) or a click: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle (button 3), a named key or a click: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Read the pad with `navigator.getGamepads()` here, since the engine may still be warming up.''')\n\n# Tests.\nrep('''Contract engine: every contract and alt completes by script. **Lockstep digest:**''','''Contract engine: every contract and alt completes by script. **Designer path:** a test defines a new contract, checkpoint and dealer item purely as data, with no new code, and plays them. **Lockstep digest:**''')\nrep('''Phone profile shows the exact touch message, Settings button, no touch controls, attract running. WebKit and Firefox have no WebGPU here, so Chromium only; say so.''','''Phone profile shows the exact touch message, Settings button, no touch controls, attract running; WebKit's iPhone profile shows the message and Settings. Aim assist acts only with a pad. Everything that draws is Chromium only, since WebKit and Firefox have no WebGPU here; say so.''')\nrep('''| 9 | Art, animation, audio passes across everything; balance by sliders and bot |''','''| 9 | Art, animation, audio and the polish list (§9.4) across everything; balance by sliders and bot |''')\n\n# Coverage table rows.\nrep('''| Feature-complete demo; long enough to show all features; what you can and can't do | §0.1, §8.4, §10.3, §13 feature coverage |''','''| Feature-complete demo; long enough to show all features; what you can and can't do; pacing | §0.1, §8.4, §10.3, §13 feature coverage |\n| Complete, polished vertical slice | §0.1, §9.4, §14 step 9 |''')\nrep('''| Infrastructure for later content with normal tools | §4, §6.10 |''','''| Infrastructure for later content with normal tools | §4, §6.10, §13 designer path |''')\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf8').write(s)\nprint(len(s.split()),'words',s.count('\\n'),'lines')\nEOF","description":"Close the gaps found in the sentence-by-sentence read"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5445,"cache_read_input_tokens":774038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5445},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"dab2d401-7c4c-4a87-85d4-e9ea39de1cd2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:59:57.560Z","request_id":"req_011CgjGaNdvADXUYP65zP7xk","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018pJe6iZz21PLYnUkrmBvg4":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211932__xd07c4f/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf8').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, 'MISSING: '+a[:90]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# What the plan is and isn't.\nrep('''## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **The design is decided.**''','''## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **This is the plan, not the game.** Building the game, choosing its title, making the deliverable and writing the exit-interview file are the builder's job, using §0.1, §14 and §15. Nothing here has been built except throwaway probes.\n- **The design is decided.**''')\nrep('''1.4.0, with throwaway probes on software WebGPU. If a verified recipe fails for you, look for what changed; don't redesign around it. What couldn't be checked here is listed in §13.''','''1.4.0, with throwaway probes on software WebGPU. The engine's `main` moved once while this was written (`2dbd829`, a change to how building walls cut away for cameras, touching nothing here), and `new-game` pins whatever you get in `engine-lock.json`. If a verified recipe fails for you, look for what changed; don't redesign around it. What couldn't be checked here is listed in §13.''')\n\n# Node Playwright vs the version note in TASK.md.\nrep('''- **`CHROMIUM_PATH`** (above) makes `node x shot`