{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wr4KKFFrkmd5onPybtpoyS","type":"tool_result","content":"T1: SOURCE: Far Cry 2\n  P3: PLAN: an open-world first-person mercenary game in a burning country  Inspired by Far Cry 2. Original world, cast, story, art and music.\nT3: Genre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n  P60: Genre: open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure. Perspective: first person, with an over-the-shoulder option in Settings, a chase option while driving, and short eye-level \"body camera\" mo\nT5: Perspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n  P60: Perspective: first person, with an over-the-shoulder option in Settings, a chase option while driving, and short eye-level \"body camera\" moments (§10.2).\nT7: Game Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name.\n  P31: A playful riff on the source's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles (don't show the list), then pick one with a real random number generator by running code; don't choose by tas\nT7: Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n  P31: Silently brainstorm 50 titles (don't show the list), then pick one with a real random number generator by running code; don't choose by taste.\nT9: Research: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n  P33: Research. How much is your choice. The engine and the hub are already researched here.\nT11: Package: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932].html.\n  P34: Develop in separate modules in the dev folder, then bundle into one HTML file in the folder above it, named as your TASK.md says. External CDN dependencies are allowed, but this plan's recipe inlines everything (§15.1).\nT11: Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n  P34: External CDN dependencies are allowed, but this plan's recipe inlines everything (§15.1).\nT13: Tech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace.\n  P36: my-3dge in its full workspace. The engine stays read-only; add whatever systems, art and animation the game needs on top.\n  P104: s is elsewhere):  sh mkdir -p out && mv ../titles.txt ../title-pick.json out/ # the pick's evidence, for the final check mkdir -p .cache && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge .cache/engine-src # stays: the engine's examples, to read node .cache/engine-src/tools/game/new-game.mjs .\nT13: The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it.\n  P36: The engine stays read-only; add whatever systems, art and animation the game needs on top. The rest of the stack is your choice; this plan needs nothing beyond what the workspace ships (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright).\nT13: The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n  P36: The rest of the stack is your choice; this plan needs nothing beyond what the workspace ships (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright).\nT15: Art Direction: Use the engine's native style as the base.\n  P38: The engine's native style and animation system as the base, with new assets and animations for whatever makes it feel like the source; the engine's asset library is fine for generic props (§9.1, §9.2).\nT15: Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine's asset library is fine for generic props.\n  P583: Props to make: trees and grass (§5), charred variants, scorch, flame patch, termite mound, boulders; mud-brick huts, tin roofs, concrete bunker, watchtowe\n  P586: Engine props are fine for generic chairs, tables, lamps. - Buildings as defineLevel format 2 storeys on the site, tile 0.5, 2–3 interiors worth entering per place, doors with addLevelDoors driven\nT17: Animation: Use the engine's native animation system as the base.\n  P592: ### 9.2 Animation: the engine's system plus new work  Author with defineAction (node x new action), checked by actionCheck.\nT17: Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n  P594: ### 9.2 Animation: the engine's system plus new work  Author with defineAction (node x new action), checked by actionCheck.\nT19: Audio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files.\n  P39: All music, effects and voices are written in code with no audio files, voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish, and music defaults to 10% (§9.3).\nT19: Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n  P39: All music, effects and voices are written in code with no audio files, voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish, and music defaults to 10% (§9.3).\nT19: Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n  P605: Music at 10% by default: right after createAudio(), audio.volume('music', 0.1). The engine's default is 0.5, so this is required and tested.\nT21: Online: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n  P40: Follow the [AI Hub session protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub) both for integration into the hub and for the game on its own (§12).\nT24: Create a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932] to keep all your dev files in.\n  P100: st i=require(\"crypto\").randomInt(50);fs.writeFileSync(\"title-pick.json\",JSON.stringify({count:50,index:i,title:t[i]}));console.log(t[i])'   Name the dev folder from that title by the pattern in your TASK.md, make it and go in. Then, with this plan at ../PLAN.md (change the path if yours is elsewhere):  sh mkdir -p out && mv ../titles.txt ../title-pick.json out/ #\nT24: You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261104-211932].html in root whe