{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C8RAfNMcsGGtysKw455hB6","type":"tool_result","content":"T68: Build an original game inspired by the source.\n  P27: An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters, themes and tone into mechanics,\nT72: Create a feature-complete demo.\n  P27: An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters, themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX.\nT72: Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game.\n  P27: An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters, themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX. It must be long enough to show all features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and feels and what they can and can\nT72: The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do.\n  P28: It must be long enough to show all features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and feels and what they can and cannot do (§8.4, field notes in §10.3, the coverage test in §13), with all the infrastructure for building out the rest with normal designer tools (§\n  P802: Feature coverage: the main path must fire each of these at least once, proving the demo is long enough to show everything: jam, clear, gun break, syrette, surgery, down, rescue, mercy prompt, buddy death, feve\nT72: Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice.\n  P618: ### 9.4 Polish: what \"complete and polished\" means here  Do this pass before calling anything done: hit confirmation by sound and a small marker; muzzle light and flash, shell casings, dust puffs by surface; camera shake scaled by distanc\nT72: It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n  P240: Adding a contract or a checkpoint takes no new code, and a test proves it (§13).\nT74: Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly.\n  P706: ### 11.3 Dev mode: Pause → Dev Mode (not in hub sessions)  Pad and mouse friendly, big rows, one category at a time:  - Balance: live sliders on scenes/settings.ts, each with\n  P711: Cheats: god, infinite ammo, no wear/jam, no fever, invisible to AI, noclip, one-shot kills.\n  P712: Teleport: every place, the crosshair point, a chosen map point.\nT74: Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n  P710: Balance: live sliders on scenes/settings.ts, each with its value and a per-row and global Reset to defaults: wear per shot, jam multiplier, player damage, enemy damage, enemy accuracy, reaction, sight, hearing, attackers, respawn hours; fire sprea\nT76: Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it.\n  P699: ### 11.2 Attract mode  After 10 s without input on the title or any menu off the title (keys, mouse move or click, pad buttons or sticks past deadzone all reset the timer), switch to game.mode=attract and restart.\n  P704: Any input exits at once to the title (restart).\nT76: The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc).\n  P700: It's an AI-played demo in arcade and kiosk style, and it may cheat freely: infinite health, ammo and abilities, no clip. Each vignette runs 15–25 s with a banner: title + \"Press any button\":  1.\nT76: Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n  P700: Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible; it plays no part in balance. It's an AI-played demo in arcade and kiosk style, and it may cheat freely: infinite health, ammo and abilities, no clip.\nT78: Multiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n  P726: Multiplayer  Decision: shared-world, host-sequenced deterministic lockstep. The engine's one superpower is bit-exact determinism, so instead of replicating fire cells, ragdolls and NPCs, every copy runs the same sce\n  P782: ### 12.4 The game on its own  Outside the hub, Title → Co-op runs the same Contract Run with no server, through the same Net interface (send, on('message'), time, players, isHost):  - net/tabs.ts: \"Same computer\": BroadcastChannel; host lists\nT80: Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source's.\n  P514: A writing style similar to the source's, in entirely original words. Dry, weary, literate; menace delivered politely; gallows humour with no jokes for the audience.\nT83: Design the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet.\n  P622: UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first  Design for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping, played on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse get the same game on the same screens, and must feel native rather than like stand-ins for controller buttons: mouse loo\nT83: With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons.\n  P622: Keyboard and mouse get the same game on the same screens, and must feel native rather than like stand-ins for controller buttons: mouse look is direct under pointer lock with no stick imitation or acceleration, number keys and the wheel pick weapons outright, menus an\nT83: The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre.\n  P622: er lock with no stick imitation or acceleration, number keys and the wheel pick weapons outright, menus and the map follow the pointer, and keys are the ones PC players expect.\n  P633: | Crouch | Circle | C (never Ctrl: Ctrl+W closes the tab)\nT83: The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it.\n  P648: Mouse is native: pointer lock in play; in menus hover moves focus and click picks (createMenu; custom panels too, createPadCursor for pad).\n  P649: Things in the world: under lock, the crosshair is the pointer: looking at something usable highlights it with a world-anchored label, and with no weapon out a left click uses it, as well as E.\n  P622: Keyboard and mouse get the same game on the same screens, and must feel native rather than like stand-ins for controller buttons: mouse look is direct under pointer lock with no stick imitation or acceleration, number keys and the wheel pick weapons outright, menus and the map follow the pointer, and keys ar\nT83: On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n  P653: Prompts follow the last input. ui/glyphs.ts: inline SVG for PlayStation shapes (✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1 L2 R2 L3 R3 Options Create, D-pad) and Xbox (A B X Y LB RB LT RT LS RS Menu\n  P653: Pick by input.lastDevice: gamepad → PlayStation glyphs for any pad, or Xbox if Settings says so; keyboard → key caps and mouse icons with the actual binding (input.bindingsFor, codeLabel, readKeyboardLayout).\nT85: Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n  P667: 3.2vh (34 px at 1080p), headings 6vh, subtitles 3.4vh on a dark band; 5% safe margins; one decision a screen; at most 7 visible list items; no dense, web-style data screens and no tables of data: the dealer and ledger show one thing large, stepped with left/right.\nT87: Touch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button.\n  P685: ### 10.4 Touch-only devices  If matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)') and no pad or key has been seen: no touch controls (?touch=off in search, no touchLayout; verified on Playwright's Pixel 7 profile, where the query is true, WebGPU runs, and a tap wakes no t\n  P685: Show a full-screen message exactly \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button over the attract mode, which keeps running. A pad press or key press dismisses it for good.\nT90: No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features.\n  P52: No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n  P52: The demo is complete.  ## 1.\nT93: CRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen.\n  P842: ### 15.3 Exit interview  Critical, from the brief: put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your final chat reply and on the About/Credits screen, writing \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Say plainly what you did not verify.\nT94: - Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:32\n  P846: |---|---| | Date | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\nT95: - Source: the work it's based on\n  P847: | Source | Far Cry 2 |\nT96: - Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n  P848: | Plan | The file name of the plan you were given: PLAN.md |\nT97: - Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n  P849: | Genre and Perspective | Open-world first-person shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure; first person, with shoulder and chase options and body-camera moments\nT98: - Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n  P850: | Tech | my-3dge at the commit engine-lock.json names; TypeScript; three.js r182 on WebGPU; Rapier 0.21; HTML/CSS UI; Vite, Vitest, Playwright; what else you add, with versions\nT99: - Art Direction: style used\n  P851: | Art Direction | The engine's native low-poly style, sun-bleached, with new procedural art (§9.1)\nT100: - Animation: system used\n  P852: | Animation | The engine's animation system (procedural humanoid, actions, library clips) with new actions and flipbook guns\nT101: - Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n  P853: | Audio | All in code: tracker songs and synthesized effects through the engine's audio; Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default\nT102: - Online: network features used, or none\n  P854: | Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (the client version you bundled), shared-world host-sequenced lockstep; on its own, a same-computer tab link and WebRTC invite codes\nT103: - Testing: testing parameters allowed\n  P855: | Testing | The testing parameters allowed: from your TASK.md, as you used them\nT104: - Research: research done, or none\n  P856: | Research | Engine and hub source reading and this plan's verified probes, plus whatever you did, or none\nT105: - Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n  P857: | Environment, Model | Exactly as your TASK.md gives them |\nT106: - Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\n  P857: | Environment, Model | Exactly as your TASK.md gives them |\nT107: - Usage: write \"added on import\".\n  P858: | Usage | Write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records it, and the import puts it on the About screen\nT109: Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm).\n  P860: The exit-interview file, in exactly this shape, every value filled in as plain text (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm), checked with JSON.parse. Tools read it.\nT128: The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n  P883: The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked here.\n---\nT110-127, the exit-interview JSON: PLAN.md block identical to TASK.md, byte for byte: True\nT132-151, the 14 feedback questions, each found verbatim in the plan: True\nNo plan action, machine facts only: T33 timestamp; T35 CPU and OS; T36 disk; T42 tool list; T43 Rust; T44 not installed; T45 network\nexit: 0","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c3db9ff-22de-4370-a6c8-816de35b9215","uuid":"32b1acb9-a5d2-425f-954a-c214787c7051","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:17:56.789Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"T68: Build an original game inspired by the source.\n  P27: An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters, themes and tone into mechanics,\nT72: Create a feature-complete demo.\n  P27: An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters, themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX.\nT72: Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game.\n  P27: An original game inspired by Far Cry 2: a feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice that turns the source's world, characters, themes and tone into mechanics, pacing and UX. It must be long enough to show all features and mechanics, so a player clearly understands how it plays and feels and what they can and can\nT72: The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do.\n  P28: It must be long enough to show all features and m